OAI_PAPER({"slug": "De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026", "title": "A positive resolution of De Giorgi's conjecture in dimension eight", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/article.pdf", "family": "375", "family_title": "De Giorgi's conjecture in dimension eight", "abstract_html": "We resolve De Giorgi's conjecture positively in dimension eight: every entire <i>C</i><sup>2</sup> solution \\(u:\\mathbb R^8\\to(-1,1)\\) of \\(\\Delta u=u^3-u\\) with an everywhere positive directional derivative is a planar heteroclinic. We prove that every stable solution \\(v:\\mathbb R^7\\to[-1,1]\\) of this equation is a constant well or a planar heteroclinic, without an energy-growth assumption.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Monotone rigidity in dimension eight — original Theorem 1.1.** Assume that \\(u\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^8,(-1,1))\\) satisfies \\( \\Delta u=u^3-u,\\qquad \\partial_8u>0\\quad\\text{in }\\mathbb R^8. \\) Then there exist …", "statement": "<p>**Monotone rigidity in dimension eight — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><p>Assume that \\(u\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^8,(-1,1))\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\Delta u=u^3-u,\\qquad \\partial_8u&gt;0\\quad\\text{in }\\mathbb R^8.\n\\]\nThen there exist \\(e\\in\\mathbb S^7\\), with \\(e_8&gt;0\\), and \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\) such that\n\\[\nu(x)=\\tanh\\!\\left(\\frac{e\\cdot x-c}{\\sqrt2}\\right)\n\\quad\\text{for every }x\\in\\mathbb R^8.\n\\]\nNo energy-growth, minimizing, or directional-limit assumption is imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 11 gives global minimization and\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(u;B_R)\\le CR^7.\n\\]\nThis proposition uses the independently proved analytic classification through Propositions 1, 3, and 10, rather than a forward citation to Theorem 2.</p><p>By Proposition 1,\n\\[\nM_r(u)=\\frac{\\mathcal E(u;B_r)}{s_0\\omega_7r^7}\n\\]\nis nondecreasing, with a finite positive limit \\(M_\\infty\\). Apply Proposition 12 to any blow-down sequence. At continuity radii, exact energy convergence gives\n\\[\nP(E;B_r)=\\omega_7r^7M_\\infty.\n\\]\nSqueezing between continuity radii gives the identity for every \\(r&gt;0\\).</p><p>The perimeter varifold of a locally minimizing boundary is stationary, integral, and multiplicity one. Its constant centered area ratio forces its nonempty support to be a cone containing \\(0\\). Directional monotonicity passes to the phases:\n\\[\n-\\int u_j\\partial_8\\psi=\\int(\\partial_8u_j)\\psi\\ge0\n\\quad(\\psi\\ge0),\n\\]\nso \\(\\partial_8\\mathbf1_E\\ge0\\). Proposition 13 makes the cone a multiplicity-one hyperplane. Exact measure convergence therefore gives\n\\[\nM_\\infty(u)=1.\n\\]</p><p>Wang's near-unit-density theorem [25, Theorem 11.1] applies to the nonconstant bounded entire solution, with the Modica bound and limiting normalized density one. Hence\n\\[\nu(x)=h(e\\cdot x)\n\\]\nfor a unit vector \\(e\\).</p><p>Because \\(u_8=e_8h'&gt;0\\), reverse the scalar orientation if needed so \\(e_8&gt;0\\), \\(h'&gt;0\\). The finite end limits \\(a_\\pm\\) are distinct. Translated ODE compactness gives\n\\[\na_\\pm^3-a_\\pm=0,\\qquad h'(s)\\to0\n\\]\nat both ends. The first integral\n\\[\n\\frac12h'^2-W(h)=\\text{constant}\n\\]\nimplies \\(W(a_-)=W(a_+)\\). The only distinct stationary roots with equal potential are \\(-1,1\\). Thus\n\\[\na_-=-1,\\qquad a_+=1,\\qquad\nh'=\\frac{1-h^2}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\nIntegration gives\n\\[\nh(s)=\\tanh((s-c)/\\sqrt2).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nu(x)=\\tanh((e\\cdot x-c)/\\sqrt2),\\qquad e_8&gt;0.\n\\]\nConversely this profile satisfies the equation, strict range, and positive eighth derivative. This proves original Theorem 1.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Stable rigidity in dimension seven — original Theorem 1.2.** Let \\(v\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^7,[-1,1])\\) satisfy \\( \\Delta v=v^3-v. \\) Assume stability:", "statement": "<p>**Stable rigidity in dimension seven — original Theorem 1.2.**</p><p>Let \\(v\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^7,[-1,1])\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\Delta v=v^3-v.\n\\]\nAssume stability:\n\\[\nQ_v(\\phi):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^7}\n\\left(|\\nabla\\phi|^2+(3v^2-1)\\phi^2\\right)\\,dx\\ge0\n\\quad\\text{for every }\\phi\\in C_c^1(\\mathbb R^7).\n\\]\nThen either \\(v\\equiv1\\), \\(v\\equiv-1\\), or\n\\[\nv(x)=\\tanh\\!\\left(\\frac{a\\cdot x-b}{\\sqrt2}\\right)\n\\]\nfor some \\(a\\in\\mathbb S^6\\) and \\(b\\in\\mathbb R\\). No energy-growth assumption is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(v\\) attains either endpoint of \\([-1,1]\\), the strong maximum principle gives the corresponding constant well. The remaining constant zero is unstable: for nonzero \\(\\chi\\in C_c^1(\\mathbb R^7)\\),\n\\[\nQ_0(\\chi(\\,\\cdot\\,/R))\n=R^5\\int|\\nabla\\chi|^2-R^7\\int\\chi^2&lt;0\n\\]\nfor large \\(R\\).</p><p>For a nonconstant solution with values in \\((-1,1)\\), Proposition 1 gives the planar classification if \\(D\\) vanishes anywhere. Otherwise \\(D&gt;0\\). Proposition 10 gives finite density, and Proposition 3 gives exactly the stated constant wells or planar heteroclinics. This proves original Theorem 1.2 independently of the geometric deduction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Classical estimates and diffuse variables — original Section 2, equations (5.3)–(5.8), and Lemmas 5.1–5.2.** Let \\(n=m+1\\ge2\\), and let \\(v\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^n,[-1,1])\\) solve \\(\\Delta v=v^3-v\\).", "statement": "<p>**Classical estimates and diffuse variables — original Section 2, equations (5.3)–(5.8), and Lemmas 5.1–5.2.**</p><p>Let \\(n=m+1\\ge2\\), and let \\(v\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^n,[-1,1])\\) solve \\(\\Delta v=v^3-v\\). Set\n\\[\nW(t)=\\frac{(1-t^2)^2}{4},\\quad\ng(s)=\\tanh(s/\\sqrt2),\\quad G(s)=1-g(s)^2,\n\\]\n\\[\ns_0=\\int_{\\mathbb R}(g')^2\\,ds=\\frac{2\\sqrt2}{3},\n\\qquad\n\\sigma=\\int_{\\mathbb R}G^2\\,ds=2s_0=\\frac{4\\sqrt2}{3},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ne(v)=\\frac12|\\nabla v|^2+W(v),\\qquad\nM_r(y)=\\frac{\\int_{B_r(y)}e(v)\\,dx}{s_0\\omega_mr^m}.\n\\]</p><p>The following hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The solution is smooth, with uniform bounds for every fixed derivative order, and\n\\[\n\\frac12|\\nabla v|^2\\le W(v).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nM_r'(y)=\\frac1{s_0\\omega_m}\n\\left[\nr^{-m-1}\\int_{B_r(y)}\n\\left(W(v)-\\frac12|\\nabla v|^2\\right)\n+r^{-m}\\int_{\\partial B_r(y)}|\\partial_rv|^2\n\\right]\\ge0.\n\\]\nIts limit at infinity is independent of \\(y\\); writing\n\\[\nM_\\infty=\\sup_{r&gt;0}M_r(0),\n\\]\none has \\(M_r(y)\\le M_\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(v\\) attains \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\), it is that constant. Otherwise \\(|v|&lt;1\\), and the variables\n\\[\nq=1-v^2,\\quad U=g^{-1}(v),\\quad p=\\nabla U,\\quad T=\\nabla^2U,\n\\quad D=1-|p|^2\n\\]\nsatisfy \\(0\\le D\\le1\\), uniform bounds for all positive-order derivatives of \\(U\\), and\n\\[\n\\Delta U=-\\sqrt2\\,vD,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal LD=2|T|^2,\\qquad\n\\mathcal L=-\\Delta+2\\sqrt2\\,vp\\cdot\\nabla+2q|p|^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\Delta+3v^2-1)q=-q^2D,\n\\]\n\\[\nW(v)-\\frac12|\\nabla v|^2=\\frac14q^2D,\n\\qquad e(v)=\\frac14q^2(2-D).\n\\]\nIf \\(D\\) vanishes anywhere, then \\(D\\equiv0\\), \\(T\\equiv0\\), and \\(v=g(a\\cdot x-b)\\) for a unit vector \\(a\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(v\\) is stable, then for every compactly supported Lipschitz \\(\\eta\\),\n\\[\n0\\le Q_v(q\\eta)\n=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\\left(q^2|\\nabla\\eta|^2-q^3D\\eta^2\\right)\\,dx.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. If \\(D&gt;0\\), then\n\\[\n|T|^2\\le CD,\\qquad |\\nabla D|\\le CD,\n\\]\nand, for every \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nD(y)\\le C_\\gamma e^{(2\\sqrt2+\\gamma)|y-z|}D(z).\n\\]\nFor every fixed \\(R,k\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\frac D{D(z)}\\right\\|_{C^k(B_R(z))}\n+\\left\\|\\frac T{\\sqrt{D(z)}}\\right\\|_{C^k(B_R(z))}\n\\le C_{R,k},\n\\qquad\n\\inf_{B_R(z)}D\\ge C_R^{-1}D(z).\n\\]\nWith \\(\\kappa_\\gamma=2\\sqrt2+\\gamma\\),\n\\[\n|\\nabla^kD(x)|\\le C_{k,\\gamma}e^{\\kappa_\\gamma|x-z|}D(z),\n\\qquad\n|\\nabla^kT(x)|\\le C_{k,\\gamma}e^{\\kappa_\\gamma|x-z|/2}\\sqrt{D(z)}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Every solution with \\(|v|&lt;1\\) has a nonempty zero set \\(\\Sigma=\\{v=0\\}\\), and, for \\(0&lt;\\gamma&lt;\\sqrt2\\),\n\\[\nq(x)\\le C_\\gamma e^{-(\\sqrt2-\\gamma)\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\Sigma)}.\n\\]\nThe same bound, with \\(\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\partial\\Omega)\\), holds for a solution of one strict sign in an open set \\(\\Omega\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 1.1–1.4 give the asserted estimates and identities. The endpoint alternative follows from the strong maximum principle applied to \\(1-v\\) and \\(1+v\\). Interior elliptic estimates give the smoothness and uniform derivative bounds used throughout.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Modica bound and energy monotonicity — original Sections 2.1–2.2.** Under the setting of Proposition 1, \\( \\frac12|\\nabla v|^2\\le W(v), \\) and the displayed formula for \\(M_r'(y)\\) in Proposition 1 holds in integrated …", "statement": "<p>**Modica bound and energy monotonicity — original Sections 2.1–2.2.**</p><p>Under the setting of Proposition 1,\n\\[\n\\frac12|\\nabla v|^2\\le W(v),\n\\]\nand the displayed formula for \\(M_r'(y)\\) in Proposition 1 holds in integrated form for every pair of positive radii.</p>", "proof": "<p>For\n\\[\nP=\\frac12|\\nabla v|^2-W(v),\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\nabla P=\\nabla^2v\\,\\nabla v-W'(v)\\nabla v,\\qquad\n\\Delta P=|\\nabla^2v|^2-W'(v)^2.\n\\]\nWhere \\(\\nabla v\\ne0\\),\n\\[\n\\Delta P\\ge\\frac{2W'(v)}{|\\nabla v|^2}\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla P.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\sup P&gt;0\\), translate a maximizing sequence and pass to a locally smooth limit. The strong maximum principle makes its \\(P\\) constant on the component of \\(\\{\\nabla v\\ne0\\}\\) containing the maximum. That component is closed because \\(|\\nabla v|^2\\ge2\\sup P&gt;0\\), and hence is all of \\(\\mathbb R^n\\). Along the complete forward flow of the globally bounded vector field \\(\\nabla v\\), the function \\(v\\) increases at rate at least \\(2\\sup P\\), contradicting boundedness.</p><p>The stress tensor \\(e(v)\\operatorname{Id}-\\nabla v\\otimes\\nabla v\\) is divergence free. Contracting with the derivative of \\(x-y\\) gives\n\\[\nr\\int_{\\partial B_r(y)}\\left(e(v)-|\\partial_rv|^2\\right)\n=\\int_{B_r(y)}\\left((m+1)e(v)-|\\nabla v|^2\\right).\n\\]\nDifferentiating \\(r^{-m}\\int_{B_r(y)}e(v)\\) gives the stated formula. Its nonnegativity follows from the gradient bound. The formula holds almost everywhere and therefore in integrated form at every pair of radii. Shifted-ball containment makes the limiting density independent of the center.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Inverse-profile identities and their equality case — original Section 5.1.** Assume \\(|v|<1\\), and define \\(q,U,p,T,D\\) as in Proposition 1.", "statement": "<p>**Inverse-profile identities and their equality case — original Section 5.1.**</p><p>Assume \\(|v|&lt;1\\), and define \\(q,U,p,T,D\\) as in Proposition 1. Then the diffuse equations and energy identities in Proposition 1 hold. If \\(v\\) is stable,\n\\[\nQ_v(q\\eta)=\\int(q^2|\\nabla\\eta|^2-q^3D\\eta^2).\n\\]\nIf \\(D\\) vanishes at one point, then \\(v\\) is a planar heteroclinic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Direct substitution using \\(g'=G/\\sqrt2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\Delta U=-\\sqrt2\\,vD,\\qquad\n\\mathcal LD=2|T|^2,\\qquad\n(-\\Delta+3v^2-1)q=-q^2D.\n\\]\nThe gradient bound gives \\(|p|\\le1\\), and direct calculation gives the two energy identities.</p><p>On any fixed ball, subtract the central value of \\(U\\). The resulting function is uniformly bounded because \\(U\\) is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz. The equation first yields uniform interior \\(W^{2,r}\\) bounds, then \\(C^{1,\\alpha}\\) bounds. Its right side is the smooth function\n\\[\n-\\sqrt2\\,g(U)(1-|\\nabla U|^2),\n\\]\nwhose translated profile coefficients have uniform derivatives. Schauder estimates and induction give all positive-order derivative bounds.</p><p>The zeroth-order coefficient of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is nonnegative. If \\(D\\) vanishes, the strong minimum principle gives \\(D\\equiv0\\), and its equation gives \\(T\\equiv0\\). Thus \\(U\\) is affine with unit gradient.</p><p>For the stability identity, expand \\(Q_v(q\\eta)\\). Integration by parts combines the cross term and \\(\\eta^2|\\nabla q|^2\\) with the potential term into\n\\[\n\\int q\\eta^2(-\\Delta+3v^2-1)q=-\\int q^3D\\eta^2.\n\\]\nApproximation permits compactly supported Lipschitz \\(\\eta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Uniform estimates relative to small defect — original Lemma 5.1.** Assume \\(D>0\\).", "statement": "<p>**Uniform estimates relative to small defect — original Lemma 5.1.**</p><p>Assume \\(D&gt;0\\). Then all pointwise, normalized, Harnack, and growing-window derivative estimates in part 4 of Proposition 1 hold, uniformly over the entire solutions under consideration.</p>", "proof": "<p>The drift, potential, and their fixed-order derivatives in the \\(D\\)-equation are uniformly bounded. Weak Harnack on a fixed enclosing ball gives\n\\[\n\\int_{B_4(z)}D\\le CD(z).\n\\]\nIntegrating \\(\\mathcal LD=2|T|^2\\) against a cutoff gives\n\\[\n\\int_{B_3(z)}|T|^2\\le CD(z).\n\\]\nWriting \\(b=2\\sqrt2\\,vp\\), twice differentiation of the \\(U\\)-equation gives\n\\[\n(\\Delta-b\\cdot\\nabla)T_{ij}\n=-qDT_{ij}+\\sqrt2\\,vqDp_ip_j\n-q(p_iD_j+p_jD_i)+2\\sqrt2\\,v(T^2)_{ij}.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(F_0=D^2+|T|^2\\) satisfies\n\\[\n(-\\Delta+b\\cdot\\nabla)F_0\\le CF_0.\n\\]\nThe local mean-value estimate yields \\(|T(z)|^2\\le CD(z)\\). The equation for \\(D\\) can now be viewed as a homogeneous equation with bounded potential\n\\[\n2q|p|^2-\\frac{2|T|^2}{D}.\n\\]\nOrdinary Harnack gives local comparability of \\(D\\), and interior \\(W^{2,r}\\) estimates give \\(|\\nabla D|\\le CD\\).</p><p>For \\(\\kappa_\\gamma=2\\sqrt2+\\gamma\\) and \\(r=|x-y|\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(e^{-\\kappa_\\gamma r})\n\\le e^{-\\kappa_\\gamma r}\n\\left[-\\kappa_\\gamma^2+\\frac{(n-1)\\kappa_\\gamma}{r}\n+2\\sqrt2\\,\\kappa_\\gamma|v|+2(1-v^2)\\right].\n\\]\nThe last two terms have maximum \\(2\\sqrt2\\,\\kappa_\\gamma&lt;\\kappa_\\gamma^2\\). Thus the expression is negative outside a uniform ball. Local Harnack supplies an inner-boundary comparison; comparison on annuli, after subtracting a small constant and then letting it decrease to zero, proves the exponential estimate.</p><p>Finally set \\(\\epsilon=D(z)\\), \\(d_\\epsilon=D/\\epsilon\\), and \\(t_\\epsilon=T/\\sqrt\\epsilon\\). Their equations are\n\\[\n(\\Delta-b\\cdot\\nabla)d_\\epsilon\n=2q|p|^2d_\\epsilon-2|t_\\epsilon|^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\Delta-b\\cdot\\nabla)(t_\\epsilon)_{ij}\n=-q\\epsilon d_\\epsilon(t_\\epsilon)_{ij}\n+\\sqrt{2\\epsilon}\\,vq d_\\epsilon p_ip_j\n-q\\sqrt\\epsilon(p_i\\partial_jd_\\epsilon+p_j\\partial_id_\\epsilon)\n+2\\sqrt{2\\epsilon}\\,v(t_\\epsilon^2)_{ij}.\n\\]\nInterior \\(W^{2,r}\\), Schauder estimates, and induction give all normalized bounds. Applying the fixed-radius estimates at \\(x\\), followed by the exponential comparison between \\(x\\) and \\(z\\), gives the growing-window derivative estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Zeros and clearing inside a phase — original Lemma 5.2.** Every entire solution with \\(|v|<1\\) has a zero.", "statement": "<p>**Zeros and clearing inside a phase — original Lemma 5.2.**</p><p>Every entire solution with \\(|v|&lt;1\\) has a zero. For a positive solution \\(0&lt;v\\le1\\) in an open set \\(\\Omega\\),\n\\[\n1-v^2\\le C_\\gamma\ne^{-(\\sqrt2-\\gamma)\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\partial\\Omega)}\n\\quad(0&lt;\\gamma&lt;\\sqrt2).\n\\]\nThe corresponding negative-phase assertion follows by changing sign.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\varphi_R\\) be the positive first Dirichlet eigenfunction of \\(B_R(x)\\), normalized by \\(\\varphi_R(x)=1\\). If \\(0&lt;c&lt;1\\) and \\(\\lambda_1(B_R)&lt;1-c^2\\), then\n\\[\n\\Delta(c\\varphi_R)+c\\varphi_R-(c\\varphi_R)^3\n=c\\varphi_R(1-\\lambda_1(B_R)-c^2\\varphi_R^2)&gt;0.\n\\]\nSliding its amplitude below \\(v\\) excludes a first interior contact, so \\(v(x)\\ge c\\). This proves uniform clearing as the distance to the phase boundary tends to infinity. An entire strict-sign solution would satisfy \\(|v|\\ge1\\), a contradiction.</p><p>In the positive phase put \\(w=1-v\\). Then\n\\[\n\\Delta w=(2-3w+w^2)w.\n\\]\nFix \\(k=\\sqrt2-\\gamma/2\\). Clearing supplies \\(R_0\\) such that \\(\\Delta w\\ge k^2w\\) at distance at least \\(R_0\\) from the boundary. If \\(d=\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\partial\\Omega)&gt;R_0+1\\), compare in \\(B_{d-R_0}(x)\\) with\n\\[\n\\frac{A_k(|y-x|)}{A_k(d-R_0)},\\qquad\nA_k(r)=\\frac1{|\\mathbb S^{n-1}|}\n\\int_{\\mathbb S^{n-1}}e^{kr\\theta_1}\\,d\\theta.\n\\]\nA spherical cap gives\n\\[\nA_k(r)\\ge c_kr^{-(n-1)/2}e^{kr}.\n\\]\nAbsorb the polynomial factor into \\(e^{\\gamma r/2}\\), and use \\(q\\le2w\\). Balls may first have closure inside \\(\\Omega\\) and then increase by a limit; boundary regularity is unnecessary.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Small-defect limits and sharp mean-curvature control — original Lemmas 5.3–5.4 and 5.6, with Remark 5.5.** Use the diffuse variables of Proposition 1.", "statement": "<p>**Small-defect limits and sharp mean-curvature control — original Lemmas 5.3–5.4 and 5.6, with Remark 5.5.**</p><p>Use the diffuse variables of Proposition 1. At a regular zero, orient\n\\[\n\\nu=\\frac p{|p|},\\qquad A=d\\nu|_{T\\Sigma},\\qquad H=\\operatorname{tr}A.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nH=\\frac{\\partial_\\nu D}{2|p|^2}.\n\\]</p><p>If zeros \\(z_j\\) satisfy \\(\\epsilon_j=D_j(z_j)&gt;0\\) and \\(\\epsilon_j\\to0\\), translate and rotate so that \\(z_j=0\\), \\(\\nu_j(0)=e_n\\), and write \\(x=(x',s)\\). A subsequence satisfies\n\\[\nU_j\\to s,\\qquad D_j/\\epsilon_j\\to\\mathcal D&gt;0,\\qquad\nT_j/\\sqrt{\\epsilon_j}\\to\\mathcal T\n\\quad\\text{in }C^k_{\\mathrm{loc}}\n\\]\nfor every \\(k\\). Here \\(\\mathcal D(0)=1\\); \\(\\mathcal T\\) is tangential, independent of \\(s\\), trace free, and harmonic in \\(x'\\). Setting \\(F=G\\mathcal D\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0F=2G|\\mathcal T|^2,\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_0=-\\Delta+2-G(s),\\qquad\n\\frac{H_j(0)}{\\epsilon_j}\\to\\frac12F_s(0).\n\\]</p><p>For any smooth \\(F\\ge0\\) satisfying \\(\\mathcal L_0F=S\\), with \\(S\\ge0\\) even in \\(s\\), there is a decomposition\n\\[\nF=E+h,\\quad E,h\\ge0,\\quad\n\\mathcal L_0E=S,\\quad\\mathcal L_0h=0,\\quad E(x',s)=E(x',-s).\n\\]\nEvery extreme point of\n\\[\n\\{h\\ge0:\\mathcal L_0h=0,\\ h(0)=1\\}\n\\]\nhas the form \\(e^{b\\cdot x'}f(s/\\sqrt2)\\), where\n\\[\na=\\sqrt{4-2|b|^2}\\in[1,2].\n\\]\nFor \\(a&gt;1\\),\n\\[\nf(y)=a^{-1}e^{ay}(a-\\tanh y)\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\na^{-1}e^{-ay}(a+\\tanh y);\n\\]\nfor \\(a=1\\), \\(f(y)=\\operatorname{sech}y\\).</p><p>For every \\(c&gt;1/(2\\sqrt2)\\), there are \\(P&lt;\\infty\\) and \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), uniform in the entire solution, such that every zero with \\(0&lt;D(z)&lt;\\delta\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|H(z)|\\le cI_P(z),\\qquad\nI_P(z)=\\frac1\\sigma\\int_{-P}^{P}\nq(z+s\\nu(z))^3D(z+s\\nu(z))\\,ds.\n\\]\nThe inequality remains valid on any larger normal interval. In particular, one may fix\n\\[\n\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}&lt;c_*&lt;\\frac{\\sqrt2}{3}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The normalized-limit statement follows from Lemma 2.1, and the positive decomposition from Lemma 2.2.</p><p>For the finite-window estimate, choose\n\\[\n\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}&lt;c_1&lt;c\n\\]\nand fix \\(P\\) supplied by Lemma 2.2 for \\(c_1\\). If no uniform defect threshold existed, select violating zeros with \\(\\epsilon_j=D_j(z_j)\\to0\\). Lemma 2.1 gives, on this fixed interval,\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\epsilon_j}\\int_{-P}^{P}q_j(se_n)^3D_j(se_n)\\,ds\n\\longrightarrow\n\\int_{-P}^{P}G(s)^2F(0,s)\\,ds&gt;0,\n\\]\nand \\(H_j/\\epsilon_j\\to F_s(0)/2\\). The limit obeys the estimate with coefficient \\(c_1\\), whereas violation gives the opposite weak inequality with coefficient \\(c\\). This contradicts positivity of the limiting mass. Nonnegativity of the integrand permits larger intervals.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Normalized limits — original Lemma 5.3.** Under the small-defect sequence assumptions in Proposition 2, the stated smooth limits, tensor properties, limiting equation, and mean-curvature limit hold.", "statement": "<p>**Normalized limits — original Lemma 5.3.**</p><p>Under the small-defect sequence assumptions in Proposition 2, the stated smooth limits, tensor properties, limiting equation, and mean-curvature limit hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 1 gives normalized derivative bounds and positive local lower bounds for \\(D_j/\\epsilon_j\\). Since\n\\[\nU_j(0)=0,\\qquad p_j(0)=\\sqrt{1-\\epsilon_j}\\,e_n,\\qquad T_j\\to0,\n\\]\none gets \\(U_j\\to s\\).</p><p>The identities\n\\[\nT_jp_j=-\\frac12\\nabla D_j,\\qquad\n(p_j\\cdot\\nabla)T_j=-T_j^2-\\frac12\\nabla^2D_j\n\\]\ngive \\(\\mathcal Te_n=0\\) and \\(\\partial_s\\mathcal T=0\\). The \\(U\\)-equation gives \\(\\operatorname{tr}\\mathcal T=0\\), and\n\\[\n\\Delta T_j=-\\sqrt2\\,\\nabla^2(v_jD_j)=O(\\epsilon_j)\n\\]\ngives \\(\\Delta'\\mathcal T=0\\). The limiting defect equation is\n\\[\n(-\\Delta+2\\sqrt2\\,g\\partial_s+2G)\\mathcal D=2|\\mathcal T|^2.\n\\]\nConjugating by \\(G\\), using\n\\[\nG'/G=-\\sqrt2\\,g,\\qquad G''=(3g^2-1)G,\n\\]\ngives the equation for \\(F\\).</p><p>At a zero,\n\\[\n0=\\Delta U=T(\\nu,\\nu)+|p|H,\\qquad\n\\partial_\\nu D=-2|p|T(\\nu,\\nu),\n\\]\nwhich proves the zero-level identity. Since \\(G(0)=1\\) and \\(G'(0)=0\\), it also gives the asserted limit of \\(H_j/\\epsilon_j\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Positive decomposition and normal derivative — original Lemma 5.4.** Under the assumptions on \\(F,S,\\mathcal L_0\\) in Proposition 2, the stated decomposition and classification of extreme positive homogeneous …", "statement": "<p>**Positive decomposition and normal derivative — original Lemma 5.4.**</p><p>Under the assumptions on \\(F,S,\\mathcal L_0\\) in Proposition 2, the stated decomposition and classification of extreme positive homogeneous solutions hold. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\frac{|F_s(0)|}{2}\n\\le\\frac1{2\\sqrt2\\,\\sigma}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}G(s)^2F(0,s)\\,ds,\n\\]\nwith the integral allowed to be infinite. For every \\(c&gt;1/(2\\sqrt2)\\), one finite \\(P=P(c)\\), independent of \\(F,S\\), satisfies\n\\[\n\\frac{|F_s(0)|}{2}\\le\\frac c\\sigma\n\\int_{-P}^{P}G(s)^2F(0,s)\\,ds.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Solve \\(\\mathcal L_0E_R=S\\) on \\(B_R(0)\\) with zero boundary data. Since \\(2-G\\in[1,2]\\), comparison gives\n\\[\n0\\le E_R\\le F,\\qquad E_R\\le E_{R+1}.\n\\]\nReflection makes \\(E_R\\) even in \\(s\\). Interior estimates give a smooth increasing limit \\(E\\); set \\(h=F-E\\).</p><p>The normalized cone of positive homogeneous solutions is compact by Harnack and interior estimates. For a horizontal translation \\(t\\), Harnack chains give\n\\[\nh(x'+t,s)\\le C_th(x',s)\n\\]\nuniformly in the base point. Extremality makes each translate proportional to \\(h\\). The factors form a continuous multiplicative character, yielding\n\\[\nh(x',s)=e^{b\\cdot x'}f(s/\\sqrt2).\n\\]\nIts equation is\n\\[\n-f''-2\\operatorname{sech}^2y\\,f=-(4-2|b|^2)f.\n\\]\nThe ground-state identity, tested with compact approximations to \\(\\operatorname{sech}y\\), gives \\(4-2|b|^2\\ge1\\). The upper bound is \\(4\\). For \\(a&gt;1\\), the positive basis is\n\\[\ne^{ay}(a-\\tanh y),\\qquad e^{-ay}(a+\\tanh y).\n\\]\nPositive solutions have nonnegative coefficients by their end behavior, and extremality allows only one term. At \\(a=1\\), the second reduction-of-order solution\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sech}y\\int_0^y\\cosh^2t\\,dt\n\\]\nhas opposite dominant signs at the two ends, leaving only \\(\\operatorname{sech}y\\).</p><p>For \\(f_a^+(y)=e^{ay}(a-\\tanh y)\\), put\n\\[\nM(a)=\\frac34\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{ay}\\operatorname{sech}^4y\\,dy.\n\\]\nIntegration by parts gives\n\\[\n\\frac1\\sigma\\int_{\\mathbb R}G(s)^2f_a^+(s/\\sqrt2)\\,ds\n=\\frac{3a}{4}M(a),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac d{ds}f_a^+(s/\\sqrt2)\\bigg|_{s=0}\\right|\n=\\frac{a^2-1}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\nThus the ratio of half the derivative to mass is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt2}{3}\\frac{a-1/a}{M(a)}.\n\\]\nConvexity and\n\\[\nM(2)=2,\\qquad M'(2)=\\frac32\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\nM(a)\\ge\\frac32a-1\\ge\\frac43(a-1/a),\n\\]\nbecause the second difference is \\((a-2)(a-4)/(6a)\\ge0\\). The ratio is therefore at most \\(1/(2\\sqrt2)\\).</p><p>The normalized extreme profiles form a compact family with a uniform integrable tail and uniformly positive full mass. For each strictly larger \\(c\\), one finite \\(P\\) works for every extreme point. The two signed inequalities are continuous linear inequalities on the normalized cone; Krein–Milman extends them to the entire cone. The even source part has zero normal derivative and nonnegative mass. This proves the finite-window inequality; bounding its truncated mass by the full mass and letting \\(c\\downarrow1/(2\\sqrt2)\\) gives the full-line inequality.</p><p>At \\(a=2,b=0\\), the full-line ratio is exactly \\(1/(2\\sqrt2)\\), while finite truncation decreases the denominator. Thus the strict finite-window relaxation is necessary, as stated in original Remark 5.5.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Finite-density stable rigidity — original Theorem 1.3.** Let \\(4\\le n=m+1\\le7\\).", "statement": "<p>**Finite-density stable rigidity — original Theorem 1.3.**</p><p>Let \\(4\\le n=m+1\\le7\\). Suppose \\(v\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^n,[-1,1])\\) solves \\(\\Delta v=v^3-v\\) and is stable. Use the energy normalization of Proposition 1. If \\(M_\\infty&lt;\\infty\\), then\n\\[\nv\\equiv1,\\qquad v\\equiv-1,\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad v(x)=g(a\\cdot x-b)\n\\]\nfor \\(a\\in\\mathbb S^{n-1}\\), \\(b\\in\\mathbb R\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose the proposition fails, and use Lemmas 3.1–3.5. The case \\(D=0\\) has already been classified, so \\(D&gt;0\\).</p><p>Fix a small \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), and set\n\\[\n\\psi(l)=l+\\log l,\\qquad w=\\min\\{T_0,\\psi(l)\\}.\n\\]\nFor large \\(S\\), \\(\\psi'\\le1+\\delta\\), and\n\\[\n-\\psi''/\\psi'=\\frac1{l(l+1)}\\ge C/s\n\\]\nbecause \\(l^2=o(s)\\). The Green inequality applies with \\(L\\le1+\\delta\\).</p><p>Choose \\(1/(2\\sqrt2)&lt;c_*&lt;\\sqrt2/3\\) and its fixed \\(P\\) from Proposition 2. Increase \\(S\\) to make its \\(P\\)-tube injective. Source curvature occurs once and foot curvature at most twice, so Jacobian and weight changes give at most\n\\[\n(1+C\\delta)3\\int_{\\Sigma_{S/2}\\cap\\{3R_0/4&lt;r&lt;3R\\}}\n|H|r^{2-m}\\,dS.\n\\]\nApply \\(|H|\\le c_*I_P\\), transfer through the injective tube, and use the Hardy estimate:\n\\[\n\\limsup_{R\\to\\infty}\\frac L{\\log R}\n\\int_{\\Sigma_S\\cap\\{R_0&lt;r&lt;2R\\}}\nr^{2-m}(|H|+|H\\circ\\pi|)\\,dS\n\\le(1+C\\delta)3c_*Km\\omega_m\\frac{(m-2)^2}{4}.\n\\]</p><p>The endpoint normals of an adjacent gap have opposite orientations. Integrate\n\\[\n|\\nabla p|=|T|\\le C\\sqrt D\n\\]\nalong the two half-chords, using the exponential defect estimate. For every \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nD(z)+D(\\pi(z))\\ge c_\\gamma e^{-(\\sqrt2+\\gamma)l(z)}.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nT_0=(\\sqrt2-\\delta)\\log R,\n\\]\nand choose \\(\\gamma\\) such that\n\\[\n\\beta=(\\sqrt2+\\gamma)(\\sqrt2-\\delta)&lt;2.\n\\]\nOn\n\\[\nE_R=\\{z\\in\\Sigma_S:R/2&lt;r&lt;2R,\\ l(z)&lt;T_0\\},\n\\]\nthe preceding lower bound and the defect-mass estimate imply\n\\[\n\\mathcal H^m(E_R)\\le CR^\\beta\n\\int_{E_R}(D+D\\circ\\pi)\\,dS\n\\le CR^{m-2+\\beta}=o(R^m).\n\\]\nOutside \\(E_R\\), \\(w=T_0\\). The surface-mass estimate yields\n\\[\n\\liminf_{R\\to\\infty}\\frac{R^{-m}}{\\log R}\n\\int_\\Sigma w\\chi F(r/R)\\,dS\n\\ge(\\sqrt2-\\delta)\\big(Km\\omega_m(m-2)-\\delta\\big).\n\\]</p><p>Choose, in order, \\(\\delta\\), then \\(P,S\\), then \\(R_0\\), and finally let \\(R\\to\\infty\\). The cap-independent errors vanish after division by \\(\\log R\\). Let \\(R_0\\to\\infty\\), then \\(\\delta\\to0\\). Cancellation gives\n\\[\n\\sqrt2\\le\\frac{3c_*}{4}(m-2).\n\\]\nFor \\(3\\le m\\le6\\), the right side is at most \\(3c_*&lt;\\sqrt2\\), a contradiction. This proves finite-density rigidity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Critical solution and its good regions — original Lemma 6.1.** If Proposition 3 fails in a fixed dimension, there is a nonplanar entire stable solution with integer density \\(M_\\infty=K\\ge2\\), and a nonempty closed …", "statement": "<p>**Critical solution and its good regions — original Lemma 6.1.**</p><p>If Proposition 3 fails in a fixed dimension, there is a nonplanar entire stable solution with integer density \\(M_\\infty=K\\ge2\\), and a nonempty closed set \\(Z\\), such that, with\n\\[\ns(x)=\\operatorname{dist}(x,Z),\\qquad\\Sigma=\\{v=0\\},\n\\]\nthe following hold uniformly in \\(y\\in Z\\).</p><p>1.\n\\[\nM_r(y)\\to K,\\qquad\nr^{2-m}|B_h(Z)\\cap B_r(y)|\\to0\n\\]\nfor every fixed \\(h&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. At every zero \\(z\\) with large \\(s(z)\\), an interior cylinder of radius \\(cs(z)\\) contains exactly \\(K\\) ordered zero graphs. Their heights are \\(o(s(z))\\), slopes are \\(o(1)\\), separations tend to infinity, and\n\\[\n\\|\\nabla^2f_i\\|_\\infty\n+s(z)^{1/2}[\\nabla^2f_i]_{C^{1/2}}\\le C/s(z),\n\\qquad |H_i|\\le C_\\iota s(z)^{-2+\\iota}.\n\\]\nAt inner zeros \\(D=o(1)\\), and every fixed normal window converges smoothly to the oriented \\(g\\)-profile.</p><p>3.\n\\[\nr^{-m}\\int_{B_r(y)}q^2D\\,dx\\to0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Call \\(K_f\\) subcritical when the classification holds for all stable entire solutions with \\(M_\\infty\\le K_f\\). The literature inputs are the preliminary results of Florit-Simon–Serra [10], version 1, not their final four-dimensional classification.</p><p>Near-unit-density rigidity [10, Theorem 2.16], originating in Wang [25, Theorem 11.1], makes \\(1+\\delta(n)\\) subcritical. If some finite threshold is not subcritical, [10, Proposition 3.6] attains the finite critical density. Take the bad set of [10, Definition 3.7], whose centers satisfy \\(|v|\\le0.9\\) and\n\\[\n\\int_{B_1(x)}\\mathcal A_v^2|\\nabla v|^2\\ge\\delta_{\\rm bad},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal A_v=\\left|\\nabla\\left(\\frac{\\nabla v}{|\\nabla v|}\\right)\\right|.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_v^2|\\nabla v|^2\n=|\\nabla^2v|^2-|\\nabla|\\nabla v||^2\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. Take its closure. Lemma 3.8 and Propositions 3.9–3.10 of [10] give nonemptiness, uniform flatness at bad centers, uniform convergence of density, integrality, and the no-gaps conclusion. Section 5 of [10] gives sparsity for \\(n\\le7\\). A fixed-scale separated covering enlarges \\(B_1(Z)\\) to \\(B_h(Z)\\).</p><p>For a distant good zero \\(z\\), choose a nearest \\(y\\in Z\\), and write \\(s=|z-y|\\). The good-ball theorem [10, Theorem 3.4] gives on \\(B_{s/4}(z)\\)\n\\[\n|\\nabla v|\\ge C^{-1},\\qquad\\mathcal A_v\\le C/s\n\\quad\\text{on }\\{|v|\\le0.9\\}.\n\\]\nUniform flatness at \\(y\\), at radius \\(A_0=4s\\), confines the zeros to an \\(o(s)\\)-slab. Let \\(a_0\\) be the projection of \\(z\\) to the corresponding plane. The no-gaps result gives one modulus \\(\\omega\\) such that\n\\[\n|z-a_0|\\le A_0\\omega(A_0^{-1}),\\qquad\nM_{A_0\\omega(A_0^{-1})}(a_0)\\ge K-\\omega(A_0^{-1}).\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\theta&gt;0\\), ball containment and monotonicity yield\n\\[\nK\\ge M_{\\theta s}(z)\n\\ge\\left(\\frac{\\theta s-|z-a_0|}{\\theta s}\\right)^m\n(K-\\omega(A_0^{-1}))=K-o(1).\n\\]</p><p>Apply [10, Theorem 4.8] and Lemma 4.9 to the thin slab. If \\(N\\) is its graph count, then\n\\[\n|M_{s/8}(z)-N|\\le\\delta_1N.\n\\]\nFirst \\(\\delta_1\\le1/2\\) gives \\(N\\le2K\\); then choose \\(\\delta_1&lt;1/(8K)\\) and density deficit below \\(1/4\\). Thus \\(|N-K|&lt;1/2\\), so \\(N=K\\). The curvature, separation, and profile assertions are the corresponding sheeting and Wang–Wei estimates. Their hypotheses include the full ambient derivative \\(\\mathcal A_v\\), not only tangential curvature. Wang–Wei's normalization is obtained by\n\\[\n\\widehat W=W/2,\\qquad \\widehat v(y)=v(y/\\sqrt2).\n\\]</p><p>Finally, integrate the discrepancy term in the energy-monotonicity formula from \\(r\\) to \\(2r\\):\n\\[\nr^{-m}\\int_{B_r(y)}q^2D\n\\le C(M_{2r}(y)-M_r(y)).\n\\]\nUniform density convergence proves the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Tube, mass, and Hardy estimates — original Lemma 6.2.** Fix the critical solution of Lemma 3.1 and let \\( \\Sigma_S=\\Sigma\\cap\\{s>S\\}. \\) For every fixed \\(P\\), increasing \\(S\\) makes …", "statement": "<p>**Tube, mass, and Hardy estimates — original Lemma 6.2.**</p><p>Fix the critical solution of Lemma 3.1 and let\n\\[\n\\Sigma_S=\\Sigma\\cap\\{s&gt;S\\}.\n\\]\nFor every fixed \\(P\\), increasing \\(S\\) makes\n\\[\n(z,t)\\mapsto z+t\\nu(z),\\qquad z\\in\\Sigma_{S/2},\\quad |t|\\le P,\n\\]\ninjective, with Jacobian \\(1+o_S(1)\\) and \\(q(z+t\\nu)=G(t)+o_S(1)\\).</p><p>For \\(r\\ge1\\), good-surface area in every radius-\\(r\\) ball is at most \\(Cr^m\\), and\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Sigma_{S/2}\\cap B_r(x)}D\\,dS\n\\le C\\int_{B_{r+1}(x)}q^3D\\,dx\n\\le Cr^{m-2}.\n\\]\nFor \\(y\\in Z\\), write \\(r=|z-y|\\), \\(e_r=(z-y)/r\\). Then\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Sigma_S\\cap\\{r&gt;R_0\\}}\n\\left((e_r\\cdot\\nu)^2r^{-m}+|H|r^{1-m}\\right)\\,dS\n\\le\\eta(R_0),\\qquad \\eta(R_0)\\to0.\n\\]\nThe same \\(\\eta\\) works for all \\(S\\) above one fixed threshold.</p><p>Choose a smooth ambient cutoff \\(0\\le\\chi\\le1\\), supported in \\(\\{s&gt;S\\}\\), equal to one on \\(\\{s\\ge2S\\}\\), with bounded first two derivatives. For nonnegative Lipschitz \\(F\\) supported in \\([1/2,2]\\), and every \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), \\(S\\) can be chosen so that\n\\[\n\\liminf_{R\\to\\infty}R^{-m}\\int_\\Sigma\\chi F(r/R)\\,dS\n\\ge Km\\omega_m\\int_0^\\infty t^{m-1}F(t)\\,dt-\\delta.\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\n\\limsup_{R\\to\\infty}\n\\frac1{\\sigma\\log R}\n\\int_{\\{R_0/2&lt;|x-y|&lt;4R\\}}q^3D|x-y|^{2-m}\\,dx\n\\le Km\\omega_m\\frac{(m-2)^2}{4}.\n\\]\nAll limiting assertions are uniform in \\(y\\in Z\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The fixed-width tube follows from curvature \\(O(1/s)\\), diverging sheet separation, and profile convergence. A collision would occur in one common good chart; distinct sheets are excluded by separation, and one sheet by uniqueness of projection. A unit tube, where \\(q\\) is bounded below, transfers energy bounds to area. Harnack compares \\(D\\) along that tube. Testing diffuse stability with a cutoff on \\(B_{2r+2}(x)\\) gives the defect bound, using \\(q^2\\le4e(v)\\).</p><p>The flux part of monotonicity gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\{|x-y|&gt;R_0\\}}|\\partial_rv|^2|x-y|^{-m}\\,dx\n\\le s_0\\omega_m(K-M_{R_0}(y)).\n\\]\nOn a unit tube,\n\\[\n|p(z+t\\nu)-\\nu(z)|\\le C\\sqrt{D(z)}.\n\\]\nChanging radial directions costs \\(C/r\\). Consequently the radial-normal surface integral is bounded by this flux tail and\n\\[\nC\\int_{\\Sigma_S\\cap\\{r&gt;R_0\\}}(Dr^{-m}+r^{-m-2})\\,dS.\n\\]\nDyadic shells bound the last terms by \\(Ca^{-2}\\), and the mean-curvature term by \\(Ca^{-1}\\), since \\(|H|\\le CD\\). Thus one may take\n\\[\n\\eta(R_0)\\le C\\left[\\sup_{y\\in Z}(K-M_{R_0-2}(y))+R_0^{-1}\\right].\n\\]</p><p>Mollify the distance \\(s\\) at scale \\(S/8\\), and compose with a smooth cutoff, to obtain\n\\[\n|\\nabla\\chi|\\le C/S,\\qquad|\\nabla^2\\chi|\\le C/S^2.\n\\]\nSparsity and a unit tube give\n\\[\n\\sup_{y\\in Z}\\mathcal H^m\n(\\Sigma\\cap\\{S&lt;s&lt;2S\\}\\cap B_r(y))=o(r^{m-2}).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\ne(v)=q^2/2-q^2D/4,\n\\]\nLemma 3.1 gives\n\\[\nR^{-m}\\int_{B_R(y)}q^2\\,dx\\to\\sigma K\\omega_m.\n\\]\nIts radial distributional consequence supplies the \\(F\\)-weighted ambient limit.</p><p>To control the phase tail, take a maximal unit-separated net on \\(\\Sigma\\). Every zero has a uniform positive energy in a fixed small ball. The density bound controls the number of net points, and phase decay gives uniformly \\(o_P(1)R^m\\) mass at distance greater than \\(P\\) from \\(\\Sigma\\). Points close to zeros with \\(s\\le2S\\) have negligible volume by sparsity; all remaining points belong to the injective good tube. Normal integration gives at most \\(\\sigma+o_S(1)\\). Choose \\(P\\), then \\(S\\), then let \\(R\\to\\infty\\), obtaining the surface-mass lower bound.</p><p>For the Hardy estimate put \\(a=(m-2)/2\\) and use \\(\\eta=|x-y|^{-a}\\), with inner and outer dyadic cutoffs, in diffuse stability. Cutoff costs are \\(O(1)\\). Stieltjes integration of the radial \\(q^2\\)-mass limit gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\{b&lt;|x-y|&lt;cR\\}}q^2|x-y|^{-m}\\,dx\n=\\sigma Km\\omega_m\\log R+o(\\log R).\n\\]\nThe right side of stability has coefficient \\(a^2\\). Divide by \\(\\sigma\\log R\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Nearest-sheet distance and weak Laplacian — original Lemma 6.3 and equation (6.10).** On \\(\\Sigma_S\\), for sufficiently large \\(S\\), let \\(l(z)\\) be the shortest Euclidean distance to a distinct sheet in a local good …", "statement": "<p>**Nearest-sheet distance and weak Laplacian — original Lemma 6.3 and equation (6.10).**</p><p>On \\(\\Sigma_S\\), for sufficiently large \\(S\\), let \\(l(z)\\) be the shortest Euclidean distance to a distinct sheet in a local good chart. This is chart independent,\n\\[\nl(z)\\to\\infty,\\qquad l(z)=o(s(z)).\n\\]\nLocally \\(l\\) is the minimum of the smooth distances to the one or two adjacent sheets. For an active branch \\(l_b\\), with nearest foot \\(z'=\\pi_b(z)\\),\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\Sigma l_b\n\\le |H(z)|+|H(z')|+\\frac C{s(z)}|\\nabla_\\Sigma l_b|^2.\n\\]\nEvery measurable active-foot selection has inverse multiplicity at most two, branch area Jacobian \\(1+o_S(1)\\), and\n\\[\ns(z')/s(z)=1+o_S(1),\\qquad\n|z'-y|/|z-y|=1+o_S(1)\\quad(y\\in Z).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(\\psi\\) is nonnegative, increasing, and concave, and\n\\[\n-\\psi''(l)/\\psi'(l)\\ge C/s,\\qquad 0\\le\\psi'(l)\\le L,\n\\]\nthen \\(w=\\min\\{T_0,\\psi(l)\\}\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\Sigma w\\le L(|H(z)|+|H(\\pi(z))|)\n\\]\nas a measure.</p>", "proof": "<p>The \\(K\\) sheets have heights \\(o(s)\\) and diverging separation. A nonadjacent sheet cannot minimize because the chord crosses an intervening zero first. Equivalently, the nearest distinct sheet has opposite oriented normal. Every improving competitor lies in the same buffered chart.</p><p>Projection is smooth: the target curvature is \\(O(s^{-1})\\), the distance is \\(o(s)\\), and the squared-distance Hessian near a minimizing foot is positive definite. With target normal \\(N\\) pointing toward the source,\n\\[\nz=z'+l_bN(z').\n\\]\nThe ambient distance Hessian has tangential eigenvalues\n\\[\n\\frac{\\kappa_i}{1+l_b\\kappa_i}\\le\\kappa_i\n\\]\nand zero normal eigenvalue. Restriction to the source removes a normal-normal component bounded by\n\\[\n(C/s)(1-(\\nu\\cdot N)^2)=(C/s)|\\nabla_\\Sigma l_b|^2.\n\\]\nThe restriction formula contributes at most \\(|H(z)|\\), proving the Laplacian bound.</p><p>The projection Jacobian is\n\\[\nJ_{\\pi_b}\n=\\frac{|\\nu(z)\\cdot N(z')|}\n{|\\det(\\operatorname{Id}+l_bA_N(z'))|}\n=1+o_S(1).\n\\]\nAll sources projecting to one target point lie in one good chart. Its normal line meets each small-slope graph at most once, and only its immediately adjacent graphs can supply sources. Thus the inverse multiplicity is at most two. Lipschitz continuity of \\(s\\) and distance to \\(y\\) gives the ratio assertions.</p><p>The finite minimum of the local smooth distance branches and the constant cap is locally semiconcave. Its singular Hessian is nonpositive, and its almost-everywhere Hessian is that of an active branch. Concavity of \\(\\psi\\) absorbs the gradient term. In particular \\(\\psi(l)=\\sqrt l\\) is admissible for large \\(S\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Green inequality with cap-independent collar errors — original Lemma 6.4.** Fix \\(F\\in C_c^\\infty((1/2,2))\\), nonnegative and positive near \\(1\\), normalized by \\( \\int_0^\\infty t^{m-1}F(t)\\,dt=m-2. \\) For \\(0<r<2R\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Green inequality with cap-independent collar errors — original Lemma 6.4.**</p><p>Fix \\(F\\in C_c^\\infty((1/2,2))\\), nonnegative and positive near \\(1\\), normalized by\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty t^{m-1}F(t)\\,dt=m-2.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;r&lt;2R\\), define\n\\[\n\\varphi_R(r)=\n\\int_r^{2R}t^{1-m}\n\\left(R^{-m}\\int_t^{2R}u^{m-1}F(u/R)\\,du\\right)\\,dt,\n\\]\nand set \\(\\varphi_R=0\\) for \\(r\\ge2R\\).</p><p>Suppose the weak inequality of Lemma 3.3 holds with \\(L\\le2\\), and \\(\\psi(l)\\) is uniformly bounded on each fixed range \\(S&lt;s&lt;A\\). Then\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR^{-m}\\int_\\Sigma w\\chi F(r/R)\\,dS\n\\le{}&amp;\nL\\int_{\\Sigma_S\\cap\\{R_0&lt;r&lt;2R\\}}\nr^{2-m}(|H(z)|+|H(\\pi(z))|)\\,dS\\\\\n&amp;+C_{R_0,S}+o_{R\\to\\infty;R_0,S}(\\log R)+T_0\\eta(R_0).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe constant and \\(o(\\log R)\\) term are independent of the cap \\(T_0\\) and uniform in \\(y\\in Z\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Direct differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\varphi_R''+\\frac{m-1}{r}\\varphi_R'=R^{-m}F(r/R),\\qquad\n0\\le\\varphi_R\\le r^{2-m},\n\\]\n\\[\nr|\\varphi_R'|+r^2|\\varphi_R''|\\le Cr^{2-m}.\n\\]\nTest the weak Laplacian inequality with \\(\\alpha\\chi\\varphi_R\\), where \\(\\alpha\\) vanishes on \\(r\\le R_0\\) and is one for \\(r\\ge2R_0\\). The support lies in the regular open surface.</p><p>For radial \\(f\\),\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\Sigma f\n=f''+(m-1)f'/r+(f'/r-f'')(e_r\\cdot\\nu)^2-Hf'(e_r\\cdot\\nu).\n\\]\nThe radial geometric error is bounded by\n\\[\nC((e_r\\cdot\\nu)^2r^{-m}+|H|r^{1-m}),\n\\]\nand hence contributes at most \\(T_0\\eta(R_0)\\).</p><p>Terms differentiating \\(\\alpha\\) lie in a fixed region, where \\(w\\le\\psi(l)\\le C_{R_0,S}\\), independently of the cap. Terms differentiating \\(\\chi\\) lie in \\(S&lt;s&lt;2S\\), where \\(w\\le C_S\\). Their cost is\n\\[\nC_S\\int_{\\Sigma\\cap\\{S&lt;s&lt;2S,\\ R_0&lt;r&lt;2R\\}}r^{2-m}\\,dS.\n\\]\nThe sparse-collar estimate in Lemma 3.2 makes each late dyadic-shell contribution tend to zero. Its Cesàro sum is \\(o(\\log R)\\). The main flat Laplacian produces the displayed left side.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Rough gap bound — original Lemma 6.5.** For the critical solution, at every sufficiently distant good zero, \\( l(z)\\le C\\big((\\log s(z))^2+s(z)^{1/10}\\big). \\) Consequently \\(l(z)^2=o(s(z))\\).", "statement": "<p>**Rough gap bound — original Lemma 6.5.**</p><p>For the critical solution, at every sufficiently distant good zero,\n\\[\nl(z)\\le C\\big((\\log s(z))^2+s(z)^{1/10}\\big).\n\\]\nConsequently \\(l(z)^2=o(s(z))\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 3.4 to \\(w=\\min\\{T_0,\\sqrt l\\}\\). Since \\(|H|\\le CD\\), the curvature integral is \\(O(1+\\log R)\\) by Lemma 3.2. The foot term obeys the same bound using its Jacobian, radial comparability, and inverse multiplicity two.</p><p>For a given zero \\(z\\), choose a nearest \\(y\\in Z\\), let \\(R=s(z)\\), and set \\(T_0=\\sqrt{l(z)}\\). In its good chart, an adjacent height difference \\(h=f_{i+1}-f_i&gt;0\\) solves\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}(a(x')\\nabla h)=\\mathcal M(f_{i+1})-\\mathcal M(f_i),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal M(f)=\\operatorname{div}\\frac{\\nabla f}{\\sqrt{1+|\\nabla f|^2}},\n\\]\nwith uniformly elliptic \\(a\\) and forcing bounded by \\(CR^{-2+1/10}\\). Rescaled Harnack yields\n\\[\n\\inf h\\ge ch(x'_z)-CR^{1/10}.\n\\]\nThus either \\(l(z)\\le C'R^{1/10}\\), or an area-\\(cR^m\\) patch has \\(l\\ge cl(z)\\). Shrink it so \\(F(r/R)\\ge c\\), and note that \\(\\chi=1\\) there for large \\(R\\). The Green inequality gives\n\\[\nc'T_0\\le C(1+\\log R)+T_0\\eta(R_0).\n\\]\nFix \\(R_0\\) with \\(\\eta(R_0)&lt;c'/2\\), absorb, and square.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Local regularity from any finite density threshold — original Corollary 6.6.** Fix \\(4\\le n\\le7\\) and \\(K_f<\\infty\\).", "statement": "<p>**Local regularity from any finite density threshold — original Corollary 6.6.**</p><p>Fix \\(4\\le n\\le7\\) and \\(K_f&lt;\\infty\\). There are \\(c&gt;0\\), \\(C&lt;\\infty\\), and \\(r_0&lt;\\infty\\), depending only on \\(n,K_f\\), such that every entire stable parameter-one solution with\n\\[\nM_r(z)\\le K_f,\\qquad r\\ge r_0,\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n|\\nabla v|\\ge C^{-1},\\qquad\n\\left|\\nabla\\left(\\frac{\\nabla v}{|\\nabla v|}\\right)\\right|\\le C/r\n\\quad\\text{on }B_{cr}(z)\\cap\\{|v|\\le0.9\\}.\n\\]</p><p>For any prescribed slope tolerance and \\(\\iota&gt;0\\), if \\(v(z)=0\\) and the zero set is confined to a sufficiently thin slab about a hyperplane through \\(z\\), then a smaller cylinder contains ordered graphs with the prescribed slope bound and\n\\[\n\\|\\nabla^2f_i\\|_\\infty+r^{1/2}[\\nabla^2f_i]_{C^{1/2}}\\le C'/r,\n\\qquad |H_i|\\le C'_\\iota r^{-2+\\iota}.\n\\]\nOn fixed windows about inner zeros, the solution converges smoothly to the oriented planar profile as \\(r\\to\\infty\\), uniformly over this class.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 3 makes every finite \\(K_f\\) subcritical. Apply [10, Theorem 3.2] with its tolerance \\(\\delta(K_f,n)\\) and \\(r^{-1}\\le\\delta\\); its permitted density is \\(K_f+\\delta\\). Its full ambient-direction estimate supplies the sheeting hypotheses. Then [10, Theorem 2.14, Theorem 4.8, and Lemmas 4.9–4.10] give the graphs, curvature, separation, and profile conclusions. Interior elliptic estimates upgrade profile approximation to smooth convergence. The constants \\(\\pm1\\) have an empty core band.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Penalized reach, isolated interfaces, and positive-foot transfer — original Proposition 7.7, with Lemmas 7.1 and 7.3–7.6, Remark 7.2, and Corollary 7.8.** Assume \\(v:\\mathbb R^7\\to(-1,1)\\) is entire and stable …", "statement": "<p>**Penalized reach, isolated interfaces, and positive-foot transfer — original Proposition 7.7, with Lemmas 7.1 and 7.3–7.6, Remark 7.2, and Corollary 7.8.**</p><p>Assume \\(v:\\mathbb R^7\\to(-1,1)\\) is entire and stable, \\(D&gt;0\\), and \\(M_\\infty=\\infty\\). Write \\(m=6\\),\n\\[\nK(R)=1+\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^7}M_R(x),\\quad\n\\Sigma=\\{v=0\\},\\quad\n\\nu=p/|p|,\\quad A=d\\nu|_{T\\Sigma},\\quad a=|A|,\\quad H=\\operatorname{tr}A.\n\\]\nThen \\(1\\le K(R)\\le1+CR\\) and \\(K(R)\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>Fix a smooth angular penalty \\(\\Gamma:(0,1]\\to(0,\\infty)\\), constructed by choosing \\(\\delta_1\\), then \\(L_\\Gamma\\), then \\(\\delta_2=\\delta_1e^{-L_\\Gamma}\\), so that\n\\[\ns^{-1}\\le\\Gamma(s)\\le(1+C\\delta_1)s^{-1},\n\\]\n\\(\\Gamma(s)=s^{-1}\\) for \\(s\\le1-\\delta_1\\), and \\(\\Gamma=G_1\\) for \\(s\\ge1-\\delta_2\\). On the transition,\n\\[\n\\Gamma'\\le0,\\qquad 0\\le(1-s)\\Gamma''\\le\\epsilon_1.\n\\]\nFor distinct zeros \\(z,y\\), with \\(z\\) regular, set\n\\[\n\\ell=|y-z|,\\quad e=(y-z)/\\ell,\\quad s=|\\nu(z)\\cdot e|,\n\\qquad\nd(z)=\\inf_{y\\in\\Sigma\\setminus\\{z\\}}\\ell\\Gamma(s),\n\\]\ninterpreting \\(s=0\\) as infinite cost.</p><p>The following conclusions hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(d\\) is finite and positive,\n\\[\nd\\max_i|\\lambda_i(A)|\\le2,\\qquad\n|(y-z)\\cdot\\nu(z)|\\le C_\\Gamma|y-z|^2/d(z).\n\\]\nSmall defect at a zero forces \\(d\\) arbitrarily large. There are zero-free tangent balls of radius \\(c_\\Gamma d\\) on both normal sides. Normal tubes over all bases with \\(d&gt;L\\) are jointly injective for \\(|t|&lt;cL\\), have uniformly positive Jacobian, and give\n\\[\n\\mathcal H^m(\\Sigma\\cap B_{2R}\\cap\\{d&gt;L\\})\\le CR^{m+1}/L\n\\quad(L\\le R).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. At large reach, with\n\\[\nh=d\\exp\\{-C\\sqrt{\\log(K(d)+1)}\\},\n\\]\none has \\(M_h(z)\\le K_0\\), for a fixed \\(K_0\\), and \\(h\\ge d^{1-o(1)}\\). A smaller \\(h\\)-scale neighborhood contains one zero graph, on which\n\\[\n|A|+h^{1/2}[A]_{C^{1/2}}\\le Ch^{-1},\\quad\n|\\nabla_\\Sigma A|\\le Ch^{-2},\\quad |H|\\le Ch^{-2},\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\nabla_\\Sigma H|+|\\nabla_\\Sigma^2H|\\le Ch^{-3-\\gamma}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. A zero-free tangent ball of radius \\(r\\) at \\(x\\), with inward normal \\(N\\), gives\n\\[\nx\\in\\Sigma_{\\rm reg},\\quad D(x)+a(x)\\le C/r,\\quad\nH_N(x)\\le Cr^{-1-\\gamma}.\n\\]\nAfter choosing the sign positive in the ball,\n\\[\nU(x+sN)\\ge s-C(1+s)/r\\quad(0\\le s\\le\\log r).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Given \\(\\eta_0&gt;0\\), choose the penalty sufficiently close to \\(1/s\\). The reach is locally semiconcave, its singular surface Laplacian is nonpositive, and almost everywhere\n\\[\n|\\nabla_\\Sigma d|\\le\nC_\\Gamma\\exp\\{C_\\Gamma\\sqrt{\\log(K(d)+1)}\\}.\n\\]\nAt nonplateau or diagonal active contacts,\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\Sigma d\\le\n(1+\\eta_0+C_\\Gamma d^{-\\gamma})a^2d\n+B\\frac{|\\nabla_\\Sigma d|^2}{d},\n\\qquad B\\le1+C_\\Gamma d^{-\\gamma}.\n\\]\nIf \\(ad\\) is sufficiently small, one may use\n\\[\nB\\le\\eta_0+C_\\Gamma ad+C_\\Gamma d^{-\\gamma},\n\\qquad |\\nabla_\\Sigma d|\\ge c_\\Gamma.\n\\]\nAt plateau contacts,\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\Sigma d\\le\nG_1(|H(z)|+H_{N_y}(y)_+)\n+\\eta_0\\frac{|\\nabla_\\Sigma d|^2}{d},\n\\qquad N_y=-e.\n\\]\nPositive receiving-foot charges have inverse multiplicity at most one and inverse area factor\n\\[\nJ_{y\\to z}\n=\\frac{|\\det(\\operatorname{Id}+\\ell A_{N_y}(y))|}{s}\\le C_\\Gamma,\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\nd(y)\\le d(z)=G_1\\ell,\\qquad a(y)+D(y)\\le C_\\Gamma/d(z).\n\\]\nIf \\(a(y)\\ell\\) is sufficiently small,\n\\[\nJ_{y\\to z}\\le1+C\\delta_2+Ca(y)\\ell.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. For \\(X=\\{z\\in\\Sigma:D(z)\\ge\\delta\\}\\), fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), and fixed \\(L_0\\), reach is uniformly bounded at good bases within distance \\(10L_0\\) of \\(X\\). The source reach is also uniformly bounded when a receiving foot lies in that collar.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 5.1–5.4 establish conclusions 1–4. The penalty itself is obtained from a nonincreasing smooth cutoff \\(\\chi\\) by\n\\[\n\\Gamma(s)=\\frac1{1-\\delta_1}\n-\\int_{1-\\delta_1}^s\nu^{-2}\\chi\\!\\left(\\frac{\\log(\\delta_1/(1-u))}{L_\\Gamma}\\right)\\,du\n\\]\nfor \\(s\\ge1-\\delta_1\\), and \\(1/s\\) below. Its differentiated bounds follow by first decreasing \\(\\delta_1\\), then increasing \\(L_\\Gamma\\).</p><p>For conclusion 5, tangent balls at bases and second feet give \\(D\\le C_\\Gamma/d\\), measured by source reach at a receiving foot. Exponential defect comparison gives a uniform positive lower bound for \\(D\\) within a fixed distance of \\(X\\). Combining the bounds controls the corresponding reach. This is original Corollary 7.8.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Elementary reach geometry and delayed density control — original Lemmas 7.1 and 7.3, and Remark 7.2.** Under Proposition 5, conclusions 1 and the density-window assertion in conclusion 2 hold.", "statement": "<p>**Elementary reach geometry and delayed density control — original Lemmas 7.1 and 7.3, and Remark 7.2.**</p><p>Under Proposition 5, conclusions 1 and the density-window assertion in conclusion 2 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Local graph representation bounds the chord quotient near a regular base, and distant competitors have positive cost, proving positivity. If every zero were in the tangent plane, phase decay would give surface-order energy and finite density. Thus a finite competitor exists. A principal geodesic gives\n\\[\n\\ell/s\\to2/|\\lambda_i|,\n\\]\nand the upper penalty bound gives the chord estimate.</p><p>If \\(D(z_j)\\to0\\), Proposition 1 gives convergence to one affine inverse profile on every fixed ball. All nearby zeros form one graph with slope and curvature tending to zero. Any competitor of bounded cost has bounded chord length, but then \\(s=o(1)\\ell\\), contradicting \\(\\ell\\Gamma(s)\\ge\\ell/s\\).</p><p>The chord estimate excludes zeros from tangent balls of radius \\(c_\\Gamma d\\). If \\(d(z)&gt;L\\) and \\(|t|&lt;cL\\),\n\\[\n|z+t\\nu(z)-y|^2-t^2\n\\ge(1-2cC_\\Gamma)|y-z|^2&gt;0.\n\\]\nThus \\(z\\) is the unique nearest zero. The curvature bound makes\n\\[\n\\det(\\operatorname{Id}+tA)\\ge(1-2c)^m&gt;0.\n\\]\nVolume packing proves the area estimate.</p><p>For delayed monotonicity, translate \\(z=0\\), \\(\\nu(z)=e_n\\). The zeros in \\(B_{3R}\\) lie in\n\\[\n|x_n|\\le CR^2/d.\n\\]\nTesting the divergence-free stress tensor \\(\\mathbb S\\) against \\(x_ne_n\\zeta^2\\), and using\n\\[\n\\mathbb S_{nn}\\ge cq^2(1-p_n^2),\\qquad\n|\\mathbb Se_n|^2\\le Cq^2\\mathbb S_{nn},\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\int_{B_R}q^2(1-p_n^2)\n\\le C\\int_{B_{2R}}q^2(x_n/R)^2.\n\\]\nOutside the slab enlarged by \\(\\sqrt R\\), phase decay is exponentially small; inside it,\n\\[\n(x_n/R)^2\\le C((R/d)^2+R^{-1}).\n\\]\nFor a fixed \\(B_0\\ge8\\), energy monotonicity consequently gives\n\\[\n0\\le M_R(z)-M_{R/B_0}(z)\n\\le C((R/d)^2+R^{-1})M_{B_0R}(z)\n\\quad(10\\le R\\le cd).\n\\]</p><p>Set \\(R_j=dB_0^{-j}\\), \\(k_j=M_{R_j}(z)\\), and\n\\[\nb_j=C(B_0^{-2j}+R_j^{-1}).\n\\]\nThe delayed recurrence is\n\\[\nk_j-k_{j+1}\\le b_jk_{j-1}.\n\\]\nTake a fixed endpoint radius and starting index so \\(b_j&lt;1/8\\), and put\n\\[\nJ=\\left\\lceil C_0\\sqrt{\\log(K(d)+1)}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nFor large \\(d\\), the relevant early radii exceed \\(d^{1/2}\\). If a halving \\(k_{j+1}&lt;k_j/2\\) occurred after \\(J-2\\), it would force\n\\[\nk_{j-1}&gt;k_j/(2b_j)&gt;4k_j\n\\]\nand propagate backward through \\(cJ\\) indices on which \\(b_i\\le Ce^{-cJ}\\). Multiplication would give\n\\[\nK(d)\\ge c\\exp(c'J^2),\n\\]\ncontradicting large \\(C_0\\). Thus there is no such halving.</p><p>Hence \\(k_{j-1}\\le2k_j\\) and\n\\[\nk_{j+1}\\ge(1-2b_j)k_j.\n\\]\nThe sum of the \\(b_j\\) up to the fixed-scale endpoint is uniformly bounded. The endpoint density is uniformly bounded by the energy-integrand bound, so \\(k_J\\le K_0\\). Increasing \\(C\\) in the definition of \\(h\\) makes \\(h\\le R_J\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Half-line barrier and two parity projections — original Lemmas 7.4–7.5.** Under Proposition 5, the isolated-graph and mean-curvature derivative assertions in conclusion 2 hold.", "statement": "<p>**Half-line barrier and two parity projections — original Lemmas 7.4–7.5.**</p><p>Under Proposition 5, the isolated-graph and mean-curvature derivative assertions in conclusion 2 hold.</p><p>For every fixed \\(a_0\\in\\mathbb R\\), the auxiliary function\n\\[\nv_{a_0}(t)=g'(t)\\left[\n1+\\int_{a_0}^{t}(g'(s))^{-2}\\int_s^\\infty g'(r)\\,dr\\,ds\n\\right],\\qquad t\\ge a_0,\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n(-\\partial_t^2+3g^2-1)v_{a_0}=1,\\qquad\n0&lt;c_{a_0}\\le v_{a_0}\\le C_{a_0},\n\\]\nwith all fixed derivatives bounded, uniformly for \\(a_0\\) in a compact interval.</p>", "proof": "<p>Writing \\(v_{a_0}=g'b\\),\n\\[\n(-\\partial_t^2+3g^2-1)(g'b)\n=-\\frac1{g'}((g')^2b')'=1.\n\\]\nThe formula is positive and tends to \\(1/2\\) at infinity; the derivative bounds follow by differentiation and the equation.</p><p>Apply Proposition 4 at the fixed density \\(K_0\\) and radius \\(h\\). Reach confinement has relative thickness \\(O(h/d)\\), which can be made sufficiently small by increasing the fixed constant defining \\(h\\). Sheeting gives small slope,\n\\[\n|A|+h^{1/2}[A]_{C^{1/2}}\\le Ch^{-1},\\qquad\n|H|\\le C_\\iota h^{-2+\\iota}.\n\\]\nAn additional inner graph would meet the normal line at distance \\(O(h)\\), contradicting the reach. Fixed-window profile convergence and unit-scale derivative bounds are uniform.</p><p>Fix an inner zero, write its graph \\(X(y)\\), and use Fermi coordinates \\(X(y)+t\\nu(y)\\). Let \\(R_h=h^\\epsilon\\), with \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) chosen last, and work in finitely many nested boxes of that scale. Put\n\\[\n\\phi(y,t)=v(X(y)+t\\nu(y))-g(t).\n\\]\nIt tends uniformly to zero. At distant normal faces, phase decay and elliptic estimates give superpolynomially small errors.</p><p>Interpolation between the \\(C^{1/2}\\) curvature bound and high fixed unit-scale bounds gives, for the needed finite orders,\n\\[\n|\\partial_y^kA|\\le Ch^{-5/4}\\quad(1\\le k\\le30),\n\\qquad\n|\\partial_y^kH|\\le C_\\epsilon h^{-2+\\epsilon}\\quad(0\\le k\\le30).\n\\]\nThe exact coordinate equation is\n\\[\n(-\\Delta_y-\\partial_t^2+V_g)\\phi\n=K_tg'+K_t\\phi_t+(g_t^{ij}-\\delta^{ij})\\partial_{ij}\\phi\n+b_t^i\\partial_i\\phi-3g\\phi^2-\\phi^3,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nV_g=3g^2-1,\\qquad K_t=\\operatorname{tr}[A(\\operatorname{Id}+tA)^{-1}].\n\\]\nThrough the required derivative orders,\n\\[\nK_t=H-t|A|^2+t^2\\operatorname{tr}A^3+O(h^{-4+C\\epsilon}),\n\\]\n\\[\nK_t=O(h^{-2+C\\epsilon}),\\qquad\ng_t^{ij}-\\delta^{ij},\\,b_t^i=O(h^{-1+C\\epsilon}).\n\\]\nUse \\(v_0\\) separately on the two half-boxes. After division by that positive supersolution, comparison is valid; decaying face barriers and the forcing bound give\n\\[\n\\|\\phi\\|_{C^{15}}\\le C_\\epsilon h^{-2+C\\epsilon}\n\\]\non smaller boxes.</p><p>The one-dimensional operator\n\\[\nL_t=-\\partial_t^2+V_g\n\\]\nhas kernel spanned by \\(g'\\) and a positive spectral gap on its \\(L^2\\)-orthogonal complement. For\n\\[\n(-\\Delta_y+L_t)w=P^\\perp F,\\qquad P^\\perp w=w,\n\\]\nthe regularized \\(L_t^2\\)-norm of each tangential derivative is a subsolution for \\(-\\Delta_y+\\lambda\\). Comparison and the equation control the corresponding \\(H_t^2\\)-norms and values at \\(t=0\\), with exponentially small boundary contributions. This applies separately to even and odd parts and also to differentiated equations.</p><p>Multiply \\(\\phi\\) by an even normal cutoff. Its commutators and the restored kernel tails are superpolynomially small. On the whole normal line write\n\\[\n\\phi=u_0(y)g'(t)+\\phi^\\perp,\\qquad\n(-\\Delta_y+L_t)\\phi=Hg'+E.\n\\]\nThe first parity calculation gives\n\\[\nE=-t|A|^2g'+O(h^{-3+C\\epsilon}),\\qquad\nE^{\\rm even}=O(h^{-3+C\\epsilon})\n\\]\nin the required \\(L_t^2\\) derivative norms. The even orthogonal inverse bounds \\(\\phi^\\perp(y,0)\\) and its derivatives. Since \\(\\phi(y,0)=0\\), it bounds \\(u_0\\) through order nine. The kernel equation\n\\[\n-\\Delta_yu_0=H+E_0\n\\]\nthen bounds \\(H\\) through order seven by \\(Ch^{-3+C\\epsilon}\\).</p><p>Positive tangential derivatives of \\(-t|A|^2g'\\) gain \\(h^{-1/4}\\). The full orthogonal equation therefore gives\n\\[\n\\|\\partial_y^k\\phi\\|_{L_t^2}\\le Ch^{-2-1/4+C\\epsilon}\n\\quad(1\\le k\\le8).\n\\]\nReturn to the equation for a second even projection. The leading odd term still vanishes; every differentiated cubic-curvature term gains \\(h^{-1/4}\\), and every horizontal metric error contains a positive tangential derivative of \\(\\phi\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\|\\partial_y^kE^{\\rm even}\\|_{L_t^2}\n\\le Ch^{-3-1/4+C\\epsilon}\\quad(1\\le k\\le6).\n\\]\nThe even inverse, the zero condition, and the kernel equation give\n\\[\n|\\partial_yH|+|\\partial_y^2H|\\le Ch^{-3-1/4+C\\epsilon}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\epsilon\\) so all exponent losses are below \\(1/8\\), obtaining a fixed \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\).</p><p>Finally rescale the original graph by \\(h^{-1}\\). Its \\(C^{2,1/2}\\) norm is bounded, and \\(hH\\) has bounded \\(C^{1,1}\\) norm. Differentiating the prescribed-mean-curvature equation and applying Schauder estimates gives \\(|\\nabla_\\Sigma A|\\le Ch^{-2}\\). Repeating at inner centers proves uniformity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**One-sided trace improvement from a tangent phase ball — original Lemma 7.6.** Under Proposition 5, suppose a zero-free open ball of radius \\(r\\) is tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\) at \\(x\\), with inward normal \\(N\\).", "statement": "<p>**One-sided trace improvement from a tangent phase ball — original Lemma 7.6.**</p><p>Under Proposition 5, suppose a zero-free open ball of radius \\(r\\) is tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\) at \\(x\\), with inward normal \\(N\\). For all sufficiently large \\(r\\), conclusion 3 of Proposition 5 holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the sign positive inside the ball. For inward depth \\(0&lt;s&lt;\\sqrt r\\), define\n\\[\nw(s)=g'(s)\\int_0^s(g'(t))^{-2}\n\\int_t^\\infty(g'(u))^2\\,du\\,dt.\n\\]\nThen \\(w(0)=0\\),\n\\[\n(-\\partial_s^2+V_g)w=g',\\qquad\n|w|+|w'|\\le C(1+s)g'.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large fixed \\(C_0\\),\n\\[\nv_*=g-C_0w/r\n\\]\nis a positive strict radial subsolution in this annulus. Its linear correction contributes \\(C_0g'/r\\), dominating the radial drift and quadratic error. Multiplying by \\(1-e^{-c\\sqrt r}\\) handles the inner boundary by phase decay. Sliding its amplitude from zero, with Hopf comparison at outer zeros, yields comparison with \\(v\\).</p><p>Inverting \\(g\\) for \\(s\\le\\log r\\) gives the stated \\(U\\)-bound. At the contact,\n\\[\n\\partial_Nv\\ge g'(0)-C/r&gt;0,\n\\]\nso \\(x\\) is regular, \\(N=\\nu\\), and \\(D(x)\\le C/r\\). The ball gives \\(\\lambda_i(A_N)\\ge-1/r\\). The zero-level identity and \\(|\\nabla D|\\le CD\\) give \\(|H_N|\\le C/r\\), hence \\(a\\le C/r\\).</p><p>For the one-sided gain, place \\(x=0,N=e_n\\), and work in \\(B_P\\), \\(P=\\alpha_0\\log r\\), with \\(\\alpha_0\\) small. Diffuse estimates give\n\\[\nD=O_{C^k}(r^{-1+C\\alpha_0}),\\qquad\nT,\\ U-s=O_{C^k}(r^{-1/2+C\\alpha_0}),\n\\qquad\n\\partial_sT=O(r^{-1+C\\alpha_0}).\n\\]\nFor \\(F=G(s)D\\),\n\\[\n(-\\Delta+2-G)F=2G(s)|T(x',0)|^2+\\mathcal E,\n\\qquad\n\\|\\mathcal E\\|_\\infty\\le Cr^{-3/2+C\\alpha_0}.\n\\]\nThe zero-boundary source solution is nonnegative and even. The error solution has interior \\(C^1\\)-norm of the displayed error size. Decompose the remaining homogeneous solution into \\(u_++u_-\\), with upper- and lower-hemisphere boundary data. Reflection gives \\(\\partial_su_-(0)\\le0\\), and local estimates give \\(|\\partial_su_+(0)|\\le Cu_+(0)\\).</p><p>At \\(x_*=(0,P/2)\\), positivity and the inward comparison, integrated using\n\\[\nD\\le2(1-\\partial_sU),\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\nu_+(x_*)\\le C(1+P)r^{-1}G(P/2)+Cr^{-3/2+C\\alpha_0}.\n\\]\nWriting \\(A_\\partial\\) for the upper-boundary integral of \\(F\\), comparison with constant potentials \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\nu_+(0)\\le CP^Ce^{-P}A_\\partial,\n\\qquad\nu_+(x_*)\\ge c_\\zeta\ne^{-(\\sqrt2+\\zeta)\\sqrt5P/2}A_\\partial.\n\\]\nThe lower Poisson bound follows by propagation in a fixed wide cylinder along the segment to the boundary, followed by fixed-distance Harnack and tangent-ball barriers.</p><p>Since\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}2-1-\\frac{\\sqrt2}2&lt;0,\n\\]\nfirst choose small \\(\\zeta\\), then small \\(\\alpha_0\\). The preceding estimates yield \\(u_+(0)\\le Cr^{-1-\\gamma}\\). The even source has zero derivative, the lower-boundary part has nonpositive derivative, and the error is smaller. Therefore \\(F_s(0)\\le Cr^{-1-\\gamma}\\), and the zero-level identity gives the asserted upper bound for \\(H_N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Weak reach calculus and positive-foot counting — original Proposition 7.7.** Under Proposition 5, conclusion 4 holds, including diagonal contacts, angular-transition contacts, the nonpositive singular Laplacian, and …", "statement": "<p>**Weak reach calculus and positive-foot counting — original Proposition 7.7.**</p><p>Under Proposition 5, conclusion 4 holds, including diagonal contacts, angular-transition contacts, the nonpositive singular Laplacian, and the multiplicity-one transfer of positive plateau charges.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a compact regular patch, reach is bounded above and below. Relevant off-diagonal competitors with chord length at least \\(\\varepsilon\\) have angles bounded away from zero and uniformly bounded upper Hessians in the base variable.</p><p>For short chords, write the local graph as \\(X(u)\\) and the second point as \\(X(u+t\\omega)\\). Their reciprocal unmodified reach is\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\nu(u)\\cdot(X(u+t\\omega)-X(u))|}\n{|X(u+t\\omega)-X(u)|^2}.\n\\]\nCancel \\(t^2\\) by the integral Taylor formula. On relevant branches the absolute value stays bounded away from zero, so reciprocation gives uniformly \\(C^2\\) upper branches, including diagonal limits. Compactness realizes an active off-diagonal or diagonal parameter. The infimum of these branches is locally semiconcave. Its singular Hessian is nonpositive; at Alexandrov points, active upper branches give upper Hessian bounds.</p><p>For an off-diagonal contact choose \\(N=\\pm\\nu(z)\\) so \\(N\\cdot e=s&gt;0\\). The region\n\\[\n\\{z+\\ell'e':N\\cdot e'&gt;0,\\ \\ell'\\Gamma(N\\cdot e')&lt;d\\}\n\\]\ncontains no zeros. After division by \\(d\\), its smooth compact radial boundary has a uniform interior rolling ball. Lemma 5.3 at the second foot gives regularity and\n\\[\na(y)+D(y)\\le C_\\Gamma/d,\\qquad\nH_{N_y}(y)\\le C_\\Gamma d^{-1-\\gamma}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(\\Gamma=1/s\\), put\n\\[\nr=d/2,\\qquad b=e-sN,\\qquad M=\\operatorname{Id}+rA_N.\n\\]\nReflect a tangent frame across \\(e^\\perp\\) to the second foot and move the points along the corresponding geodesics. Differentiating the touching chord gives\n\\[\n\\nabla_\\Sigma r=-Mb/s\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_\\Sigma d\\le{}&amp;\na^2d+\n\\left(2-\\frac2{1+r\\max_i|\\lambda_i|}\\right)\n\\frac{|\\nabla_\\Sigma d|^2}{d}\\\\\n&amp;+\\frac{H_{N_y}(y)-(1-2s^2)H_N(z)-\\ell b\\cdot\\nabla_\\Sigma H_N(z)}{s^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTo obtain the gradient coefficient, differentiate\n\\[\n|y-z|^2/2-rN\\cdot(y-z),\n\\]\nand use \\(0\\le M\\le(1+r\\max|\\lambda_i|)\\operatorname{Id}\\) and\n\\[\nb\\cdot\\nabla r=-b\\cdot Mb/s\n\\le-\\frac{s|\\nabla r|^2}{1+r\\max|\\lambda_i|}.\n\\]\nThe gradient coefficient is at most one and is \\(O(ad)\\) for small \\(ad\\).</p><p>If \\(s\\ge d^{-\\gamma'}\\), the trace quotient is \\(O(d^{-1-\\gamma''})\\) from above by Lemmas 5.2–5.3. If \\(s&lt;d^{-\\gamma'}\\), the second point lies in the isolated graph. Taylor expansion cancels the first two mean-curvature terms:\n\\[\nH_{N_y}(y)-H_N(z)-\\ell b\\cdot\\nabla H_N(z)\n=O(\\ell^2h^{-3-\\gamma}).\n\\]\nThe same error bound follows. When \\(ad\\) is small, \\(|b|\\) is bounded below in this angular range and the first-variation formula gives \\(|\\nabla d|\\ge c_\\Gamma\\); otherwise \\(a^2d\\ge c_\\Gamma/d\\). These facts absorb the trace error.</p><p>For the gradient bound, long chords give \\(|\\nabla d|\\le Cd/h\\). Short chords lie in the isolated graph and satisfy\n\\[\n-2sb=\\ell A_Nb+O(\\ell^2/h^2),\n\\]\ngiving \\(|\\nabla d|\\le Cd^2/h^2\\). Both have the stated subpolynomial bound.</p><p>At a diagonal contact put \\(S=-A_N\\), with top positive eigenvalue \\(\\lambda=2/d\\). Signed geodesic expansion and maximality imply\n\\[\n\\nabla_vS(v,v)=0\n\\]\non the top eigenspace; Codazzi and polarization make \\(\\nabla S\\) vanish on triples in that eigenspace. For a unit extension of an active top eigenvector, prescribe\n\\[\n\\langle\\nabla_iv_1,v_j\\rangle\n=\\frac{\\nabla_iS_{1j}}{\\lambda-\\lambda_j}\n\\quad(\\lambda_j&lt;\\lambda).\n\\]\nUsing\n\\[\n\\Delta S=\\nabla^2\\operatorname{tr}S+(\\operatorname{tr}S)S^2-a^2S\n\\]\ngives for \\(f=S(v_1,v_1)\\)\n\\[\n\\Delta f\\ge-a^2\\lambda-C(|\\nabla^2H|+a^2|H|)\n+\\frac{|\\nabla f|^2}{\\lambda}.\n\\]\nDifferentiating \\(2/f\\) yields\n\\[\n\\Delta d\\le a^2d+\\frac{|\\nabla d|^2}{d}\n+Cd^2(|\\nabla^2H|+a^2|H|).\n\\]\nLemma 5.2 absorbs the last term. Diagonal contact has \\(ad\\ge2\\), and \\(|\\nabla d|\\le Cd^2|\\nabla A|\\).</p><p>For \\(s\\ge1-\\delta_1\\), put\n\\[\nc_1=\\Gamma-s\\Gamma',\\qquad Z_1=c_1e+\\Gamma'N.\n\\]\nSecond-foot stationarity gives \\(N_y=-Z_1/|Z_1|\\). Rotate a tangent frame from \\(N\\) to \\(Z_1/|Z_1|\\). Writing \\(w_i=v_i'-v_i\\), differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\nabla d=(-c_1\\operatorname{Id}+\\ell\\Gamma'A_N)b\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta d\\le{}&amp;\n|Z_1|H_{N_y}+H_NZ_1\\cdot N+\\ell\\Gamma'b\\cdot\\nabla H_N\n-\\ell\\Gamma'sa^2\\\\\n&amp;+\\sum_i\\left\\{\n\\frac{c_1}{\\ell}|P_{e^\\perp}w_i|^2\n+2\\Gamma'w_i\\cdot A_Nv_i\n+\\ell\\Gamma''\n\\left[b\\cdot A_Nv_i+\\frac{(N-se)\\cdot w_i}{\\ell}\\right]^2\n\\right\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe rotation estimates are\n\\[\nw_i=O(|b|)N+O(|b|^2),\\quad\nP_{e^\\perp}w_i=O(|b|^2),\\quad\n(N-se)\\cdot w_i=O(|b|^3).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\Gamma''|b|^2\\le C\\epsilon_1\\), the algebraic terms are bounded by\n\\[\n(1+C(\\epsilon_1+\\delta_1))a^2d+Ca|b|^2+C|b|^4/d.\n\\]\nYoung's inequality, the gradient formula for small \\(ad\\), and \\(|b|^2\\le C\\delta_1\\) otherwise give the required coefficients after choosing \\(\\epsilon_1,\\delta_1\\) small. Before the plateau, \\(|b|\\) has a positive penalty-dependent lower bound, so the trace errors are absorbed as above. On the plateau,\n\\[\n\\nabla d=-G_1b,\\qquad N_y=-e,\n\\]\nand only \\(C|b|^4/d\\) remains, giving the plateau inequality.</p><p>Finally, for a plateau pair,\n\\[\nz=y+\\ell N_y.\n\\]\nThe implicit function theorem gives a local inverse parametrization. Tangential projection of its derivative leaves \\(\\operatorname{Id}+\\ell A_{N_y}\\), while projection between tangent planes has area factor \\(s\\). Thus its inverse area Jacobian is the claimed quotient. The reverse normal chord gives \\(d(y)\\le G_1\\ell=d(z)\\).</p><p>If \\(H_{N_y}(y)&gt;0\\), the normal orientation is unique. All bases sending positive charge to \\(y\\) lie on the same ray. Two at lengths \\(0&lt;\\ell_1&lt;\\ell_2\\) are impossible: the nearer base is a strictly cheaper competitor for the farther base, with the same angle. Hence positive inverse multiplicity is at most one. Countably many inverse charts, measurable selection, and the area formula justify transfer for arbitrary nonnegative weights; critical images have zero surface measure.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Sharp normal means and strengthened surface stability — original Lemmas 8.1–8.3 and Proposition 8.4.** For a fixed ambient dimension, use the diffuse variables of Proposition 1 and the zero-surface conventions of …", "statement": "<p>**Sharp normal means and strengthened surface stability — original Lemmas 8.1–8.3 and Proposition 8.4.**</p><p>For a fixed ambient dimension, use the diffuse variables of Proposition 1 and the zero-surface conventions of Proposition 2. No global density bound is assumed.</p><p>For every \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), choose \\(P\\) sufficiently large and then \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) sufficiently small. Every zero with \\(0&lt;D(z)&lt;\\delta\\) satisfies\n\\[\ncD(z)\\le I_P(z)\\le C_PD(z),\\qquad\nI_P(z)\\ge(1-\\epsilon)a(z)^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n|H(z)|\\le\\left(\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}+\\epsilon\\right)I_P(z).\n\\]\nThe lower comparison constant is independent of \\(P\\ge1\\).</p><p>For every \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), there are a fixed \\(S\\ge10\\) and \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) such that \\(D(z)&lt;\\delta\\) gives one regular graph in \\(B_{4S}(z)\\), and\n\\[\n|\\nabla_\\Sigma H(z)|^2\n\\le\\frac{\\epsilon}{S^m}\n\\int_{B_S(z)\\cap\\Sigma_{\\rm reg}}D^2\\,dS.\n\\]</p><p>For every compactly supported Lipschitz \\(\\psi\\) on \\(\\{z\\in\\Sigma:D(z)&lt;\\delta\\}\\),\n\\[\n(1-\\epsilon)\\int_\\Sigma\\frac{a^2+I_P}{2}\\psi^2\\,dS\n\\le\\int_\\Sigma|\\nabla_\\Sigma\\psi|^2\\,dS.\n\\]\nIn the lifted computation, the gradient coefficient approaches \\(\\sigma\\), and the negative coefficient approaches \\(\\sigma(a^2+I_P)/2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemma 6.2 and physical stability, discarding the nonnegative subtracted variance. The tangential metric differs by \\(O(\\alpha_0)\\). Cutoff-derivative terms are bounded, after Young's inequality, by\n\\[\nC_{\\alpha_0,\\eta}e^{-c\\alpha_0/a}\\psi^2=o(D)\\psi^2.\n\\]\nThus the gradient part is at most\n\\[\n(1+C\\alpha_0+\\eta)\\sigma|\\nabla_\\Sigma\\psi|^2+o(D)\\psi^2.\n\\]</p><p>Expansion of \\(J\\), evenness, and\n\\[\n-\\int_{\\mathbb R}sGG'\\,ds=\\sigma/2\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\n-\\int\\beta^2J_sGG'\\,ds\n=\\frac{\\sigma}{2}(H^2-a^2)+o(D)\n=-\\frac{\\sigma}{2}a^2+o(D).\n\\]</p><p>The potential term is nonpositive because \\(|U(z+s\\nu)|\\le|s|\\). On each normal side,\n\\[\nr-U_\\pm(r)\\ge\\frac12\\int_0^rD(z\\pm t\\nu)\\,dt.\n\\]\nOn a fixed segment,\n\\[\n|p(z+s\\nu)-\\nu|+|U(z+s\\nu)-s|\\le C_PD(z).\n\\]\nIndeed \\(Tp=-\\nabla D/2\\), so\n\\[\n|T\\nu|\\le C_PD+C_P\\sqrt D\\,|p-\\nu|,\n\\]\nand Gronwall applies.</p><p>After normalization, the potential gain is at least\n\\[\n-\\frac12(G^3)'(s)\\int_0^s\\mathcal D(0,t)\\,dt.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(P\\), truncate the inner integral to \\([-P,P]\\), and integrate over \\([-Q,Q]\\):\n\\[\n-\\frac12\\int_{-Q}^{Q}(G^3)'h_P\n=\\frac12\\int_{-P}^{P}G^3\\mathcal D\n-\\frac{G(Q)^3}{2}\\int_{-P}^{P}\\mathcal D.\n\\]\nHarnack bounds the last integral uniformly. Choose \\(Q\\) after \\(P\\), then shrink the defect threshold. This gives potential gain at least \\((1-\\eta)\\sigma I_P/2\\).</p><p>Lemma 6.1 supplies \\(I_P\\ge cD\\), so the \\(o(D)\\) errors are absorbed. Choose the tolerances, then \\(\\alpha_0\\), then the defect threshold. The strengthened surface inequality follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Sharp means and averaged mean derivative — original Lemmas 8.1–8.2.** The pointwise normal-average estimates and averaged mean-derivative estimate in Proposition 6 hold.", "statement": "<p>**Sharp means and averaged mean derivative — original Lemmas 8.1–8.2.**</p><p>The pointwise normal-average estimates and averaged mean-derivative estimate in Proposition 6 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Harnack comparison along a fixed normal interval gives \\(cD\\le I_P\\le C_PD\\). Proposition 2 gives the mean-curvature bound.</p><p>For curvature, use its normalized limit \\(F=G\\mathcal D\\), with\n\\[\n(-\\Delta+2-G)F=2G|\\mathcal T|^2.\n\\]\nThe function \\(|\\mathcal T|^2\\) is horizontally subharmonic. On product cylinders, the positive Dirichlet Green kernel evaluated at \\((0,s)\\) is horizontally radial. Spherical averaging therefore bounds the source solution below by the constant-horizontal source with value \\(|\\mathcal T(0)|^2\\). Exhaustion gives\n\\[\nF(0,s)\\ge|\\mathcal T(0)|^2f(s),\n\\qquad\n(-\\partial_s^2+2-G)f=2G,\n\\]\nwith \\(f\\ge0\\) bounded. Bounded entire solutions with this source are unique by comparison with exponential barriers; translations make the limit independent of \\(x'\\). Since\n\\[\n(-\\partial_s^2+2-G)G=2G^2,\n\\]\nintegration against \\(G\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac1\\sigma\\int_{\\mathbb R}G^2F(0,s)\\,ds\\ge|\\mathcal T(0)|^2.\n\\]\nIf no finite interval and threshold gave \\(I_P\\ge(1-\\epsilon)a^2\\), choose \\(P_j\\to\\infty\\), \\(D_j(z_j)\\to0\\), violating the estimate. The local mass bound prevents \\(a_j^2/D_j\\) from vanishing. Normalized convergence on each fixed interval contradicts the full-line inequality.</p><p>For the derivative estimate, in the normalized limit\n\\[\n\\nabla_\\Sigma H/D(z)\\to\\nabla_{x'}F_s(0)/2.\n\\]\nThe even source contributes zero. On an extreme homogeneous solution,\n\\[\n|\\nabla_{x'}F_s(0)|\\le C|b|F(0),\\qquad |b|\\le\\sqrt{3/2}.\n\\]\nThe average of \\(e^{b\\cdot x'}\\) on \\(B_{S/2}^m\\) is at least one and grows uniformly for \\(|b|\\) bounded away from zero. First treat \\(|b|&lt;\\eta\\), then take \\(S\\) large on its complement. For arbitrarily small \\(\\zeta\\),\n\\[\n\\frac12|\\nabla_{x'}F_s(0)|\n\\le\\zeta\\fint_{B_{S/2}^m}F(x',0)\\,dx'.\n\\]\nPositive mixtures and the nonnegative even source preserve this estimate. Cauchy–Schwarz, normalized smooth convergence, and a strict relaxation give the stated surface estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Pushing a lifted test to physical space — original Lemma 8.3.** Let \\(\\Omega\\subset\\Sigma_{\\rm reg}\\) be open, and let \\( \\Phi(z,s)=z+s\\nu(z). \\) Fix \\(0<\\alpha_0<1/4\\) and an even cutoff …", "statement": "<p>**Pushing a lifted test to physical space — original Lemma 8.3.**</p><p>Let \\(\\Omega\\subset\\Sigma_{\\rm reg}\\) be open, and let\n\\[\n\\Phi(z,s)=z+s\\nu(z).\n\\]\nFix \\(0&lt;\\alpha_0&lt;1/4\\) and an even cutoff\n\\[\n\\beta(z,s)=\\beta_0(sa(z)),\n\\]\nequal to one for \\(|s|a\\le\\alpha_0/2\\) and supported in \\(|s|a&lt;\\alpha_0\\).</p><p>For a compactly supported Lipschitz lifted amplitude, let \\(\\xi_j\\) denote its local inverse branches, and set \\(\\xi=(\\sum_j\\xi_j^2)^{1/2}\\). Then \\(\\xi\\) is an admissible physical test, and its quadratic form is the lifted form minus the nonnegative variance\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\min_{b\\in\\mathbb R^n}\\sum_j|\\nabla\\xi_j-\\xi_jb|^2.\n\\]\nFor the amplitude \\(G(s)\\beta(z,s)\\psi(z)\\), the lifted form is\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Omega\\times\\mathbb R}\n\\left\\{\nG^2J|\\nabla(\\beta\\psi)|^2\n+\\beta^2\\psi^2[-J_sGG'+3(v(\\Phi)^2-g^2)G^2J]\n\\right\\}\\,dS\\,ds,\n\\]\nwhere \\(J=\\det(\\operatorname{Id}+sA)\\), and the metric is the pullback Euclidean metric. The formula also permits a bounded Lipschitz physical factor in \\(\\psi\\), initially with finite normal cap.</p>", "proof": "<p>On the cutoff tube, \\(\\Phi\\) is a local diffeomorphism with \\(J&gt;0\\). A fiber meeting compact lifted support is discrete and compact, hence finite. Locally, only finitely many nonzero inverse branches occur. Their square-sum root is Lipschitz, including at zero, and\n\\[\n\\sum_j|\\nabla\\xi_j|^2-|\\nabla\\xi|^2\n=\\sum_j|\\nabla\\xi_j|^2-\n\\frac{|\\sum_j\\xi_j\\nabla\\xi_j|^2}{\\sum_j\\xi_j^2}\n=\\mathcal V\\ge0\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. Potential terms add exactly. The area formula proves the physical/lifted identity.</p><p>For \\(Gf\\), expand the normal derivative and integrate the cross term \\(2GG'ff_sJ\\) by parts. Since \\(G''=(3g^2-1)G\\), this gives the displayed formula. There are no artificial chart boundaries. Normal caps can be removed when terms are dominated by a polynomial in \\(|s|\\) times \\(G^2\\), as in the applications; at \\(a=0\\), take \\(\\beta=1\\) and use the same limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Exact exponential and weighted integral comparisons — original Proposition 8.5 and Lemmas 8.7–8.8.** Return to the seven-dimensional setting of Proposition 5.", "statement": "<p>**Exact exponential and weighted integral comparisons — original Proposition 8.5 and Lemmas 8.7–8.8.**</p><p>Return to the seven-dimensional setting of Proposition 5. For \\(0&lt;\\delta\\le1/4\\), set\n\\[\nX=\\{z\\in\\Sigma:D(z)\\ge\\delta\\},\\qquad\n\\bar a(z)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&amp;z\\in X,\\\\\na(z),&amp;z\\notin X,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\kappa=\\sqrt2/G_1,\\qquad E=e^{-\\kappa d},\\qquad\nJ_0(z)=\\sup_{y\\in\\Sigma}e^{-\\lambda|y-z|}\\bar a(y)^2.\n\\]\nThere exist \\(\\lambda&gt;0,C&lt;\\infty\\), independent of \\(\\delta\\), such that on \\(\\Sigma\\setminus X\\),\n\\[\nD\\le C(J_0+E),\\qquad E\\le C(J_0+D).\n\\]</p><p>Good-surface area in a unit ball is uniformly bounded independently of \\(\\delta\\), and therefore in a radius-\\(r\\) ball is at most \\(C(1+r)^n\\). At a good zero more than distance \\(2\\) from \\(X\\), there is a fixed graph neighborhood with comparable \\(D\\) and\n\\[\n|\\nabla_\\Sigma A|\\le C\\sqrt D.\n\\]</p><p>For a maximal unit-separated \\(\\mathcal X\\subset X\\), center \\(x_c\\), and\n\\[\n\\chi_R(x)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{1+|x-x_c|^2}}R\\right),\n\\]\nthere are \\(C_D,R_D&lt;\\infty\\), independent of \\(\\delta,x_c\\), such that\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Sigma\\setminus X}(D^2+E^2)\\chi_R^2\\,dS\n\\le C_D\\left[\n\\int_{\\Sigma\\setminus X}(a^4+DE)\\chi_R^2\\,dS\n+\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}\\chi_R(x)^2\n\\right]\n\\]\nfor \\(R\\ge R_D\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.2 proves the pointwise comparison. Multiplying its first inequality by \\(D\\), its second by \\(E\\), and using Young's inequality gives\n\\[\nD^2+E^2\\le C(J_0^2+DE).\n\\]\nUse Lemma 7.3. Since\n\\[\n\\chi_R(z)\\le e^{|z-w|/R}\\chi_R(w),\n\\]\nthe uniform polynomial good-area bound gives, for \\(R\\ge2/\\lambda\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Sigma\\setminus X}e^{-2\\lambda|z-w|}\\chi_R(z)^2\\,dS_z\n\\le C_\\lambda\\chi_R(w)^2.\n\\]\nTonelli estimates the weighted \\(J_0^2\\)-integral. Choose \\(\\mu\\) small enough to absorb the resulting \\(D^2\\)-term. All integrals are finite by polynomial area and net growth and exponential decay of the weight.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Half-space inverse — original Lemma 8.6.** Let \\(p_0>n\\), \\(V_g=3g^2-1\\), and \\( L_g=-\\Delta_y-\\partial_t^2+V_g(t) \\) on \\(\\mathbb R^m\\times(0,\\infty)\\), with zero Dirichlet trace.", "statement": "<p>**Half-space inverse — original Lemma 8.6.**</p><p>Let \\(p_0&gt;n\\), \\(V_g=3g^2-1\\), and\n\\[\nL_g=-\\Delta_y-\\partial_t^2+V_g(t)\n\\]\non \\(\\mathbb R^m\\times(0,\\infty)\\), with zero Dirichlet trace. For some \\(b_0&gt;0\\), uniformly in \\(0\\le b\\le b_0\\), its inverse maps\n\\[\n\\sup_{(y,t)}e^{-b|y|}\n\\|f\\|_{L^{p_0}(B_1(y,t)\\cap\\{t&gt;0\\})}\n\\]\nto the analogous uniformly local \\(W^{2,p_0}\\) norm, and is unique in that weighted class.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the positive bounded \\(v_0\\) of Lemma 5.2. Ground-state substitution for compact zero-trace functions gives\n\\[\n\\int(|\\nabla f|^2+V_gf^2)\n=\\int v_0^2|\\nabla(f/v_0)|^2+\\int v_0^{-1}f^2\n\\ge C^{-1}\\|f\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nThus the energy inverse exists for compact sources. Energy estimates, interior and flat-boundary elliptic estimates, and finite Sobolev bootstrapping give local \\(W^{2,p_0}\\) bounds for a source in one unit block.</p><p>Off that block, \\(Cv_0(t)e^{-c_1r}\\) is a supersolution for small fixed \\(c_1\\), because \\(L_gv_0=1\\) dominates the new derivative terms. Comparison, followed by local elliptic estimates, gives exponential decay. Partition a general source into unit blocks and sum; the weighted sum converges for \\(b&lt;c_1/2\\).</p><p>For uniqueness let \\(R_*(x)=\\sqrt{1+|x|^2}\\). For small \\(c_*&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nL_g(v_0e^{c_*R_*})\n\\ge e^{c_*R_*}\n[1-2c_*\\sup|v_0'|-nc_*\\sup v_0-c_*^2\\sup v_0]\n\\ge\\tfrac12e^{c_*R_*}.\n\\]\nTake \\(b_0\\le\\min(c_1/4,c_*/2)\\). A homogeneous weighted-class solution is bounded by \\(C_ue^{b|y|}\\). On large half-balls, \\(\\eta v_0e^{c_*R_*}\\) dominates both signs on the hemispherical boundary, and the solution has zero flat trace. Divide by \\(v_0\\), apply the maximum principle, and let \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Pointwise comparison at the exact exponential rate — original Proposition 8.5.** With \\(J_0,E\\) as in Proposition 7, \\( D(z)\\le C(J_0(z)+E(z)),\\qquad E(z)\\le C(J_0(z)+D(z)) \\) on \\(\\Sigma\\setminus X\\), with constants …", "statement": "<p>**Pointwise comparison at the exact exponential rate — original Proposition 8.5.**</p><p>With \\(J_0,E\\) as in Proposition 7,\n\\[\nD(z)\\le C(J_0(z)+E(z)),\\qquad\nE(z)\\le C(J_0(z)+D(z))\n\\]\non \\(\\Sigma\\setminus X\\), with constants independent of \\(\\delta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(z=0\\), \\(\\nu(0)=e_n\\), and put \\(B=J_0(0)+E(0)\\). Only \\(B\\to0\\) needs proof. If \\(E\\le J_0\\), the second inequality is immediate. Otherwise bounded reach forces a uniform positive defect by single-profile compactness.</p><p>Set \\(P_0=A_0\\log(1/B)\\), and choose \\(\\lambda\\) after \\(A_0\\) so \\(10\\lambda A_0&lt;1/2\\). Every zero in \\(B_{10P_0}\\) is good and has\n\\[\n|A|^2\\le J_0(0)e^{10\\lambda P_0}\\le B^{1/2}.\n\\]\nTangent-projection continuation gives regular graph disks on the \\(P_0\\)-scale, with affine errors \\(o(1)\\). Nonintersection forces nearby disks to have nearly parallel unoriented normals, hence they form a finite ordered graph system in smaller boxes.</p><p>If the first upper graph has small intercept and shortest distance \\(\\ell\\), nonintersection on the full buffered scale gives\n\\[\n|n'-e_n|\\le C(\\ell+o(1))/P_0,\n\\qquad \\{n'\\cdot x=\\ell\\}\n\\]\nas its tangent plane at the foot. Choose the angular parameter small enough that the shortest chord uses the plateau. If \\(E\\ge J_0\\), every realizing contact lies in this graph system. Base-sheet contacts have cost at least \\(cB^{-1/4}\\gg P_0\\), and therefore\n\\[\nd(0)=G_1\\ell_{\\min}\n\\]\nfor the nearest adjacent graph.</p><p>For a fixed unit \\(n_0\\), define\n\\[\nw=q(1-p\\cdot n_0)\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe diffuse identities give\n\\[\n(-\\Delta+2)w=3qw-q^2D\\ge qw\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe positive kernel\n\\[\n\\mathcal K(x)=\\int_0^\\infty(4\\pi t)^{-n/2}\ne^{-2t-|x|^2/(4t)}\\,dt\n\\]\nis comparable, for \\(|x|\\ge1\\), to\n\\[\n|x|^{-m/2}e^{-\\sqrt2|x|}.\n\\]\nConvolution represents bounded solutions. Take \\(n_0=e_n\\). On the opposite adjacent sheet, \\(qw\\) has a fixed positive lower bound on a tangent disk of radius \\(c\\sqrt{\\ell_{\\min}}\\) and fixed thickness. Distances to the base are \\(\\ell_{\\min}+O(1)\\); its volume cancels the kernel's polynomial factor. Thus\n\\[\nD(0)\\ge w(0)\\ge ce^{-\\sqrt2\\ell_{\\min}}=cE(0).\n\\]</p><p>For the upper bound, write the base as \\(x_n=h(y)\\), extend it by the same cutoff on both sides, and retain\n\\[\n\\|h\\|_{C^2}=o(1),\\qquad\n|\\nabla h|+|\\nabla^2h|\n\\le C\\sqrt{J_0(0)}e^{2\\lambda|y|}.\n\\]\nRadial integration and cutoff differentiation give these bounds with no factor growing in \\(\\log(1/B)\\).</p><p>In coordinates \\(x_n=h(y)\\pm t\\), construct artificial zero-trace solutions \\(v_\\pm^*=g+\\phi_\\pm\\). Their equations are\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_g\\phi_\\pm={}&amp;\n|\\nabla h|^2\\partial_{tt}(g+\\phi_\\pm)\n\\mp2\\nabla h\\cdot\\nabla_y\\partial_t\\phi_\\pm\n\\mp\\Delta h\\,\\partial_t(g+\\phi_\\pm)\\\\\n&amp;-3g\\phi_\\pm^2-\\phi_\\pm^3.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nLemma 7.1 gives a contraction in a small unweighted \\(W_{\\rm uloc}^{2,p_0}\\) ball and then\n\\[\n\\|\\phi_\\pm\\|_{W^{2,p_0}(B_1(y,t)\\cap\\{t&gt;0\\})}\n\\le C\\sqrt{J_0(0)}e^{3\\lambda|y|}.\n\\]\nThe solutions lie in \\((0,1]\\). Adding their equations cancels the terms \\(\\mp\\Delta h\\,g'\\). Every remaining source is \\(O(J_0(0))\\) in weight \\(10\\lambda\\). Hence\n\\[\n|\\partial_t(\\phi_++\\phi_-)(0,0)|\\le CJ_0(0).\n\\]</p><p>It remains to compare an artificial solution with the true chamber without losing the exact exponential rate. In a chamber between two adjacent graphs, let\n\\[\nt_1=x_n,\\qquad t_2=\\ell-n'\\cdot x.\n\\]\nOn its middle region,\n\\[\n1-v,\\ 1-v_+^*\n\\le C(e^{-\\sqrt2t_1}+e^{-\\sqrt2t_2}+e^{-cP_0}).\n\\]\nTo prove this, subtract the \\(o(1)\\) graph errors from the two affine distances and add buffered lateral face distances \\(l_j\\). On a fixed-depth inner polytope, clearing makes the deficits small. The barrier\n\\[\n\\mathcal W=\\sum_j(C_je^{-\\sqrt2l_j}-kC_j^2e^{-2\\sqrt2l_j})\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n(-\\Delta+2)\\mathcal W=6k\\sum_jC_j^2e^{-2\\sqrt2l_j}\n\\ge3\\mathcal W^2.\n\\]\nChoose its boundary values above the deficits and compare.</p><p>The difference \\(u_*=v-v_+^*\\) solves\n\\[\n(-\\Delta+V_*)u_*=0,\\qquad\nV_*=v^2+vv_+^*+(v_+^*)^2-1.\n\\]\nTangent phase balls with radii tending uniformly to infinity give fixed-depth convergence to \\(g(t_1)\\). At increasing depth, explicit phase balls of radius comparable to \\(t_1\\) fit in the buffered chamber. Clearing therefore gives, for fixed \\(\\gamma_1&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nV_*\\ge V_g(t_1)-\\eta(B)e^{-\\gamma_1t_1},\n\\qquad \\eta(B)\\to0.\n\\]</p><p>Choose a fixed \\(\\theta_0&gt;0\\), and define on \\(t\\ge-\\theta_0\\)\n\\[\nY(t)=g'(t)\\left[1+\\int_{-\\theta_0}^t(g'(s))^{-2}\\,ds\\right],\n\\]\n\\[\nk'(t)=-Y(t)^{-2}\\int_{-\\theta_0}^tY(s)^2e^{-\\gamma_1s}\\,ds.\n\\]\nChoose the additive constant so \\(1\\le k\\le C\\), and set\n\\[\n\\varphi=e^{-\\sqrt2t}Yk.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n0&lt;c\\le\\varphi\\le C,\\qquad |\\varphi'|\\le Ce^{-\\gamma_1t},\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\varphi''-2\\sqrt2\\,\\varphi'+(V_g-2)\\varphi\n=e^{-\\sqrt2t}Y(t)e^{-\\gamma_1t}\\ge ce^{-\\gamma_1t}.\n\\]\nThus \\(e^{-\\sqrt2t_2}\\varphi(t_1)\\) is a supersolution when the normal-angle error and \\(B\\) are sufficiently small. Coarse barriers \\(v_0(t_1)e^{-c_4l}\\) handle lateral faces and an artificial top face. Comparison after division by \\(v_0\\), followed by boundary gradient estimates, gives\n\\[\n|\\partial_n(v-v_+^*)(0)|\\le C(e^{-\\sqrt2\\ell}+e^{-cP_0}),\n\\]\nor \\(Ce^{-cP_0}\\) if no small adjacent intercept exists. The same argument holds below.</p><p>Fix constants in the order: profile and inverse estimates; uniform box constants and \\(\\gamma_1\\); angular parameter; \\(A_0\\) large enough that \\(cA_0&gt;1\\); then \\(\\lambda\\) small; finally \\(B\\) small. Thus \\(e^{-cP_0}\\le B\\). Combining the two true/artificial comparisons with cancellation gives\n\\[\n|2\\partial_nv(0)-2g'(0)|\\le CB.\n\\]\nSince \\(D(0)=1-2(\\partial_nv(0))^2\\), this proves \\(D\\le C(J_0+E)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Uniform good-area and supremum-to-integral control — original Lemma 8.7 and the local estimate in Lemma 8.8.** The good-area and local graph assertions in Proposition 7 hold.", "statement": "<p>**Uniform good-area and supremum-to-integral control — original Lemma 8.7 and the local estimate in Lemma 8.8.**</p><p>The good-area and local graph assertions in Proposition 7 hold. Moreover, for sufficiently small fixed \\(\\mu&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nJ_0(z)^2\\le C\\int e^{-2\\lambda|z-w|}\n\\left(C_\\mu a(w)^4\\,dS_w+C\\mu^4D(w)^2\\,dS_w+d\\#\\mathcal X(w)\\right),\n\\]\nwhere surface measures are restricted to \\(\\Sigma\\setminus X\\), and the coefficient multiplying \\(\\mu^4\\) is independent of \\(\\mu,\\delta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At a good zero,\n\\[\n|\\nabla v|\\ge\\sqrt{3/8}.\n\\]\nUniform second derivative bounds give a fixed ball in which the initial normal derivative stays positive. All zeros in a smaller ball form one graph with uniform derivatives. Ambient packing of a separated family of these balls gives the unit-area bound and the polynomial larger-ball bound. Harnack and differentiated diffuse estimates give local defect comparability and \\(|\\nabla_\\Sigma A|\\le C\\sqrt D\\).</p><p>For a good \\(w\\) more than distance \\(2\\) from \\(X\\), and intrinsic distance at most \\(\\mu\\),\n\\[\na(w)^4\\le Ca(w')^4+C\\mu^4D(w)^2.\n\\]\nAverage over the small graph disk; then use a fixed graph disk and Harnack to replace \\(D(w)^2\\) by a fixed-disk integral. This gives\n\\[\na(w)^4\\le C_\\mu\\int_{B_1(w)\\cap(\\Sigma\\setminus X)}a^4\\,dS\n+C\\mu^4\\int_{B_1(w)\\cap(\\Sigma\\setminus X)}D^2\\,dS.\n\\]\nValues within distance \\(2\\) of \\(X\\), including assigned bad values \\(\\bar a=1\\), are charged to a net point within distance \\(3\\). Bounded shifts alter the exponential kernel only by a fixed factor. Taking the supremum gives the claimed convolution estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Clean-window geometry and sharp interaction constants — original Lemma 8.9 and Proposition 8.10.** Fix \\(\\omega>0\\), \\(C_0<\\infty\\), and a fractional margin \\(0<\\theta_0<1\\).", "statement": "<p>**Clean-window geometry and sharp interaction constants — original Lemma 8.9 and Proposition 8.10.**</p><p>Fix \\(\\omega&gt;0\\), \\(C_0&lt;\\infty\\), and a fractional margin \\(0&lt;\\theta_0&lt;1\\). Suppose\n\\[\nD(w)\\le N^{-\\omega}\n\\quad\\text{for }w\\in\\Sigma\\cap B_{N^\\omega}(z_0),\n\\qquad N\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nUniformly for bases \\(z\\in\\Sigma\\cap B_{(1-\\theta_0)N^\\omega}(z_0)\\), all zero pieces within \\(C_0\\log N\\) form finitely many ordered graphs with polynomial buffers, unique smooth projections, curvature at most \\(CN^{-\\omega/2}\\), and common-plane slopes at most\n\\[\nC(N^{-\\omega/8}\\log N+N^{-3\\omega/8}).\n\\]\nDistinct participating graphs have separation at least \\(c_0\\log N\\).</p><p>These descriptions hold on every fixed logarithmic-power lateral disk and fixed multiple of logarithmic normal depth. True versus vertical distances and affine graph-height errors are additive\n\\[\nO(N^{-c}(1+\\log N)^{C_k})=o(1).\n\\]\nThe descriptions agree on overlaps. If a distinct graph is within \\(C_0\\log N\\),\n\\[\nd(z)=G_1\\ell_{\\min}(z),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\ell_{\\min}\\) is the shortest Euclidean distance to an adjacent graph.</p><p>For every \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), choose a sufficiently large fixed \\(P\\). Then\n\\[\nI_P(z)\\ge(32-\\epsilon)e^{-\\sqrt2\\ell_{\\min}(z)}.\n\\]\nIf \\(x\\) is between adjacent nearest sheets, their distances \\(s_i,s_j\\le C_0\\log N\\) satisfy \\(|s_i-s_j|\\le C_1\\), and \\(N\\) is sufficiently large, then\n\\[\nq(x)\\le(4+\\epsilon)\n\\left(e^{-\\sqrt2s_i}+e^{-\\sqrt2s_j}\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemma 8.1. For the interaction bound choose \\(n_0=\\nu(z)\\) and \\(w=q(1-p\\cdot n_0)\\). On a fixed base column,\n\\[\nD=2(1-p\\cdot n_0)-|p-n_0|^2,\\qquad\nqD\\ge(2-o(1))w.\n\\]\nOn the opposite adjacent sheet, take a tangent disk of radius \\(\\ell^{2/3}\\) and fixed depth \\(|t|\\le Q\\). Opposite orientation and uniform profile convergence give\n\\[\n3qw-q^2D\\ge(6-o(1))G(t)^2.\n\\]\nThe kernel in Lemma 7.2 satisfies exactly\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^m}\\mathcal K(y,h)\\,dy\n=\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}e^{-\\sqrt2|h|}.\n\\]\nTruncation to the disk has relative error \\(o(1)\\); additive distance and angle errors are \\(o(1)\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\frac{I_P(z)}{e^{-\\sqrt2\\ell}}\n\\ge\n\\frac{2-o(1)}{\\sigma}\\frac{6-o(1)}{2\\sqrt2}\n\\left(\\int_{-P}^{P}e^{\\sqrt2s}G(s)^2\\,ds\\right)\n\\left(\\int_{-Q}^{Q}e^{\\sqrt2t}G(t)^2\\,dt\\right).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{\\sqrt2t}G(t)^2\\,dt=2\\sigma,\n\\]\nthe limiting coefficient is \\(32\\). Choose \\(P,Q\\), then \\(N\\).</p><p>For the phase tail, choose the sign positive in the gap. At a nearly middle point, both sheet distances diverge. Use the two tangent planes at perpendicular feet and lateral faces at scale \\((\\log N)^2\\); subtract the additive \\(o(1)\\) affine errors. In the polytope barrier from Lemma 7.2, the two principal coefficients can be \\(2+\\eta\\), because on fixed-depth faces profile convergence gives \\(1-v\\to1-g(M)\\), and\n\\[\n(2+\\eta)e^{-\\sqrt2M}\n-k(2+\\eta)^2e^{-2\\sqrt2M}&gt;1-g(M)\n\\]\nfor large fixed \\(M\\). Keep coarse coefficients on lateral faces. Their contribution at \\(x\\) is \\(O(e^{-c(\\log N)^2})\\), negligible relative to the principal tails. Therefore\n\\[\n1-v(x)\\le(2+2\\eta)\n(e^{-\\sqrt2s_i}+e^{-\\sqrt2s_j}).\n\\]\nUse \\(q\\le2(1-v)\\) and choose \\(\\eta\\) in terms of \\(\\epsilon\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Uniform geometry on clean windows — original Lemma 8.9.** Under Proposition 8, all its graph, separation, projection, additive-error, overlap, and exact-reach assertions hold.", "statement": "<p>**Uniform geometry on clean windows — original Lemma 8.9.**</p><p>Under Proposition 8, all its graph, separation, projection, additive-error, overlap, and exact-reach assertions hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\kappa_N=CN^{-\\omega/2}\\), \\(r=N^{\\omega/8}\\). At every relevant zero, curvature is at most \\(\\kappa_N\\) and the gradient is bounded below. Lift radial tangent-plane segments. Along length \\(10r\\), normal variation is at most \\(10\\kappa_Nr=o(1)\\), so projection remains invertible and lift length controlled. A terminal limit is a regular zero and continues the lift. This gives full graph disks and larger fixed buffers, with affine error\n\\[\nC\\kappa_Nr^2=O(N^{-\\omega/4}).\n\\]</p><p>Two such disks whose base points are distance \\(b=o(r)\\) apart have unoriented normal angle at most\n\\[\nC(b/r+\\kappa_Nr).\n\\]\nOtherwise a line in a tilt direction makes signed graph height change sign by more than the affine errors, forcing intersection with incompatible tangent planes. For \\(b=O(\\log N)\\), this gives the common-plane slope bound and ordering.</p><p>A zero in a logarithmic-power box can first be continued to the normal axis with height change \\(o(1)\\). Reapplying the angle estimate there gives the refined slope bound. Thus no new sheet enters through a side unnoticed. Local regularity excludes infinitely many distinct sheets accumulating in a closed inner box.</p><p>On lateral scale \\(L=(\\log N)^k\\), affine and common-plane errors are bounded by\n\\[\nC\\kappa_NL^2+C\\vartheta_NL,\n\\qquad\n\\vartheta_N=C(\\log N/r+\\kappa_Nr),\n\\]\nwhich are polynomial-small times logarithmic powers. For a graph \\(x_n=f(y)\\), half the squared-distance Hessian is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Id}+\\nabla f\\otimes\\nabla f-(x_n-f)D^2f.\n\\]\nIt is positive definite on buffered disks. A minimizing point cannot lie at the polynomially distant boundary, since a vertical competitor is only logarithmically distant. This proves unique smooth projection. The stationary equation gives foot displacement \\(O(\\vartheta_N\\log N)\\) and true/vertical distance difference \\(O(\\vartheta_N^2\\log N)\\).</p><p>For separation, choose \\(r_0=c_0\\log N\\) small enough. Exponential defect comparison gives \\(D\\le CN^{-\\omega/2}\\) in \\(B_{4r_0}\\), hence \\(|T|\\le CN^{-\\omega/4}\\). The initial normal derivative of \\(U\\) remains positive, so all zeros in a smaller cylinder form one graph. This excludes a different sheet within a fixed fraction of \\(r_0\\).</p><p>A shortest chord to a distinct logarithmically close graph is interior and normal to that graph; common normals put its base angle on the penalty plateau. It supplies cost \\(G_1\\ell_{\\min}\\). All better competitors remain in the same logarithmic graph system. A base-sheet chord has cost at least \\(c\\kappa_N^{-1}\\gg\\log N\\), including diagonal limits. Other chords cost at least \\(G_1\\) times their length, and nonadjacent ones cross an intervening zero first. Hence the reach identity is exact.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Fourth-order growth — original Proposition 9.1.** Assume the seven-dimensional setting of Proposition 5.", "statement": "<p>**Fourth-order growth — original Proposition 9.1.**</p><p>Assume the seven-dimensional setting of Proposition 5. Fix the penalty so that\n\\[\n\\eta_0&lt;10^{-4},\\qquad\nG_1-1&lt;10^{-3},\\qquad\n(1-\\delta_2)^{-1}-1&lt;10^{-3},\n\\]\ndecreasing these errors as required by the subsequent radial comparison.</p><p>There are a finite normal depth \\(P\\), a fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), and \\(R_0,C&lt;\\infty\\) such that, for\n\\[\nX=\\{z\\in\\Sigma:D(z)\\ge\\delta\\},\\quad\n\\Sigma_g=\\Sigma\\setminus X,\\quad\nI=I_P,\\quad E=e^{-\\kappa d},\\quad\\kappa=\\sqrt2/G_1,\n\\]\nevery maximal unit-separated \\(\\mathcal X\\subset X\\), every center \\(x_c\\), and every \\(R\\ge R_0\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\mathcal Y_R:=\n\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi_{R,x_c}^2(a^4+D^2+E^2)\\,dS\n+N_R\n\\le CK(R)R^{m-4},\\qquad m=6,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\chi_{R,x_c}(x)=e^{-\\sqrt{1+|x-x_c|^2}/R},\n\\qquad\nN_R=\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}\\chi_{R,x_c}(x)^2.\n\\]\nThe threshold \\(\\delta\\) is fixed before \\(R\\) grows.</p><p>The choices can impose\n\\[\nI\\ge.999a^2,\\qquad c_ID\\le I\\le C_PD,\\qquad |H|\\le.355I.\n\\]\nThey can also impose any prescribed smaller interface and sharp-interaction tolerances.</p><p>For use in the proof, write\n\\[\nS_0=(a^2+I)/2,\\qquad s_X=\\operatorname{dist}(\\,\\cdot\\,,X).\n\\]\nThe following gluing data are fixed before the later reach multiplier: a collar width \\(L_0\\), an interval \\([t_{\\min},t_*]\\) containing collar reach values, and a positive bad-mass coefficient \\(c_0\\). A permitted multiplier \\(f:[t_{\\min},\\infty)\\to(0,1]\\) is nonincreasing, locally Lipschitz, equals one on \\([t_{\\min},t_*]\\), has \\(|f'|\\le C_f^{(0)}f\\), and is a positive multiple of \\(e^{-\\kappa t/2}\\) eventually.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(C_D\\) from Proposition 7 before choosing the small coefficient \\(\\epsilon_2\\) in Lemma 9.1. Then fix interface tolerances, \\(P,t_{\\min}\\), and only afterward \\(\\delta\\). Choose \\(L_0,t_*\\) next and the multiplier last.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nZ_2=\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2(D^2+E^2),\\qquad\nV_R=K(R)R^{m-4}.\n\\]\nProposition 7 and Lemma 9.1, with \\(\\epsilon_2C_D\\) small, give\n\\[\nA_4+Z_2+N_R\n\\le C'\\left(N_R+\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2DE+V_R\\right),\n\\]\nwhere \\(C'\\) is fixed before constructing \\(f\\).</p><p>Since \\(E\\le f^2\\), the good collar area bound gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2DE\n\\le\\int_{\\{s_X\\ge4L_0\\}}\\chi^2Df^2+C_{L_0}N_R.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\epsilon_4\\) in Lemma 9.8 sufficiently small and substitute, absorbing its \\(A_4\\)-term. The remaining cutoff term is controlled by\n\\[\nC_fR^{-2}\\int\\chi^2f^2\n\\le C_f'R^{-2}\\int\\chi^2E\n\\le\\eta Z_2+C_{f,\\eta}R^{-4}\\int\\chi^2\n\\le\\eta Z_2+C_{f,\\eta}V_R.\n\\]\nTake \\(\\eta\\) small and absorb. This proves the fourth-order bound, uniformly under translation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Curvature controlled by a small defect cost — original Lemma 9.2.** For every \\(\\epsilon_2>0\\), the interface tolerances and \\(\\delta\\) can be chosen so that …", "statement": "<p>**Curvature controlled by a small defect cost — original Lemma 9.2.**</p><p>For every \\(\\epsilon_2&gt;0\\), the interface tolerances and \\(\\delta\\) can be chosen so that\n\\[\nA_4:=\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2a^4\\,dS\n\\le\\epsilon_2\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2D^2\\,dS\n+C_{\\epsilon_2,\\delta}N_R\n+C_{\\epsilon_2,\\delta}K(R)R^{m-4}.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\chi=\\chi_{R,x_c}\\), and the estimate is uniform in the center for large \\(R\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For symmetric \\(A\\) with totally symmetric \\(\\nabla A\\), there are dimensional \\(\\theta&gt;0,C\\) such that\n\\[\n|\\nabla A|^2\\ge(1+\\theta)|\\nabla|A||^2-C|\\nabla H|^2.\n\\]\nIndeed equality in ordinary Kato, after normalization, forces \\(\\nabla_iA=b_iA\\). Total symmetry makes \\(A\\) rank one in the \\(b\\)-direction, and then \\(\\nabla H\\ne0\\). Compactness gives a strict gap when \\(\\nabla H\\) is small; otherwise its error pays. The weak gradient of \\(|A|\\) vanishes almost everywhere on its zero set.</p><p>Choose the averaging radius \\(S\\) in Proposition 6. Let \\(m_c\\) vanish within \\(3S\\) of \\(X\\), equal one beyond \\(6S\\), and put \\(u=\\chi m_c\\). Pair\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\Sigma A=\\nabla_\\Sigma^2H+HA^2-a^2A\n\\]\nwith \\(Au^2\\), integrate by parts, and use Codazzi. For a fixed \\(\\theta'&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n(1+\\theta')G\n\\le F+C\\int u^2(|\\nabla H|^2+Da^3)\n+C\\int a^2|\\nabla u|^2,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nG=\\int u^2|\\nabla a|^2,\\qquad F=\\int u^2a^4.\n\\]\nApply strengthened surface stability to \\(au\\), using the sharp curvature comparison:\n\\[\nF\\le(1+\\theta'/2)G+C\\int a^2|\\nabla u|^2.\n\\]\nSubtract to control \\(F,G\\).</p><p>Fubini in the averaged mean-derivative estimate gives\n\\[\n\\int u^2|\\nabla H|^2\\le C\\epsilon\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2D^2.\n\\]\nThe \\(S^{-m}\\) factor cancels the area of the possible centers, and weight ratios are at most two for large \\(R\\). Also \\(a^2\\le CD\\) gives \\(Da^3\\le C\\sqrt\\delta\\,D^2\\).</p><p>The elementary weighted bounds are\n\\[\nK(t)\\le C(t/R)K(R)\\quad(t\\ge R),\\qquad\n\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2\\le CK(R)R^m,\n\\]\nand, for \\(s\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Sigma_g\\cap\\{s_X\\le s\\}}\\chi^2\\,dS\n+\\int_{\\{s_X\\le s\\}}\\chi^2\\,dx\n\\le C(1+s)^ne^{C(1+s)/R}N_R.\n\\]\nThey follow from energy-controlled local good charts, ambient ball coverings, and the maximal bad net. Thus cutoff costs are\n\\[\nC_SN_R+CR^{-2}\\int\\chi^2a^2\n\\le C_SN_R+\\epsilon' A_4+C_{\\epsilon'}R^{-4}\\int\\chi^2.\n\\]\nThe omitted collar part of \\(A_4\\) is also \\(C_SN_R\\). Absorb \\(\\epsilon'A_4\\), then choose \\(\\epsilon,\\delta\\) small. Compact exhaustion and regularization of \\(a\\) justify the tests.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**The gluing test and admissibility — construction in original Lemma 9.3.** Fix \\(0<\\tau\\le.001\\).", "statement": "<p>**The gluing test and admissibility — construction in original Lemma 9.3.**</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\tau\\le.001\\). Choose physical Lipschitz functions\n\\[\n\\theta=\\theta(s_X/L_0),\\quad\n\\theta=0\\text{ on }s_X\\le2L_0,\\quad\n\\theta=\\pi/2\\text{ on }s_X\\ge3L_0,\\quad\n|\\nabla\\theta|\\le C/L_0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha_0=\\cos\\theta,\\qquad b=\\sin\\theta.\n\\]\nOn \\(\\Sigma_g\\), choose \\(\\mu=0\\) for \\(s_X\\le L_0/2\\), \\(\\mu=1\\) for \\(s_X\\ge L_0\\), with \\(|\\nabla\\mu|\\le C/L_0\\), and put \\(p_0=\\mu f(d)\\).</p><p>For the curvature cutoff \\(\\beta\\) of Lemma 6.2, define\n\\[\n\\xi_0=q\\chi\\alpha_0,\\qquad\n\\xi_{z,s}=G(s)\\beta(z,s)\\chi(\\Phi(z,s))b(\\Phi(z,s))p_0(z).\n\\]\nThe square root of the sum of squares over \\(\\xi_0\\) and all nonzero lifted inverse branches is a valid physical stability test after compact and normal exhaustion. Its gradient variance is\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\min_w\\sum_j|\\nabla\\xi_j-\\xi_jw|^2.\n\\]\nAll collar reaches, including source reaches whose receiving feet are in the collar, lie below a fixed \\(t_*\\), so \\(f=1\\) there.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 5 and exponential defect comparison give the collar reach bound\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\Sigma_g\\cap\\{s_X\\le10L_0\\}}d\\le T(\\delta,L_0).\n\\]\nThe same holds for source reaches with a receiving foot in that collar. Choose \\(t_*\\) to cover both.</p><p>With finite normal and physical caps, the nonzero lifted support is compact inside the immersion domain and away from \\(X\\). Fibers are finite, so Lemma 6.2 gives one locally Lipschitz physical square-sum test. The potential terms add exactly and the full variance is subtracted; any subfamily variance is a lower bound.</p><p>Remove physical caps first at fixed normal cap, then normal caps. The estimates\n\\[\n\\frac{\\chi(\\Phi(z,s))}{\\chi(z)}\\le e^{|s|/R},\n\\]\nthe subpolynomial reach-gradient bound, \\(K(d)\\le1+Cd\\), the eventual exponential tail of \\(f\\), polynomial good-area growth, and polynomial normal factors times \\(G^2\\) dominate all terms. For the subtracted variance retain fixed short-segment lower bounds and use Fatou. These convergence bounds may depend on \\(f\\), but quantitative coefficients below do not.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Positive mass reserve — original Claim 1 in Lemma 9.3.** Use the test of Lemma 9.2, and set \\( S_{\\rm sw}=\\Sigma_g\\cap\\{3L_0/2\\le s_X\\le4L_0\\}, \\qquad D_{\\rm sw}=\\int_{S_{\\rm sw}}\\chi^2D\\,dS. \\) The favorable terms …", "statement": "<p>**Positive mass reserve — original Claim 1 in Lemma 9.3.**</p><p>Use the test of Lemma 9.2, and set\n\\[\nS_{\\rm sw}=\\Sigma_g\\cap\\{3L_0/2\\le s_X\\le4L_0\\},\n\\qquad\nD_{\\rm sw}=\\int_{S_{\\rm sw}}\\chi^2D\\,dS.\n\\]\nThe favorable terms reserve positive fixed multiples of \\(N_R\\) and \\(D_{\\rm sw}\\), independent of \\(f\\), while retaining the desired exterior surface mass\n\\[\n(1-\\tau/2)\\sigma\\int_{\\Sigma_g\\cap\\{s_X\\ge4L_0\\}}S_0f^2\\chi^2\\,dS,\n\\]\nup to arbitrarily small interface losses, \\(CL_0^{-1}D_{\\rm sw}\\), \\(o_R(1)D_{\\rm sw}\\), and \\(o_{L_0}(1)N_R\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Disjoint fixed small balls about the unit-separated bad net give \\(c_\\delta N_R\\) from\n\\[\n\\int q^3D\\chi^2\\alpha_0^2,\n\\]\nusing \\(q=1,D\\ge\\delta\\) at the centers and uniform local estimates.</p><p>A fixed small tube over \\(S_{\\rm sw}\\) is injective. The ambient term supplies\n\\[\nc\\int_{S_{\\rm sw}}\\chi^2\\alpha_0^2D\n-CL_0^{-1}D_{\\rm sw}-o_R(1)D_{\\rm sw}.\n\\]\nThe lifted Jacobian and potential terms supply the complementary \\(b^2D\\) mass and exterior \\(S_0f^2\\) mass. Freezing \\(b,\\chi\\) costs the displayed errors because\n\\[\n|\\partial_sJ|\\le C(1+|s|)^nD(z).\n\\]\nOutside the switch region a change of \\(b\\) requires a long normal ray; collar bounds and exponential profile tails charge it to \\(o_{L_0}(1)N_R\\). Curvature-cutoff errors are absorbed by \\(If^2\\).</p><p>Choose\n\\[\n0&lt;c_{\\rm sw}\\le\\min\\{c_{\\rm amb}/4,\\tau\\sigma c_I/32\\}.\n\\]\nOn \\(s_X&lt;4L_0\\), complementary gains give \\(2c_{\\rm sw}(\\alpha_0^2+b^2)D\\). On the level \\(s_X=4L_0\\), retain the exterior strength and use its spare sheet gain to pay \\(2c_{\\rm sw}D\\). Thus the argument also covers a positive-area level set, and leaves a positive switch reserve independent of \\(f\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Derivative costs apart from the signed partition terms — original Claim 2 in Lemma 9.3.** For the test of Lemma 9.2, all derivative costs other than …", "statement": "<p>**Derivative costs apart from the signed partition terms — original Claim 2 in Lemma 9.3.**</p><p>For the test of Lemma 9.2, all derivative costs other than\n\\[\n\\chi^2q^2|\\nabla\\alpha_0|^2\n+2\\chi q^2\\alpha_0\\nabla\\chi\\cdot\\nabla\\alpha_0\n\\]\nand the lifted density\n\\[\nG^2Jp_0^2\\beta^2\n\\left(\\chi^2|\\nabla b|^2+2\\chi b\\nabla\\chi\\cdot\\nabla b\\right)\n\\]\nare bounded by\n\\[\n(1+\\tau')\\sigma\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2f'(d)^2|\\nabla d|^2\n+C_{\\tau'}R^{-2}\\left(\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2f^2+C_{L_0}N_R\\right)\n+o_{L_0}(1)(D_{\\rm sw}+N_R),\n\\]\nplus arbitrarily small favorable-mass fractions. The constants do not depend on the permitted \\(f\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(B_s=(\\operatorname{Id}+sA)^{-1}\\). The physical vectors differentiating \\(\\chi b\\beta\\mu f\\) are\n\\[\nV_f=\\chi b\\beta\\mu f'B_s\\nabla d,\\quad\nV_\\mu=\\chi b\\beta fB_s\\nabla\\mu,\\quad\nV_\\beta=\\chi b\\mu f\\nabla\\beta,\n\\]\n\\[\nV_\\chi=b\\beta\\mu f\\nabla\\chi,\\qquad\nV_b=\\chi\\beta\\mu f\\nabla b.\n\\]\nKeep \\(|V_b|^2+2V_b\\cdot V_\\chi\\) with its sign. Bound other squares and crosses by\n\\[\n(1+C\\epsilon)|V_f|^2+\nC_\\epsilon(|V_\\mu|^2+|V_\\beta|^2+|V_\\chi|^2)\n+2V_b\\cdot(V_f+V_\\mu+V_\\beta).\n\\]\nAll lifted integrals include \\(G^2J\\).</p><p>The metric and normal integral control \\(V_f\\) with coefficient \\(\\sigma(1+o(1))\\); \\(V_\\chi\\) costs \\(R^{-2}\\). A nonzero \\(V_\\mu\\) requires \\(|s|\\ge L_0\\), so it is a long tail. Since\n\\[\n\\nabla\\beta=\\beta_0'(sa)(a\\nu+sB_s\\nabla a),\n\\]\nits normal integral is \\(O(e^{-c\\alpha_0/a})\\); freeze \\(b\\), absorb the frozen part by \\(I\\), and charge the remaining switch or long-tail errors to the reserved masses.</p><p>If \\(V_b\\cdot V_f\\ne0\\), then \\(f'\\ne0\\) forces the base outside the \\(10L_0\\)-collar while the image is in the physical switch, so \\(|s|&gt;7L_0\\). Young's inequality assigns every \\(f'\\)-term to its displayed exact derivative energy, leaving only a tail with \\(f\\le1\\). The \\(V_bV_\\mu\\) cross is also a tail; \\(V_bV_\\beta\\) costs \\(CL_0^{-1}D_{\\rm sw}\\) on the switch and a long tail elsewhere. This accounts for every derivative without using a quantitative slope bound for \\(f\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Cancellation of partition costs — original Claim 3 in Lemma 9.3.** For the two signed densities in Lemma 9.4, \\( \\int(\\text{both partition densities})-\\int\\mathcal V \\le o_{L_0}(1)(D_{\\rm sw}+N_R), \\) uniformly for …", "statement": "<p>**Cancellation of partition costs — original Claim 3 in Lemma 9.3.**</p><p>For the two signed densities in Lemma 9.4,\n\\[\n\\int(\\text{both partition densities})-\\int\\mathcal V\n\\le o_{L_0}(1)(D_{\\rm sw}+N_R),\n\\]\nuniformly for sufficiently large \\(R\\) and for every permitted \\(f\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On the physical switch \\(2L_0&lt;s_X&lt;3L_0\\), set\n\\[\nM=C_1\\log L_0.\n\\]\nTemporarily fix small \\(\\xi&gt;0\\). Call a preimage of depth \\(|s|\\le M\\) accurate if\n\\[\nB_z(s):=D(z)e^{(2\\sqrt2+\\xi)|s|}\\le b_\\xi.\n\\]\nIts base lies in \\(S_{\\rm sw}\\), where \\(p_0=1\\). Along an accurate ray, the diffuse estimates and \\(Tp=-\\nabla D/2\\) give\n\\[\n|p(x)-\\nu(z)|+|U(x)-s|\n\\le C_\\xi B_z(s)e^{-\\xi|s|/2}.\n\\]\nAlso \\(\\beta=1\\) for small \\(b_\\xi\\).</p><p>There is at most one accurate preimage. Two such preimages first have depths close to \\(U(x)\\), then normals close to \\(p(x)\\), and therefore bases within \\(C_\\xi b_\\xi\\). In their common fixed graph, tangential projection of the normal-image equality gives\n\\[\n.9|z-\\tilde z|\n\\le C|\\tilde s|\\sqrt{D(z)}\\,|z-\\tilde z|,\n\\]\nwhose coefficient is smaller than \\(.9\\). Thus they coincide.</p><p>On the accurate branch,\n\\[\n|q/G-1|+|\\nabla\\log(G/q)|\\le C_\\xi B_z.\n\\]\nRetaining only it and the ambient branch gives variance at least\n\\[\n\\chi^2\\frac{q^2G^2}{\\alpha_0^2q^2+b^2G^2}\n|\\nabla\\theta+\\alpha_0b\\nabla\\log(G/q)|^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(A=q^2,B=G^2\\),\n\\[\nAb^2+B\\alpha_0^2-\\frac{AB}{A\\alpha_0^2+Bb^2}\n=\\frac{\\alpha_0^2b^2(A-B)^2}{A\\alpha_0^2+Bb^2}.\n\\]\nExpanding and keeping signed \\(\\nabla\\chi\\)-crosses bounds the residual by\n\\[\nC_\\xi\\chi^2G^2B_z(L_0^{-1}+L_0^{-2}+(L_0R)^{-1}).\n\\]\nThere is no multiplicity factor because the accurate branch is unique.</p><p>For inaccurate lifted rays of depth at most \\(M\\),\n\\[\nG(s)^2\\le C_\\xi D(z)e^{\\xi M}.\n\\]\nIf an ambient point has no accurate preimage, select a nearest zero. At distance at most \\(M\\), it lies in \\(S_{\\rm sw}\\), its normal ray is inaccurate, and phase decay gives\n\\[\nq(x)^2\\le C_\\xi D(z)e^{2\\xi M}.\n\\]\nThe full normal-map area formula, with Jacobian bounded by \\(C(1+M)^m\\), controls this integral without assuming unique closest points. Critical images have zero volume. More distant ambient points and lifted rays of depth greater than \\(M\\) are charged by a fixed coarse exponential decay to the bad net.</p><p>For fixed dimensional \\(N_0,c&gt;0\\), all residual costs are bounded by\n\\[\nC_\\xi\\frac{(1+M)^{N_0}e^{2\\xi M}}{L_0}D_{\\rm sw}\n+C_\\xi(1+L_0+M)^{N_0}e^{-cM}N_R.\n\\]\nChoose \\(C_1&gt;(N_0+3)/c\\), then \\(\\xi\\) with \\(2\\xi C_1&lt;1/4\\), then \\(b_\\xi\\), then \\(L_0\\) large. Both coefficients tend to zero and are absorbed by the already fixed reserves.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Uniform gluing inequality — original Lemma 9.3.** For the fixed choices in Proposition 9 and every permitted \\(f\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Uniform gluing inequality — original Lemma 9.3.**</p><p>For the fixed choices in Proposition 9 and every permitted \\(f\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;(1-\\tau)\\int_{\\Sigma_g\\cap\\{s_X\\ge4L_0\\}}S_0f(d)^2\\chi^2\\,dS+c_0N_R\\\\\n&amp;\\hspace{1cm}\\le\n(1+\\tau)\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2f'(d)^2|\\nabla_\\Sigma d|^2\\,dS\n+\\frac C{R^2}\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2f(d)^2\\,dS.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe constants \\(L_0,t_*,c_0,R_1,C\\) are independent of \\(f\\); its tail and finite logarithmic slope are used only for exhaustion.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply stability to Lemma 9.2's square-sum test. Lemma 9.3 reserves the exterior sheet mass and positive switch and bad-net masses. Lemma 9.4 estimates all derivative terms except the signed partition costs. Lemma 9.5 cancels those costs with the variance. Absorb the remaining \\(C_{L_0}R^{-2}N_R\\) for large \\(R\\), divide by \\(\\sigma\\), and choose the interface and Young tolerances with room inside \\(\\tau\\). If \\(X=\\varnothing\\), strengthened surface stability applied to \\(\\chi f(d)\\) gives the same conclusion directly.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Weight and reach multiplier — original Lemma 9.4.** Let \\(\\zeta_0=.01\\), \\(k=1.53\\).", "statement": "<p>**Weight and reach multiplier — original Lemma 9.4.**</p><p>Let \\(\\zeta_0=.01\\), \\(k=1.53\\). Choose \\(t_{\\min}\\) so\n\\[\nF_0:=\\exp(C/(\\gamma t_{\\min}^{\\gamma}))&lt;1.001.\n\\]\nFor every \\(t_*\\ge t_{\\min}\\), \\(\\epsilon_3&gt;0\\), and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), there are \\(t_0&gt;t_*\\) and a permitted \\(f\\) such that, defining\n\\[\nB_*(t)=1+Ct^{-\\gamma}+\\zeta_0\\mathbf1_{\\{t\\ge t_0\\}},\n\\]\n\\[\np_*(t)=\\exp\\left(\\int_{t_{\\min}}^t\\frac{B_*(s)}s\\,ds\\right),\n\\qquad\nP_*(t)=\\frac1{p_*(t)}\\int_t^\\infty p_*(s)f'(s)^2\\,ds,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n-P_*'=f'^2+B_*P_*/t,\n\\]\n\\[\ntP_*\\le.79f^2\\quad(t&lt;t_0),\n\\]\n\\[\nP_*\\le(\\kappa/4+.001)f^2,\\qquad t^3f^2\\le\\epsilon_3\n\\quad(t\\ge t_0),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sup_{[t_{\\min},t_*]}P_*\\le\\eta,\n\\qquad\ne^{-\\kappa t}\\le f^2\\le C_fe^{-\\kappa t},\n\\qquad\n\\Psi(t):=\\int_t^\\infty P_*(s)\\,ds\\le C_ff(t)^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(t_s\\ge t_*\\), small \\(0&lt;\\mu\\ll1\\), and \\(t_s&lt;t_p&lt;t_0&lt;t_e\\), and use\n\\[\nf(t)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&amp;t\\le t_s,\\\\\n(t/t_s)^{-\\mu},&amp;t_s&lt;t&lt;t_p,\\\\\nf(t_p)(t/t_p)^{-k},&amp;t_p&lt;t&lt;t_e,\\\\\nf(t_e)e^{-\\kappa(t-t_e)/2},&amp;t\\ge t_e.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nCorners are permitted. For \\(s\\ge t\\),\n\\[\np_*(s)/p_*(t)\\le F_0(s/t)^{1+\\zeta_0}.\n\\]\nOn the main power bridge its contribution to \\(P_*/f^2\\) is at most\n\\[\nF_0A/t,\\qquad A=\\frac{k^2}{2k-\\zeta_0},\\quad F_0A&lt;.77.\n\\]\nThe exponential terminal value satisfies\n\\[\nQ_e:=P_*(t_e)/f(t_e)^2\n\\le\\frac{\\kappa^2/4}{\\kappa-(1+\\zeta_0+Ct_e^{-\\gamma})/t_e}\n=\\kappa/4+o(1).\n\\]\nIts backward contribution is at most\n\\[\nF_0Q_e(t_e/t)^{1+\\zeta_0-2k},\n\\]\nwhich tends uniformly to zero on fixed earlier ranges. Taking \\(t_0\\), then \\(t_e\\), large gives the stated early and late multiplier bounds.</p><p>On the slow bridge, \\(tP_*/f^2\\) is bounded, up to \\(F_0\\), by\n\\[\n\\frac\\mu2(1-(t/t_p)^{2\\mu})\n+(t/t_p)^{2\\mu}\\frac{t_pP_*(t_p)}{f(t_p)^2}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\mu\\) small, then \\(t_p/t_s\\) large, to make compact plateau flux arbitrarily small while preserving the \\(.79\\) bound.</p><p>Since \\(2k&gt;3\\), the quantity \\(t^3f^2\\) decreases on the main bridge; choose \\(t_0\\) to make it below \\(\\epsilon_3\\), then \\(t_e&gt;3/\\kappa\\). Also \\(-f'/f\\le\\kappa/2\\) before the tail, so \\(f^2e^{\\kappa t}\\) is nondecreasing and eventually constant. The primitive bound follows on the tail by integration and on the remaining compact range by positivity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.8", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Integrated reach estimate and numerical reserve — original Section 9.4.** Choose \\(f,P_*\\) from Lemma 9.7.", "statement": "<p>**Integrated reach estimate and numerical reserve — original Section 9.4.**</p><p>Choose \\(f,P_*\\) from Lemma 9.7. For any prescribed \\(\\epsilon_4&gt;0\\), their parameters can be fixed so that\n\\[\nc_4\\left[\nN_R+\\int_{\\{s_X\\ge4L_0\\}}\\chi^2Df^2\\,dS\n\\right]\n\\le\n\\epsilon_4A_4+C_fR^{-2}\\int_{\\Sigma_g}\\chi^2f^2\\,dS\n+C_fK(R)R^{m-4},\n\\]\nwhere \\(c_4&gt;0\\) is independent of \\(f\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(m_0\\) vanish for \\(s_X\\le5L_0\\) and equal one for \\(s_X\\ge6L_0\\). Multiply the weak reach inequality by \\(P_*(d)m_0\\chi^2\\). If its source is \\(A_{\\rm src}\\) and drift \\(B\\), integration by parts gives\n\\[\n\\int m_0\\chi^2f'^2|\\nabla d|^2\n+\\int m_0\\chi^2(B_*-B)P_*\\frac{|\\nabla d|^2}{d}\n\\le\n\\int A_{\\rm src}P_*m_0\\chi^2\n+\\int P_*\\nabla d\\cdot\\nabla(m_0\\chi^2).\n\\]\nThe singular Laplacian is favorable. Since \\(f'=0\\) on the collar, the first term is the full derivative energy of Lemma 9.6.</p><p>For nonplateau branches with \\(d&lt;t_0\\), the local curvature source costs at most \\(.80f^2a^2\\). For \\(d\\ge t_0\\), small \\(ad\\) allows the extra \\(\\zeta_0\\)-drift to absorb it using \\(|\\nabla d|\\ge c_\\Gamma\\); otherwise\n\\[\nCf^2a^2d\\le C\\epsilon_3a^2d^{-2}\\le C\\epsilon_3a^4.\n\\]\nThe outgoing plateau source satisfies\n\\[\nG_1P_*|H|\\le.13f^2I.\n\\]</p><p>The \\(\\nabla m_0\\) term costs\n\\[\nC_{\\delta,L_0}\\sup_{[t_{\\min},t_*]}P_*\\,N_R.\n\\]\nFor the weight derivative use \\(\\nabla\\Psi(d)=-P_*\\nabla d\\), then integrate once more. Convexity of \\(\\sqrt{1+|x-x_c|^2}\\) yields\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\Sigma\\chi^2\\le CR^{-2}\\chi^2+CR^{-1}D\\chi^2.\n\\]\nThus cutoff costs are bounded by a compact-flux bad-net term,\n\\[\nC_fR^{-2}\\int\\chi^2f^2\n+C_fR^{-1}\\int_{\\{s_X\\ge4L_0\\}}\\chi^2Df^2\n+C_{\\delta,L_0,f}R^{-1}N_R.\n\\]</p><p>For incoming positive charges, discard \\(d(z)&gt;\\sqrt R\\): the exponential multiplier tail and polynomial sources give \\(o(K(R)R^{m-4})\\). Otherwise\n\\[\n\\chi(z)^2/\\chi(y)^2=1+o(1).\n\\]\nA receiving foot in the fixed collar has source reach at most \\(t_*\\), so compact flux pays its cost. Outside the collar, use Proposition 5's inverse Jacobian and multiplicity one. For \\(d(z)&lt;t_0\\), choosing \\(t_{\\min}\\) large gives cost at most \\(.14\\chi(y)^2f(d(y))^2I(y)\\). For \\(d(z)\\ge t_0\\) and small \\(a(y)d(z)\\), the Jacobian is below \\(1.05\\); the limiting coefficient is \\(1.05/8=.13125&lt;.14\\). Otherwise the charge is at most \\(C\\epsilon_3\\chi(y)^2a(y)^4\\).</p><p>At each point the selected local branch is either nonplateau or plateau, and a positive incoming charge is counted at most once. The combined costs, after factoring \\(\\chi^2f^2\\), are respectively\n\\[\n.80a^2+.14I,\\qquad .27I.\n\\]\nUsing \\(I\\ge.999a^2\\) and \\(\\tau\\le.001\\),\n\\[\n(1-\\tau)\\frac{a^2+I}{2}\n-(1+\\tau)(.80a^2+.14I)\\ge.05I,\n\\]\n\\[\n(1-\\tau)\\frac{a^2+I}{2}-(1+\\tau).27I\\ge.22I.\n\\]\nCombine with Lemma 9.6. Make compact flux small enough to absorb all fixed collar costs, then fix \\(f\\) and take \\(R\\) large to absorb the \\(R^{-1}\\)-terms. Finally choose \\(\\epsilon_3\\) to obtain the prescribed \\(\\epsilon_4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Automatic bounded density — original Section 10.** Let \\(v:\\mathbb R^7\\to(-1,1)\\) be an entire stable solution with \\(D>0\\).", "statement": "<p>**Automatic bounded density — original Section 10.**</p><p>Let \\(v:\\mathbb R^7\\to(-1,1)\\) be an entire stable solution with \\(D&gt;0\\). Then \\(M_\\infty&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p>For the contradiction argument assume \\(M_\\infty=\\infty\\), retain the fixed data of Propositions 5–9, and write \\(m=6\\). For every large \\(R\\), choose and translate a center so\n\\[\nM_R(0)\\ge K(R)/2.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\n\\rho=(R^2+|x|^2)^{1/2},\\quad\n\\tau=\\log\\rho,\\quad h=\\rho^{-2},\\quad\\varphi=h^2=\\rho^{-4},\n\\quad L=\\frac R{\\sqrt{K(R)}}.\n\\]\nThus \\(L\\ge c\\sqrt R\\) and\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\rho\\ge R}\\frac{L+\\log\\rho}{\\rho}\\to0.\n\\]</p><p>Fix\n\\[\n\\vartheta=.02,\\quad c=.87,\\quad q_*=2.2,\\quad\n\\alpha=.04,\\quad\\beta_0=.01.\n\\]\nChoose smooth nondecreasing \\(f_*:\\mathbb R\\to(0,1]\\) with\n\\[\n0\\le(\\log f_*)'\\le q_*,\\qquad\nf_*=1\\text{ on }[1,\\infty),\\qquad\nf_*(s)=C_*e^{q_*s}\\text{ on }(-\\infty,-1].\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nb(t)=(t^{1-\\vartheta}-L^{1-\\vartheta})_+^{1/(1-\\vartheta)},\n\\]\n\\[\nW_*(\\tau,t)=t^{-\\vartheta}f_*(c\\tau-b(t)),\\qquad\n\\mathcal F(\\tau,t)=\\int_t^\\infty W_*(\\tau,u)\\,du.\n\\]\nAt \\(t=d(z)\\), let \\(\\lambda=(\\log f_*)'(c\\tau-b(d))\\).</p><p>Define\n\\[\nZ=\\{z\\in\\Sigma:D(z)\\ge\\rho(z)^{-\\alpha}\\}.\n\\]\nChoose a smooth ambient \\(m_H\\) that is zero when\n\\(\\operatorname{dist}(x,Z)&lt;\\rho(x)^{\\beta_0}\\), one when that distance exceeds \\(4\\rho(x)^{\\beta_0}\\), and has uniformly bounded first two derivatives. Let \\(m_H=1\\) if \\(Z=\\varnothing\\). Let \\(m_B\\) equal one on \\(B_B\\), vanish outside \\(B_{2B}\\), with derivative bounds \\(C_kB^{-k}\\), and set\n\\[\nm_c=m_Hm_B,\\quad V=h\\sqrt{W_*},\\quad\\psi=m_cV,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\int_\\Sigma I_P\\psi^2\\,dS,\\qquad\nT_r=1-|\\nabla_\\Sigma\\rho|^2\\ge R^2/\\rho^2,\\qquad\n\\mathcal A=K(R)L^{1-\\vartheta}.\n\\]\nEvery supported surface expression is extended by zero through the rough collar.</p><p>An error is called negligible if it is bounded by\n\\[\ne(R)\\mathcal A+e_R(B),\n\\qquad e(R)\\to0,\\quad e_R(B)\\to0\\ \\text{as }B\\to\\infty\n\\]\nat fixed \\(R\\). The order of limits is always \\(B\\to\\infty\\), then \\(R\\to\\infty\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume unbounded density and apply the lemmas. Supported reaches diverge uniformly by Proposition 5, since \\(D&lt;\\rho^{-\\alpha}\\) there.</p><p>The exact radial-factor identity is\n\\[\n\\int|\\nabla(hm_c\\sqrt{W_*})|^2\n=\\int\\varphi|\\nabla(m_c\\sqrt{W_*})|^2\n-\\int h\\Delta_\\Sigma h\\,m_c^2W_*,\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n-\\Delta_\\Sigma h/h=(4+8T_r-2Hz\\cdot\\nu)/\\rho^2.\n\\]\nThe \\(H\\)-term is \\(o_R(1)\\mathcal L\\); cutoff derivatives are negligible. Also\n\\[\n\\nabla\\log W_*=\\lambda\\nabla(c\\tau-b(d))-\\frac{\\vartheta}{d}\\nabla d.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\frac{.73(1.005)}{.895}&lt;.820,\n\\]\nLemma 10.6, allowing the stated small losses, bounds \\(.73\\mathcal L\\) by\n\\[\n.835\\int\\varphi m_c^2W_*\n\\left[\n\\frac{4+8T_r}{\\rho^2}\n+\\frac{1.03}{4}\n\\left|\\lambda\\nabla(c\\tau-b(d))-\\frac{\\vartheta}{d}\\nabla d\\right|^2\n\\right]\n\\]\nplus negligible errors.</p><p>Compare with Lemma 10.3. Use\n\\[\n|a-b|^2\\le1.5|a|^2+3|b|^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;\\lambda\\le2.2\\), \\(0&lt;b'\\le1\\),\n\\[\n.835\\frac{1.03}{4}1.5\\lambda^2\n\\le.70955\\lambda&lt;.72\\lambda/b'.\n\\]\nThe other gradient coefficient is smaller than \\(.012/d\\) for uniformly large supported \\(d\\). The constant radial coefficient satisfies\n\\[\n.835\\cdot4=3.34&lt;3.44.\n\\]\nFinally \\(\\mathcal F/W_*\\ge1/(2.2+o_R(1))\\), so\n\\[\n24\\mathcal F/W_*-.835\\cdot8\n\\ge5\\mathcal F/W_*.\n\\]\nSubtracting gives\n\\[\n\\int_\\Sigma\\varphi m_c^2\\mathcal F\\,\\frac{T_r}{\\rho^2}\\,dS\n\\le o_R(\\mathcal A)+o_{B\\to\\infty;R}(1).\n\\]</p><p>Take \\(B&gt;3R\\). On Lemma 10.7's set, \\(m_c=1\\), \\(\\rho\\le\\sqrt5R\\), \\(T_r\\ge1/5\\), and \\(d\\le L/2\\). Since \\(W_*=t^{-\\vartheta}\\) for \\(t\\le L\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal F(\\tau,d)\n\\ge\\int_{L/2}^{L}t^{-\\vartheta}\\,dt\n=\\frac{1-2^{-(1-\\vartheta)}}{1-\\vartheta}L^{1-\\vartheta}.\n\\]\nAlso \\(\\varphi T_r/\\rho^2\\ge cR^{-m}\\). The left side is therefore at least\n\\[\ncK(R)L^{1-\\vartheta}=c\\mathcal A.\n\\]\nFirst let \\(B\\to\\infty\\) at fixed \\(R\\), then \\(R\\to\\infty\\), a contradiction. Thus density is bounded.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Weight identities — original Lemma 10.1.** In the setting of Proposition 10, uniformly for \\(\\tau\\ge\\log R\\), \\(t\\ge t_{\\min}>0\\), \\( \\mathcal F_\\tau=(1+o_R(1))cW_* \\left(\\frac t{c\\tau\\vee b(t)}\\right)^\\vartheta, \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Weight identities — original Lemma 10.1.**</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 10, uniformly for \\(\\tau\\ge\\log R\\), \\(t\\ge t_{\\min}&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_\\tau=(1+o_R(1))cW_*\n\\left(\\frac t{c\\tau\\vee b(t)}\\right)^\\vartheta,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathcal F_\\tau+\\mathcal F}{W_*}\\le C(L+\\tau),\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\mathcal F}{W_*}\\ge\\frac1{q_*+\\vartheta/t_{\\min}},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal F\\le C(L+\\tau)^{1-\\vartheta}.\n\\]\nOn the regular zero surface,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_\\Sigma\\mathcal F\\ge{}&amp;\n\\mathcal F_\\tau\\Delta_\\Sigma\\tau\n-c^2\\vartheta J_1|\\nabla_\\Sigma\\tau|^2\n-W_*(\\Delta_\\Sigma d)_{\\rm upper}\\\\\n&amp;+\\frac{\\vartheta W_*}{d}|\\nabla_\\Sigma d|^2\n+\\frac{W_*\\lambda}{b'}|\\nabla_\\Sigma(c\\tau-b(d))|^2,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nJ_1=\\int_{b(d)}^\\infty u^{-\\vartheta-1}f_*'(c\\tau-u)\\,du\n\\le C\\mathcal F_\\tau/\\tau.\n\\]\nThe last square is zero where \\(b'=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(t&gt;L\\),\n\\[\nb'=(b/t)^\\vartheta,\\qquad t^{-\\vartheta}\\,dt=b^{-\\vartheta}\\,db.\n\\]\nSince \\(c\\tau&gt;1\\), the part \\(t\\le L\\) contributes nothing to \\(\\mathcal F_\\tau\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_\\tau\n=c\\int_{b(t)}^\\infty u^{-\\vartheta}f_*'(c\\tau-u)\\,du.\n\\]\nThis measure has total mass \\(f_*(c\\tau-b(t))\\), concentrated within \\(O(1)\\) of \\(c\\tau\\vee b(t)\\), with exponential right tail. This proves the first identity and the \\(J_1\\)-bound.</p><p>The logarithmic slopes satisfy\n\\[\n0\\le-\\partial_t\\log W_*=\\vartheta/t+\\lambda b'\n\\le q_*+\\vartheta/t_{\\min},\n\\qquad\n0\\le\\partial_\\tau\\log W_*\\le cq_*.\n\\]\nIntegrating the resulting lower exponential comparison gives the lower ratio. Splitting integrals at \\(C(L+\\tau)\\), and using the exponential tail, gives the upper bounds. If \\(t&gt;C(L+\\tau)\\), also \\(\\mathcal F/W_*\\le C\\).</p><p>The relevant derivatives are\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_t=-W_*,\n\\quad \\mathcal F_{tt}=W_*(\\vartheta/t+\\lambda b'),\n\\quad \\mathcal F_{\\tau t}=-cW_*\\lambda,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{\\tau\\tau}\n=c^2[b^{-\\vartheta}f_*'(c\\tau-b)-\\vartheta J_1].\n\\]\nTheir quadratic form completes to the displayed square. Near \\(t=L\\), \\(f_*'=0\\), so there is no singularity. The singular Laplacian of \\(d\\) is nonpositive, and multiplication by \\(\\mathcal F_t&lt;0\\) reverses its sign, proving the distributional inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Summable errors — original Lemma 10.2.** Let \\(s_j=2^jR\\).", "statement": "<p>**Summable errors — original Lemma 10.2.**</p><p>Let \\(s_j=2^jR\\). On a shell \\(\\rho\\asymp s\\), good area and ambient volume within \\(Cs^{\\beta_0}\\) of \\(Z\\) are bounded by\n\\[\nCK(s)s^{m-4+2\\alpha+n\\beta_0}.\n\\]\nThe dyadic sums of\n\\[\nCK(s)s^{-2+2\\alpha+n\\beta_0}(1+\\log s)^C(L+\\log s)^{1-\\vartheta}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nCK(s)e^{C\\sqrt{\\log(K(s)+1)}}s^{-2}(L+\\log s)^{3-\\vartheta}\n\\]\nare negligible. For some \\(\\epsilon'&gt;0\\), at fixed \\(R,L\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{\\{\\rho\\asymp s\\}\\cap(\\Sigma\\setminus X)}W_*\\,dS\n\\le C_Rs^{m-\\epsilon'}.\n\\]\nAll rough-collar derivative errors, outer-cutoff errors, and the large-reach tails used below are negligible.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take a separated net on \\(Z\\). Away from the fixed bad set \\(X\\), each net center has a fixed regular disk with \\(D\\ge cs^{-\\alpha}\\), contributing at least \\(cs^{-2\\alpha}\\) to \\(\\int D^2\\). Proposition 9 bounds the count by \\(CK(s)s^{m-4+2\\alpha}\\); centers near \\(X\\) obey the stronger bad-net bound. Enlargement by \\(Cs^{\\beta_0}\\) costs \\(Cs^{n\\beta_0}\\).</p><p>For the first sum use \\(K(2^jR)\\le C2^jK(R)\\) and\n\\[\n-2+2\\alpha+n\\beta_0=-1.85.\n\\]\nAfter division by \\(\\mathcal A\\), it is bounded by\n\\[\nCR^{-1.85}(1+\\log R)^C\\sum_{j\\ge0}2^{-.85j}(1+j)^C=o(1).\n\\]\nFor the second, subadditivity of square roots gives a bound\n\\[\nC\\frac{L^2}{R^2}e^{C\\sqrt{\\log(K(R)+1)}}\n\\sum_{j\\ge0}2^{-j}e^{C\\sqrt{j+1}}(1+j)^C\n\\le C\\frac{e^{C\\sqrt{\\log(K(R)+1)}}}{K(R)}\\to0.\n\\]</p><p>At fixed \\(L\\), for small \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nb(t)\\ge(1-\\epsilon)t-C_{\\epsilon,L}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nW_*\\le C_Rs^{cq_*}E^p,\\qquad\np=q_*(1-\\epsilon)/\\kappa&lt;2.\n\\]\nHölder, Proposition 9, and the area bound give\n\\[\n\\int_{\\rho\\asymp s}E^p\\le CK(s)s^{m-2p}.\n\\]\nUsing \\(K(s)\\le Cs\\) yields the stated estimate with\n\\[\n\\epsilon'=q_*\\left(\\frac{2(1-\\epsilon)}\\kappa-c\\right)-1&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Reach derivatives in the rough collar are harmless because\n\\[\nW_*|\\nabla\\log W_*|^2\n\\le Cd^{-\\vartheta}\n\\left[\\rho^{-2}+(1+d^{-2})e^{C\\sqrt{\\log(d+1)}}\\right]\\le C.\n\\]\nThe same applies to \\(\\mathcal F\\) using Lemma 10.1. Outer-cutoff errors without \\(H\\) decay by the preceding weighted-area estimate. The mean-curvature error is bounded using Cauchy–Schwarz, \\(\\int D^2\\le CB^{m-3}\\), and \\(\\mathcal F\\le C_R(1+\\log B)W_*\\), giving a decaying power of \\(B\\).</p><p>Finally choose fixed \\(M_0\\) large. On \\(d&gt;M_0(L+\\tau)\\),\n\\[\nW_*\\le Cd^{-\\vartheta}e^{q_*c\\tau-q_*c_1d}.\n\\]\nThis absorbs the finitely many polynomial and subpolynomial factors in reach calculations. The choice is fixed before either limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Radial reach budget — original Lemma 10.3.** With Proposition 10's fixed parameters and sufficiently large \\(R,B\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Radial reach budget — original Lemma 10.3.**</p><p>With Proposition 10's fixed parameters and sufficiently large \\(R,B\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_\\Sigma\\varphi m_c^2W_*\n\\left[\n\\frac{3.44+24(\\mathcal F/W_*)T_r}{\\rho^2}\n+\\frac{.012}{d}|\\nabla_\\Sigma d|^2\n+\\frac{\\lambda}{b'}|\\nabla_\\Sigma(c\\tau-b(d))|^2\n\\right]\\,dS\\\\\n\\le .73\\mathcal L+o_R(\\mathcal A)+o_{B\\to\\infty;R}(1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The radial identities are\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\Sigma\\tau=\\frac{m-2+2T_r-Hz\\cdot\\nu}{\\rho^2},\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac{\\Delta_\\Sigma\\varphi}{\\varphi}\n=\\frac{(m-2)(mT_r-Hz\\cdot\\nu)}{\\rho^2}.\n\\]\nIntegrate Lemma 10.1 against \\(\\varphi m_c^2\\), moving its Laplacian to the test. Lemma 10.2 pays cutoff derivatives. The radial \\(H\\)-terms are \\(o_R(1)\\mathcal L\\), because\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\rho\\ge R}(L+\\tau)/\\rho\\to0,\\qquad |H|\\le.355I.\n\\]\nThe \\(J_1\\)-term loses only \\(o_R(1)\\mathcal F_\\tau/\\rho^2\\).</p><p>For a nonplateau branch with small \\(ad\\), Proposition 5 bounds curvature and drift by\n\\[\n.008|\\nabla d|^2/d\n\\]\nafter choosing that smallness threshold and then large supported reach. Where \\(ad\\) is not small, the source is bounded by\n\\[\nCW_*a^4d^3e^{C\\sqrt{\\log(K(d)+1)}}.\n\\]\nOn \\(d\\le M_0(L+\\tau)\\), its shell integral is the second summable bound of Lemma 10.2; the tail was already removed. Plateau drift is within the same allowance.</p><p>The local plateau trace costs \\(G_1|H|\\). Transfer its positive foot term by Proposition 5. Source and receiving radii differ by \\(o_R(1)\\), and\n\\[\nW_*(z)\\le(1+o_R(1))W_*(y)\n\\]\nbecause \\(d(y)\\le d(z)\\). A receiving foot in the rough collar is charged directly to that receiving set via multiplicity one and bounded inverse Jacobian; no reach-sized enlargement of the collar is used. Its cost is bounded by the first shell error. Outside the collar, large \\(a(y)d(z)\\) is again paid by the fourth-order estimate. For small \\(a(y)d(z)\\), the inverse area factor is at most \\(1.01\\).</p><p>For outer cutoffs,\n\\[\nm_B(z)^2\\le(1+\\epsilon_c)m_B(y)^2\n+C_{\\epsilon_c}(d/B)^2\\mathbf1_{\\{\\rho\\asymp B\\}},\n\\]\nand the second term vanishes at fixed \\(R\\). Positive inverse multiplicity is one. Therefore local and incoming traces cost at most\n\\[\n(1+o_R(1))G_1(.355)[1+1.01(1+\\epsilon_c)]\\mathcal L&lt;.73\\mathcal L.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(d\\ge.8\\tau\\), Lemma 10.1 and \\(b(d)\\le d\\) give\n\\[\n(4-o_R(1))\\mathcal F_\\tau/W_*\n\\ge4(.87)(.8/.87)^{.02}-o_R(1)&gt;3.44.\n\\]\nThe \\(2T_r\\mathcal F_\\tau\\)-term may be discarded. On \\(d&lt;.8\\tau\\), one has \\(E^2\\ge s^{-1.6\\kappa}\\); Proposition 9 bounds the omitted radial mass by \\(CK(s)s^{-4+1.6\\kappa}\\), whose sum is negligible since \\(1.6\\kappa&lt;3\\). The remaining reach-gradient coefficient is \\(.02-.008=.012\\), and \\(m(m-2)=24\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Geometry of radial interpolation regions — original Lemma 10.4.** For every fixed \\(C_0<\\infty\\), Proposition 8's common-graph and projection conclusions hold within \\(C_0\\log\\rho(z)\\) of each zero satisfying …", "statement": "<p>**Geometry of radial interpolation regions — original Lemma 10.4.**</p><p>For every fixed \\(C_0&lt;\\infty\\), Proposition 8's common-graph and projection conclusions hold within \\(C_0\\log\\rho(z)\\) of each zero satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(z,Z)&gt;\\rho(z)^{\\beta_0}/3,\n\\]\nuniformly for \\(\\rho\\ge R\\), independently of \\(B\\). In particular:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Two distances within a fixed bounded amount of the least distance can compete only across an adjacent gap on opposite sides of the point; three sheets cannot compete.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For a fixed base and normal side, the only possible middle-band partner is the first adjacent graph. If its shortest base-to-graph distance is \\(\\ell_{ij}\\), then at logarithmic depth before that graph,\n\\[\ns_i+s_j=\\ell_{ij}+o_R(1),\\qquad\n\\frac{d}{ds_i}\\frac{s_i-s_j}{2}=1+o_R(1).\n\\]\nThe ray traverses a fixed-width band at most once.</p><p>All errors are additive and uniformly vanish, and the buffered descriptions agree on overlaps.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(0&lt;\\omega&lt;\\min(\\alpha,\\beta_0)\\), put \\(N=\\rho(z)\\), and consider \\(B_{2N^\\omega}(z)\\). It avoids \\(Z\\) for large \\(R\\), and each zero there satisfies\n\\[\nD&lt;\\rho^{-\\alpha}\\le CN^{-\\alpha}\\le N^{-\\omega}.\n\\]\nProposition 8 applies in a fixed fractional interior, including every fixed logarithmic-power box. Its errors are a negative power of \\(N\\) times a logarithmic power, uniformly tending to zero for \\(N\\ge R\\).</p><p>Distances differ additively by \\(o_R(1)\\) from distances to ordered graph intercepts separated by at least \\(c_0\\log\\rho\\). On a line, two nearly least distances must bracket the point and be adjacent; a third is farther by a diverging amount.</p><p>On a fixed normal ray from height zero, the first adjacent graph has intercept \\(l=\\ell_{ij}+o_R(1)\\), and projection estimates give\n\\[\ns_j=l-s_i+o_R(1),\\qquad ds_j/ds_i=-1+o_R(1).\n\\]\nThis proves monotonic traversal and excludes another band on that side. Unique graph projection gives overlap consistency.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**A single compactly supported ambient interpolation — original Section 10.6.** Fix switching half-width \\(A_{\\rm sw}=3\\), and define …", "statement": "<p>**A single compactly supported ambient interpolation — original Section 10.6.**</p><p>Fix switching half-width \\(A_{\\rm sw}=3\\), and define\n\\[\n\\mathcal U(t)=\n\\frac{\\int_t^3\\operatorname{sech}^2(\\sqrt2u)\\,du}\n{\\int_{-3}^3\\operatorname{sech}^2(\\sqrt2u)\\,du},\n\\qquad -3\\le t\\le3.\n\\]\nChoose a sufficiently large fixed depth constant \\(M\\). There is one compactly supported Lipschitz physical amplitude \\(\\eta\\), equal to \\(\\psi(z)\\) on each supported fixed normal segment, constructed as follows.</p><p>Where\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\Sigma)\\le M\\log\\rho(x)+1,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{dist}(x,Z)&gt;\\tfrac12\\rho(x)^{\\beta_0},\n\\]\nuse the nearest-sheet value \\(\\psi(z_i(x))\\), except when the two least distances satisfy \\(|s_i-s_j|\\le6\\). In that band put\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\eta\n=\\mathcal U(t)\\psi(z_i)+(1-\\mathcal U(t))\\psi(z_j),\n\\qquad t=(s_i-s_j)/2.\n\\]\nMultiply by a Lipschitz depth cutoff \\(\\zeta\\), equal one up to distance \\(M\\log\\rho\\) and zero beyond \\(M\\log\\rho+1\\), and extend by zero. Then \\(q\\eta\\) is admissible in the exact diffuse stability identity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 10.4 supplies the local graph families and projections, including when all projected amplitudes vanish. There is no triple competition. Since\n\\[\n\\mathcal U(-t)=1-\\mathcal U(t),\n\\]\nthe formula does not depend on labeling. At band endpoints it agrees with the nearest-sheet value. Near a zero it uses only its smooth projection, not a derivative of unsigned distance. Thus the local formulas agree and are locally Lipschitz.</p><p>Near the auxiliary boundary at distance \\(\\rho^{\\beta_0}/2\\) from \\(Z\\), all relevant feet have \\(m_H=0\\), leaving a full zero neighborhood before extension. The depth cutoff is Lipschitz with bounded gradient.</p><p>If \\(\\eta(x)\\ne0\\), a participating base satisfies \\(|z_i|\\le2B\\) and \\(|x-z_i|\\le C(1+\\log\\rho(x))\\). Hence\n\\[\n|x|\\le2B+C(1+\\log(R+|x|)),\n\\]\nwhich bounds the support at fixed \\(R,B\\). A finite cover gives a finite Lipschitz constant. No global enumeration or global sheet-count bound is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Normal switching cost — original Lemma 10.5.** For the physical test of Lemma 10.5, and sufficiently large \\(R\\), uniformly in subsequent \\(B\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Normal switching cost — original Lemma 10.5.**</p><p>For the physical test of Lemma 10.5, and sufficiently large \\(R\\), uniformly in subsequent \\(B\\),\n\\[\n.895\\mathcal L\n\\le1.005\\int_\\Sigma|\\nabla_\\Sigma\\psi|^2\\,dS\n+o_R(\\mathcal A)+o_{B\\to\\infty;R}(1).\n\\]\nIts normalized switching cost is at most \\(.087853\\mathcal L\\), apart from negligible collar and cutoff terms.</p>", "proof": "<p>The fixed supported tubes \\(|s|\\le P\\) are jointly injective for large \\(R\\), with Jacobian \\(1+o_R(1)\\), and \\(\\eta(z+s\\nu)=\\psi(z)\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\int q^3D\\eta^2\\,dx\\ge(1-o_R(1))\\sigma\\mathcal L.\n\\]\nIn single-projection regions,\n\\[\n\\nabla(\\psi\\circ z_i)=(\\operatorname{Id}+sA_i)^{-1}\\nabla_\\Sigma\\psi.\n\\]\nMetric and volume factors are \\(1+o_R(1)\\). Fixed-depth profile convergence followed by the phase tail gives normal \\(q^2\\)-mass at most \\(\\sigma+o_R(1)\\). In bands, convexity controls the tangential interpolation by the two projected gradients, and their normal \\(q^2\\)-mass tends to zero because both depths are logarithmically large. Small fixed Young factors therefore leave total tangential cost\n\\[\n(1+o_R(1))\\sigma\\int|\\nabla_\\Sigma\\psi|^2.\n\\]</p><p>The depth cutoff costs at most\n\\[\nC\\sum_{j\\ge0}s_j^{3-2M(\\sqrt2-\\gamma)}=o_R(\\mathcal A)\n\\]\nfor large fixed \\(M\\). The remaining normal switch is\n\\[\nS_{\\rm sw}=\\int_{\\rm bands}q^2\\mathcal U'(t)^2\n|\\psi(z_i)-\\psi(z_j)|^2\\,dx,\n\\]\nsince \\(|\\nabla t|\\le1\\).</p><p>If either \\(m_H\\ne1\\), normal parametrization puts the nonzero base in a logarithmic enlargement of the rough collar; Lemma 10.2 makes the cost negligible. Otherwise assign each physical point to the foot with larger uncut amplitude \\(V_i\\), measurably. This assignment is only a partition of the nonnegative integral and is not differentiated. With \\(r=V_j/V_i\\le1\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|m_B(z_i)V_i-m_B(z_j)V_j|^2\n\\le{}&amp;(1+\\epsilon_s)m_B(z_i)^2V_i^2(1-r)^2\\\\\n&amp;+C_{\\epsilon_s}V_i^2|m_B(z_i)-m_B(z_j)|^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last term has cutoff difference \\(O(\\log B/B)\\) and tends to zero by Lemma 10.2.</p><p>Fix an assigned base and one side. Its band partner is its first adjacent graph. Let \\(\\ell_{ij}\\) be the shortest distance from that fixed base to the partner graph, and\n\\[\n\\ell_i=d(z_i)/G_1,\\qquad u=\\ell_{ij}-\\ell_i\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe receiving foot moves, but graph projection gives\n\\[\n|z_j-z_i|=\\ell_{ij}+o_R(1),\\qquad\nd(z_j)\\le G_1(\\ell_{ij}+o_R(1)).\n\\]\nThe radial factors have ratio \\(1+o_R(1)\\), and the logarithmic weight slopes imply, uniformly,\n\\[\n\\frac{V_j}{V_i}\n\\ge(1-\\epsilon_R)\n\\exp[-(q_*G_1/2+\\epsilon_R)u],\n\\qquad \\epsilon_R\\to0.\n\\]</p><p>Sharp phase tails and additive distance control give\n\\[\nq^2\\le64(1+.001/4)^2(1+o_R(1))\ne^{-\\sqrt2\\ell_{ij}}\\cosh^2(\\sqrt2t).\n\\]\nThe finite-width interpolation is exact:\n\\[\n\\mathcal U'(t)\n=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sech}^2(\\sqrt2t)}\n{\\sqrt2\\tanh(3\\sqrt2)},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_{-3}^{3}64\\cosh^2(\\sqrt2t)\\mathcal U'(t)^2\\,dt\n=\\frac{32\\sqrt2}{\\tanh(3\\sqrt2)}.\n\\]\nNormal depth-to-\\(t\\) and volume factors tend uniformly to one. Divide by the base defect mass, using\n\\[\nI_P(z_i)\\ge31.999e^{-\\sqrt2\\ell_i}.\n\\]\nUp to the explicit finite factors, one side's normalized loss is bounded by\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\tanh(3\\sqrt2)}\\frac{\\sqrt2}{\\sigma}\ne^{-\\sqrt2u}(1-e^{-q_*G_1u/2})^2.\n\\]\nUniformity for unbounded \\(u\\) follows by first restricting to \\(u\\le U_0\\), then bounding the tail by \\(Ce^{-\\sqrt2U_0}\\).</p><p>The side realizing the nearest gap has \\(u=0\\) and vanishing normalized loss. There are at most two sides, each traversed at most once; thus at most one side requires a positive fixed allowance. No factor two multiplies its supremum. Since \\(\\sqrt2/\\sigma=3/4\\), define\n\\[\nC(G_1)=\\frac34\\sup_{u\\ge0}e^{-\\sqrt2u}(1-e^{-1.1G_1u})^2.\n\\]\nWith \\(a_*=\\sqrt2/(1.1G_1)\\),\n\\[\nC(G_1)=\\frac34\n\\left(\\frac{a_*}{a_*+2}\\right)^{a_*}\n\\left(\\frac2{a_*+2}\\right)^2.\n\\]\nIt is nondecreasing in \\(G_1\\), and \\(G_1\\le1.001\\) gives\n\\[\nC(G_1)&lt;.083272621.\n\\]</p><p>Choose the three fixed Young factors and the later Jacobian, inverse-depth derivative, and exponential-distance factors at most \\(1.001\\). After these finite choices, make the normalized residual error at most \\(.002\\) on each side. The total is\n\\[\n\\frac{.083272621}{\\tanh(3\\sqrt2)}\n(1+.001/4)^2\\frac{32}{31.999}(1.001)^6+.004\n&lt;.087853&lt;.100.\n\\]\nFor large \\(R\\), defect mass is at least \\(.998\\sigma\\mathcal L\\), and sheet-gradient costs are at most \\(1.005\\sigma\\int|\\nabla_\\Sigma\\psi|^2\\). Diffuse stability and division by \\(\\sigma\\) give\n\\[\n(.998-.100)\\mathcal L\n\\le1.005\\int|\\nabla_\\Sigma\\psi|^2+\\text{negligible errors}.\n\\]\nSince \\(.898&gt;.895\\), the claim follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Area at small reach — original Lemma 10.6.** For all sufficiently large \\(R\\), \\( \\mathcal H^m \\{z\\in\\Sigma\\cap B_{2R}:m_H(z)=1,\\ d(z)\\le L/2\\} \\ge cK(R)R^m, \\) uniformly in the selected center.", "statement": "<p>**Area at small reach — original Lemma 10.6.**</p><p>For all sufficiently large \\(R\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal H^m\n\\{z\\in\\Sigma\\cap B_{2R}:m_H(z)=1,\\ d(z)\\le L/2\\}\n\\ge cK(R)R^m,\n\\]\nuniformly in the selected center.</p>", "proof": "<p>The selected ball has energy at least \\(cK(R)R^m\\). A fixed-scale separated net on all zeros has uniform positive lower energy in fixed small balls. Packing and ball coverings bound its count in \\(B_{R+j+2}\\) by\n\\[\nCK(R)R^m(1+j/R)^{m+1}.\n\\]\nPhase decay and the gradient bound show that energy in \\(B_R\\) farther than fixed \\(P_0\\) from the zero set is at most\n\\[\nCK(R)R^m\\sum_{j\\ge P_0}\n(1+j/R)^{m+1}(j+2)^ne^{-c_1j}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(P_0\\) large to make this a small fraction.</p><p>The rough collar, enlarged by \\(2P_0\\), has negligible volume\n\\[\nO(K(R)R^{m-4+2\\alpha+n\\beta_0}).\n\\]\nEvery remaining point projects within \\(P_0\\) to a zero in \\(B_{2R}\\) with \\(m_H=1\\). Defect at these bases tends uniformly to zero, so their fixed tubes are regular with bounded Jacobian. The area formula and bounded energy density give\n\\[\n\\mathcal H^m(\\Sigma\\cap B_{2R}\\cap\\{m_H=1\\})\n\\ge cK(R)R^m.\n\\]</p><p>For bases with \\(d&gt;L/2\\), Proposition 5's jointly injective normal tubes of width \\(cL\\), on both sides, have positive Jacobian and lie in \\(B_{3R}\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal H^m(\\Sigma\\cap B_{2R}\\cap\\{d&gt;L/2\\})\n\\le CR^{m+1}/L\n=\\frac{CK(R)R^m}{\\sqrt{K(R)}}.\n\\]\nSubtract this \\(o(K(R)R^m)\\) portion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Stable end profiles, minimization, and surface-order energy — original Lemmas 3.1–3.2.** Let \\(u\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^8,(-1,1))\\) solve \\( \\Delta u=u^3-u,\\qquad u_8>0. \\) Then its vertical limits …", "statement": "<p>**Stable end profiles, minimization, and surface-order energy — original Lemmas 3.1–3.2.**</p><p>Let \\(u\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^8,(-1,1))\\) solve\n\\[\n\\Delta u=u^3-u,\\qquad u_8&gt;0.\n\\]\nThen its vertical limits \\(v_\\pm:\\mathbb R^7\\to[-1,1]\\) are smooth stable solutions, with\n\\[\nu(x',t+s)\\to v_\\pm(x')\n\\quad\\text{in }C_{\\rm loc}^k(\\mathbb R^8)\\quad(s\\to\\pm\\infty),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nv_-(x')&lt;u(x',t)&lt;v_+(x').\n\\]\nTheir coordinate-independent extensions are global minimizers. Consequently \\(u\\) is a global minimizer and\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(u;B_R(y))\\le C R^7\n\\quad(y\\in\\mathbb R^8,\\ R\\ge2),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(w;\\Omega)=\\int_\\Omega\\left(\\frac12|\\nabla w|^2+W(w)\\right)\\,dx.\n\\]\nA global minimizer minimizes in every ball against \\(H^1\\) competitors with the same trace; truncation to \\([-1,1]\\) is permitted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 11.1 gives stable seven-dimensional end profiles. Their classification follows directly from the already proved Propositions 1, 3, and 10: endpoint attainment gives wells; vanishing defect gives a plane; positive defect has finite density and then Proposition 3 applies.</p><p>Each resulting profile is a global minimizer in every ambient extension. Indeed, with\n\\[\n\\Phi(a)=\\int_{-1}^{a}\\sqrt{2W(b)}\\,db,\n\\]\nfor a unit vector \\(e\\) and \\(w\\in H^1(\\Omega,[-1,1])\\),\n\\[\n\\frac12|\\nabla w|^2+W(w)\\ge\\sqrt{2W(w)}\\,\\partial_ew\n=\\partial_e\\Phi(w).\n\\]\nEquality holds for \\(w=g(e\\cdot x-c)\\), so integration proves minimization by boundary trace. The wells have zero energy. The transverse profiles extend with \\(e_8=0\\).</p><p>Apply Lemma 11.2. Minimization in bounded Lipschitz domains also follows by extending same-trace competitors to a containing ball, applying Sobolev gluing, and canceling exterior energies.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Stable directional limits — stability portion of original Lemma 3.1.** Under Proposition 11's assumptions, the limits \\(v_\\pm\\), their smooth convergence, strict ordering, and seven-dimensional stability hold.", "statement": "<p>**Stable directional limits — stability portion of original Lemma 3.1.**</p><p>Under Proposition 11's assumptions, the limits \\(v_\\pm\\), their smooth convergence, strict ordering, and seven-dimensional stability hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>The positive function \\(h=u_8\\) solves\n\\[\n(-\\Delta+3u^2-1)h=0.\n\\]\nFor compactly supported \\(C^1\\) \\(\\eta\\),\n\\[\n\\int(|\\nabla\\eta|^2+(3u^2-1)\\eta^2)\n=\\int h^2|\\nabla(\\eta/h)|^2\\ge0.\n\\]\nDivision is legitimate on compact support.</p><p>Bounded monotonicity gives pointwise vertical limits. Translation compactness and uniform derivative bounds improve convergence to local smooth convergence; every subsequence has the same pointwise limit, and bounded vertical shifts leave it unchanged. The equation and eight-dimensional stability pass to the limit.</p><p>For \\(\\phi\\in C_c^1(\\mathbb R^7)\\) and nonzero \\(\\zeta\\in C_c^1(\\mathbb R)\\), test limit stability with \\(\\phi(x')\\zeta(t/L)\\), divide by \\(L\\int\\zeta^2\\), and obtain\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^7}(|\\nabla\\phi|^2+(3v_\\pm^2-1)\\phi^2)\n\\ge-\\frac1{L^2}\\frac{\\int|\\zeta'|^2}{\\int\\zeta^2}\n\\int\\phi^2.\n\\]\nLet \\(L\\to\\infty\\). Strict inequalities follow from\n\\[\nu(x',t)&lt;u(x',t+1)\\le v_+(x')\n\\]\nand its lower analogue.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Minimizing barriers and compact sliding — original Lemma 3.2.** Let \\(N\\ge2\\), and let a bounded solution \\( u:\\mathbb R^{N-1}\\times\\mathbb R\\to(-1,1) \\) be strictly increasing in the last coordinate.", "statement": "<p>**Minimizing barriers and compact sliding — original Lemma 3.2.**</p><p>Let \\(N\\ge2\\), and let a bounded solution\n\\[\nu:\\mathbb R^{N-1}\\times\\mathbb R\\to(-1,1)\n\\]\nbe strictly increasing in the last coordinate. Suppose its vertical limits \\(a_\\pm(x')\\), extended independently of that coordinate, are global minimizers. Then \\(u\\) is a global minimizer and\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(u;B_R(y))\\le C_NR^{N-1}\n\\quad(R\\ge2).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a ball \\(B\\), and choose an energy minimizer \\(w\\) with trace \\(u\\), truncated to \\([-1,1]\\). The Sobolev lattice identity gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(\\max\\{w,a_-\\};B)+\\mathcal E(\\min\\{w,a_-\\};B)\n=\\mathcal E(w;B)+\\mathcal E(a_-;B).\n\\]\nThe minimum has trace \\(a_-\\), so its minimality shows that lower clipping does not increase energy. Upper clipping similarly gives an unconstrained minimizer \\(\\widetilde w\\) with\n\\[\na_-\\le\\widetilde w\\le a_+.\n\\]\nIt is smooth up to the boundary. Strong comparison for the difference equation, whose zeroth-order coefficient is bounded, gives strict inequalities on the closed ball.</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(s\\), \\(u_s(x',t)=u(x',t+s)&gt;\\widetilde w\\) uniformly on the closed ball. Let\n\\[\ns_*=\\inf\\{s\\ge0:u_s\\ge\\widetilde w\\}.\n\\]\nThis set is an upper interval. If \\(s_*&gt;0\\), strict boundary inequality and strong comparison give a positive compact gap, allowing a smaller translate. Thus \\(\\widetilde w\\le u\\). Sliding upward from sufficiently negative translates gives the reverse inequality. Hence \\(u=\\widetilde w\\), proving minimization.</p><p>For energy growth choose a cutoff \\(\\eta_R\\) equal zero on \\(B_{R-1}(y)\\), one near \\(\\partial B_R(y)\\), with bounded gradient. The competitor\n\\[\n1+\\eta_R(u-1)\n\\]\nhas zero inner energy and bounded energy density on a unit-width shell, using uniform gradient bounds. The shell volume is \\(O(R^{N-1})\\).</p><p>The comparison is the minimizing-end-profile reduction of Jerison–Monneau [14]. The potential may be extended outside \\([-1,1]\\) by \\((s+1)^2\\) below \\(-1\\) and \\((s-1)^2\\) above \\(1\\); values and the first two derivatives match, its derivative is globally Lipschitz, and clipping makes the minimizing comparisons identical.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Exact perimeter limit — original Proposition 3.3.** Let \\(u:\\mathbb R^N\\to[-1,1]\\) be an entire global minimizer with \\( \\mathcal E(u;B_R)\\le CR^{N-1}\\quad(R\\ge2). \\) For any \\(R_j\\to\\infty\\), put …", "statement": "<p>**Exact perimeter limit — original Proposition 3.3.**</p><p>Let \\(u:\\mathbb R^N\\to[-1,1]\\) be an entire global minimizer with\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(u;B_R)\\le CR^{N-1}\\quad(R\\ge2).\n\\]\nFor any \\(R_j\\to\\infty\\), put \\(\\epsilon_j=R_j^{-1}\\), \\(u_j(x)=u(R_jx)\\). A subsequence and a locally finite-perimeter set \\(E\\) satisfy\n\\[\nu_j\\to2\\mathbf1_E-1\\quad\\text{in }L^1_{\\rm loc},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{\\epsilon_j}{2}|\\nabla u_j|^2+\n\\frac{W(u_j)}{\\epsilon_j}\\right)\\,dx\n\\rightharpoonup s_0|D\\mathbf1_E|\n\\]\nas local Radon measures. The set \\(E\\) is locally perimeter minimizing against compact perturbations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(\\mathcal E_j\\) for rescaled energy and \\(\\mu_j\\) for its measure. Rescaled minimization holds against every compact modification, and\n\\[\n\\mu_j(B_r)=R_j^{1-N}\\mathcal E(u;B_{R_jr}).\n\\]\nThese measures are locally bounded. The primitive \\(\\Phi\\) used in Proposition 11 satisfies\n\\[\n|\\nabla\\Phi(u_j)|\n\\le\\frac{\\epsilon_j}{2}|\\nabla u_j|^2+\\frac{W(u_j)}{\\epsilon_j},\n\\qquad\n\\int_KW(u_j)\\le\\epsilon_j\\mu_j(K)\\to0.\n\\]\nBV compactness and diagonal extraction give a limit taking values in \\(\\{0,s_0\\}\\). Uniform continuity of \\(\\Phi^{-1}\\) gives phase convergence, and lower semicontinuity gives\n\\[\n\\mu\\ge s_0|D\\mathbf1_E|.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(B_r(y)\\), \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), and a finite-perimeter \\(F\\) in \\(B_{r+\\delta}(y)\\) agreeing with \\(E\\) on the annulus \\(A\\). The Modica–Mortola recovery theorem gives \\(w_j\\to2\\mathbf1_F-1\\) and\n\\[\n\\limsup_j\\mathcal E_j(w_j;B_{r+\\delta}(y))\n\\le s_0P(F;B_{r+\\delta}(y)).\n\\]\nOn \\(A\\), boundedness and common phase convergence imply\n\\[\n\\int_A|u_j-w_j|^2\\le2\\int_A|u_j-w_j|\\to0.\n\\]</p><p>Partition the annulus, apart from a remainder leaving the outer boundary untouched, into at least \\(\\delta/(2\\epsilon_j)\\) radial layers of thickness \\(\\epsilon_j\\). In a layer \\(S\\), interpolate\n\\[\nz_j=(1-\\chi)w_j+\\chi u_j,\\qquad |\\nabla\\chi|\\le C/\\epsilon_j.\n\\]\nBounded \\(W''\\) and \\(|W'|^2\\le CW\\) give\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_j(z_j;S)\n\\le C(\\mathcal E_j(u_j;S)+\\mathcal E_j(w_j;S))\n+\\frac C{\\epsilon_j}\\int_S|u_j-w_j|^2.\n\\]\nAveraging selects a layer with cost at most\n\\[\n\\frac{C\\epsilon_j}{\\delta}\n(\\mathcal E_j(u_j;A)+\\mathcal E_j(w_j;A))\n+\\frac C\\delta\\int_A|u_j-w_j|^2=o_j(1).\n\\]\nUse \\(w_j\\) inside it, interpolation on it, and \\(u_j\\) outside. Minimality gives, at continuity radii,\n\\[\n\\mu(B_r(y))\\le s_0P(F;B_{r+\\delta}(y)).\n\\]</p><p>First take \\(F=E\\), then \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\) at continuity radii of both measures. Together with the lower bound, equality on a neighborhood basis proves\n\\[\n\\mu=s_0|D\\mathbf1_E|.\n\\]\nThus no additional diffuse transition mass remains.</p><p>For a compact perturbation \\(F\\triangle E\\), choose a continuity sphere outside that compact set but inside a containing ball. Apply the same inequality and let \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\). The perimeters coincide outside the modification, giving local perimeter minimality.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Flatness of the directed minimizing cone — original Lemma 3.4.** Let \\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^8\\) be locally perimeter minimizing.", "statement": "<p>**Flatness of the directed minimizing cone — original Lemma 3.4.**</p><p>Let \\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^8\\) be locally perimeter minimizing. Suppose its nonempty boundary support\n\\[\nC=\\operatorname{supp}|D\\mathbf1_E|\n\\]\nis a cone with vertex \\(0\\), and\n\\[\n\\partial_8\\mathbf1_E\\ge0\n\\]\ndistributionally. Then \\(C\\) is a hyperplane, and its perimeter varifold has multiplicity one.</p>", "proof": "<p>The singular-set dimension bound for a minimizing boundary in \\(\\mathbb R^8\\) is at most zero. A nonzero singular point would generate a singular ray by dilation, so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Sing}C\\subset\\{0\\}.\n\\]\nThe link \\(\\Lambda=C\\cap\\mathbb S^7\\) is therefore a smooth compact embedded minimal hypersurface, possibly disconnected.</p><p>Let \\(\\nu_E\\) point into \\(E\\). The directed assumption gives\n\\[\nf=\\nu_E\\cdot e_8\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe orientation is constant along regular rays because its normal line is dilation invariant and the side choice is continuous. Thus \\(f\\) is homogeneous of degree zero. The translation Jacobi equation becomes\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\Lambda f+|A_\\Lambda|^2f=0.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(f\\not\\equiv0\\) on a component, strong maximum gives \\(f&gt;0\\), and integration gives \\(|A_\\Lambda|=0\\) there. This component is an entire equatorial \\(\\mathbb S^6\\). Any other component would lie in an open hemisphere bounded by that equator, impossible because the signed coordinate \\(\\ell\\) on a closed minimal six-dimensional spherical hypersurface satisfies\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\Lambda\\ell+6\\ell=0\n\\]\nand therefore has integral zero.</p><p>If \\(f\\equiv0\\) on all components, the possible singular point has zero perimeter measure, so\n\\[\n\\partial_8\\mathbf1_E=0\n\\]\non all of \\(\\mathbb R^8\\). Mollification shows vertical translation invariance of the \\(L^1_{\\rm loc}\\) class, hence of its perimeter measure, support, and singular set. If \\(0\\) were singular, the entire vertical axis would be singular, a contradiction. Thus the vertex is regular. A cone smooth at its vertex equals its tangent plane there.</p><p>The perimeter measure of a minimizing boundary has multiplicity one on its reduced boundary, giving the final assertion. This is the classical directed-cone reduction, including its zero-normal-component case.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Compatibility with the dimension-nine construction — original Appendix A.** Let \\(u_\\alpha\\) be the strictly \\(x_9\\)-monotone nonplanar solutions of del Pino–Kowalczyk–Wei [7], associated with …", "statement": "<p>**Compatibility with the dimension-nine construction — original Appendix A.**</p><p>Let \\(u_\\alpha\\) be the strictly \\(x_9\\)-monotone nonplanar solutions of del Pino–Kowalczyk–Wei [7], associated with\n\\[\n\\Gamma_\\alpha=\\{(p,F_\\alpha(p)):p\\in\\mathbb R^8\\},\n\\qquad F_\\alpha(p)=\\alpha^{-1}F(\\alpha p),\n\\]\nfor fixed sufficiently small \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), and satisfying\n\\[\n|u_\\alpha(x)|\\to1\n\\quad\\text{as }\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\Gamma_\\alpha)\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nThen their vertical limits are \\(-1,1\\), they are global minimizers with \\(O(R^8)\\) energy growth, and their blow-down phases have interface\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_9=\n\\{(\\xi,\\zeta)\\in\\mathbb R^4\\times\\mathbb R^4:|\\xi|=|\\zeta|\\}\n\\times\\mathbb R.\n\\]\nThis cone has the vertical axis as its singular set and zero vertical normal component on its regular part. Thus it realizes the translation-invariant possibility excluded in dimension eight by Proposition 13. Constant extension gives examples in all dimensions greater than nine, but no eight-dimensional example.</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(p\\), continuity bounds \\(F_\\alpha\\) on bounded base neighborhoods. Therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}((p,t),\\Gamma_\\alpha)\\to\\infty\n\\quad(|t|\\to\\infty).\n\\]\nStrict monotonicity gives distinct vertical limits, and phase decay places both in \\(\\{-1,1\\}\\), hence they are \\(-1,1\\). Every vertical line has a unique zero of height \\(h_\\alpha(p)\\), and all zeros lie within a fixed distance \\(D_\\alpha\\) of \\(\\Gamma_\\alpha\\).</p><p>The graph estimates in [7, Lemma 2.1 and Theorem 2] state, in the sector \\(\\pi/4&lt;\\theta&lt;\\pi/2\\),\n\\[\nF_0(r,\\theta)=r^3g_{\\rm BDG}(\\theta),\\qquad g_{\\rm BDG}&gt;0,\n\\]\n\\[\nF_0\\le F\\le F_0+Cr^{-\\beta}\\min\\{F_0,1\\},\n\\qquad F(\\zeta,\\xi)=-F(\\xi,\\zeta).\n\\]\nFor \\(p=(\\xi,\\zeta)\\) with \\(0&lt;|\\xi|&lt;|\\zeta|\\),\n\\[\nF_\\alpha(Rp+O(1))\\ge c_{p,\\alpha}R^3.\n\\]\nThe bounded-distance zero description gives \\(h_\\alpha(Rp)/R\\to+\\infty\\). Thus \\(u_\\alpha(Rp,Rt)&lt;0\\) for fixed \\(t\\) and large \\(R\\). The graph distance tends to infinity by the same cubic lower bound, so\n\\[\nu_\\alpha(Rp,Rt)\\to-1.\n\\]\nOddness gives limit \\(+1\\) in the opposite sector. The excluded equality set and axes have measure zero, so bounded convergence gives the stated local \\(L^1\\) phase limit.</p><p>Lemma 11.2 applies to the constant end profiles, proving minimization and energy growth. The nonplanar phase limit excludes one-dimensionality. The cone's vertical singular axis is allowed by the dimension-one singular-set bound in ambient dimension nine. Constant extension in additional variables preserves the equation and strict monotonicity.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [2, 0, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [2, 8, "cite"], [2, 10, "cite"], [2, 18, "cite"], [2, 22, "cite"], [2, 48, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [6, 2, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [7, 22, "cite"], [7, 23, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 0, "cite"], [10, 1, "cite"], [10, 16, "cite"], [10, 48, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [11, 48, "cite"], [11, 49, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [12, 48, "cite"], [12, 50, "cite"], [13, 10, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 10, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [14, 52, "cite"], [15, 10, "cite"], [16, 19, "cite"], [17, 25, "cite"], [17, 31, "cite"], [17, 33, "cite"], [17, 39, "cite"], [17, 40, "cite"], [17, 43, "cite"], [17, 47, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [19, 21, "cite"], [19, 26, "cite"], [20, 7, "cite"], [20, 8, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 17, "cite"], [22, 32, "cite"], [22, 40, "cite"], [23, 11, "cite"], [23, 22, "cite"], [24, 12, "cite"], [24, 22, "cite"], [24, 33, "cite"], [25, 31, "cite"], [25, 40, "cite"], [26, 17, "cite"], [26, 18, "cite"], [26, 27, "cite"], [27, 25, "cite"], [27, 29, "cite"], [28, 17, "cite"], [28, 18, "cite"], [28, 25, "cite"], [29, 40, "cite"], [29, 44, "cite"], [30, 22, "cite"], [30, 23, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [31, 40, "cite"], [31, 42, "cite"], [31, 43, "cite"], [32, 31, "cite"], [33, 32, "cite"], [33, 35, "cite"], [33, 37, "cite"], [34, 33, "cite"], [34, 35, "cite"], [34, 36, "cite"], [34, 37, "cite"], [35, 36, "cite"], [35, 37, "cite"], [35, 38, "cite"], [36, 37, "cite"], [37, 39, "cite"], [38, 39, "cite"], [39, 31, "cite"], [40, 0, "cite"], [40, 1, "cite"], [40, 48, "cite"], [41, 42, "cite"], [41, 43, "cite"], [42, 43, "cite"], [42, 46, "cite"], [43, 40, "cite"], [44, 45, "cite"], [45, 46, "cite"], [46, 40, "cite"], [46, 47, "cite"], [47, 40, "cite"], [48, 0, "cite"], [48, 51, "cite"], [49, 48, "cite"], [50, 48, "cite"], [50, 53, "cite"], [51, 0, "cite"], [52, 0, "cite"], [52, 53, "cite"]]});
